      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>k • !' N )</head>
        <p>k • !' N )</p>
        <p>51, 53 ez 55 BRIDGE STREET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>XMAS XMAS XMAS</head>
        <p>XMAS XMAS XMAS</p>
        <p>THE YEAR'S FARMING.</p>
        <p>BY A. T. MATTHEWS. Author of " Successful Dairy Work," " Channel</p>
        <p>Island Cattle," Sze.</p>
        <p>There are but few seasons on record of which it could be said that they were alto- gether good or bad for British farmers. Cer- tainly 1909 will rank as a very chequered one, not only in the contrasts it has presented in the value of different products, but also in it. 3 partial treatment of different localities as re- gards weather at critical periods, thereby in- flicting loss and damage in some districts ,which more fortunate situations largely escaped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WEATHER AND THE CROPS</head>
        <p>THE WEATHER AND THE CROPS</p>
        <p>The first • thred weeks of January were -Mild with very moderate rainfall, but the whole •of the last week was very frosty and .cold. There was an unusual amount of bright sunshine. Wheat, -which was almost too strong and for ward, was all the better for a seasonable chick; ploughing was well "forward, and pros- pects generally were favourable. - February was a dry month, the short rainfall being de- scribed as a drought. There were over twenty frosty nights, and snow fell heavily at the end of. the month. Some barley, oats, beans, and peas were planted before the snow. March was a very wintry month with deep snow, sharp frost, and heavy rain, very unfavour- able for spring sowing, which was much de- layed. April went far to make amends, 'the weather being genial, arrears, of pla:. operations were caught up, and prospects on May Ist were considered very good. May was very dry and cold, and all crops except wheat- were in want of rain in many parts of the country. Grass was very late, and the grazing season commenced badly . June was a month of low temperatures 'with heavy and continuous rains in the south of England. Grass, however, grew rapidly, and crops were fair on the later meadows; but haymaking -was commenced under the most trying circumstances. -July was another tin- , genial month, with very heavy falls of rain. Much hay was entirely spoilt, but a portion was secured in fair condition during • the last week. Notwithstanding the low temperature the cereal crops increased rapidly in bulk. Wheat and barley aver° heavy crops as re- gards straw, and the estimates collected by the T imes and the Board of Agriculture were surprisingly favourable. The heavy storms of rain and high winds, however, were destruc- tive, laying and twisting the crops and de- stroying the hopes of a maximum yield. The first half of. August .was fine and warm, and prospects were better accordingly. In very early districts a considerable amount of harvesting was done, and, which was of equal importance, cereals everywhere ripened, and another two or three fine weeks would have been the saving of the situation. But this was not to be, and the 'remainder of the month as well as the month of September was PersiSt- ently changeable and wet, so that with only the briefest spells of fine weather harvest work was extremely slow. Sprouting Was common in most districts, and much corn was stacked in more than doubtful condition. October brought no improvement, ad much corn that was still exposed was so much damaged that it will only be fit for consump- tion as food for stock. Pastures were luxuri- ant, and the root crops did well, but mangolds would have been much better, both in weight and quality, with more sunshine. It was im- possible to keep the roots clear of weeds, and follows were very imperfect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>YIELD</head>
        <p>THE YIELD</p>
        <p>As regards the yield and quality of the various crops it is very difficult to 'summarise. The weather, as usual in these islands, was capricious, and some districts were favoured far more than others. The hay crop has been estimated at about 75 per cent. of an average, but at least half was badly damaged, and pro- bably not more than one-fourth was secured in good condition. A good deal of the wheat was secured without actual damage, but very little in good ,marketable condition, so that many damp lots have been offered, while the yield per acre has been very various. A por- tion of the barley crop was fairly well savea, and some only slightly damaged. Yields on early, warm soils are very goal, and, so much being ruined for maltin. purposes, the more fortunate growers will find the crop fairly re- munerative. Oats suffered much from -attacks of the eel-worm, and were thin in plant; but the rain suited them, and, generally speak- ing, the crop greatly improved as the summer advanced. Beans and peas were very irregu- lar, and the pods did not fill well. These crops are not likely to yield anything like an average, while the condition and quality must be very inferior. ' Perhaps the worst crop of the year is clover seed. Much of it will not pay for the cost of threshing, and useful seed will be scarce and dear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>GRAZING SEASON.</head>
        <p>THE GRAZING SEASON.</p>
        <p>The grazing season began badly. Roots and hay were early exhausted, and on May Ist there was only a very short bite of grass. Nothing grew well during that dry, cold month, but afterwards there was great abun- dance. Yes. cattle did not thrive rapidly, and all through the summer and autumn large numbers went to -market in a very unfinished condition. R,eally ripe cattle were scarce throughout, and the few on offer realised prices whieh should have convinced graziers that liberal feeding with cake or other con- centrated food would pay a handsome profit when grass. however abundant, is deficient in feeding quality through lack of sunshine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>PRICES OF PRODUCE</head>
        <p>PRICES OF PRODUCE</p>
        <p>• The fluctuations in the value of wheat since the beginning of the year have been great The average price per quarter of 4801 b. in January was 325. 6d.; in February, 335. 10d. ; in March, 355. 6d.; in April, '3Bs. 5d.; in May, 41s. lid.; in June, 425. 10d. ; in July, 435. 3d.; in August, 425. 4d.; in September, 345. 7d. In the first two weeks of October the average had declined to 31s. 11d., rising again in the week ending November 6th to 325. sd. These figures would be very mislead- ing. to casual readers who did not know that during all the period of high prices as given above there was scarcely any wheat in the hands of British farmers, and that there fall of about 10s. , per qr. immediately they were able to offer any of the new crop. A part but by no means all of this reduction was owing to . inferior condition and quality. The Official average prices of barley, includ- ing as they do a large proportion of low- grade grinding qualities, are not much guide to the values of good malting samples, and this season the range of prices is extremely wide. October and November form the season for the disposal of malting barley, and there is something like 128. per qr. difference between the highest and lowest :Prices now being given for barley by n,-11sters. The average of all barleys in Octoi.wr was about rs. per 4001 b., almost exactly the same as in 1908. Oats were a poorer crop' than in 1908, but the November average of 17s. per 3121 b. was very similar in 1909 to the corre- sponding one last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LIVE STOCK.</head>
        <p>LIVE STOCK.</p>
        <p>As 1611 presently be seen, during the whole of the year there was a great contrast., be- tween the trade for beef and that for mutton, "and it was inevitable that the values of stork' stock should be more or less in sympathy. Store cattle have been very saleable through- out, and when business has been slack it wa.c. owing to the high prices asked by breeders. These have ruled a little higher than in 1903. Sheep, on the contrary, whether fat or lean, have been a dull trade and values have been many shillings per head lower. The pedigree stock sales, however, have gone off well, par- ticularly. those of Shorthorns. The demand for this breed for export has slackened 'dur- ing the last two years; but this has been corn pen-sated by an excellent home trade. There have been more sales than usual, and not only have many new herds been started, but tenant farmers have been good buyers, and this is a happy augury of inlpiovement in the corfimon stock of the country: There has also been a marked tendency to a more careful study of pedigrm and certain old tribes of Bate? blood have sold well, together with a few of</p>
        <p>the most select , Scotch atrains. The new movement for improving the milking proper- ties of Shorthorns has received much encour- agement,. aria long prices nave been paia tor leep Milking cows; while young bulls of ex. Jeigionally good milking descent have biNt!</p>
        <p>eagerly sought after. The general average of all the principal sales v, as a little higher than that of last year. MEAT—ALIVE AND DEAD.</p>
        <p>From first to last tile trade in fat cattle and beef has been steady, and prices have ruled high in proportion to these of recent years. The monthly average prices of first-quality Shorthorn's at Islington will, I think, be suffi- cient for the purpose of indicating the fluctu- ation of values. In January this was 4s. 101 d. per stone of 81b.; in February 4s. 101 d. ; in March 4s. 10d. ; in Aura 4s. 81d. ; in May 4s. 9d.; in June 4s. 10d.; in July 4s. 11d.; in August 4s. 7d. ; in September 4s. 7d. ; in October 4s. 6d.; in November (first two weeks) 4s. 7d. Scotch beef has well main- ' taineu its position of Supremacy in the dead- meat market • but English is 'badly supplied at Smithfield, and has barely equalled American port-killed in -ice, owing .to all the best cattle being dispose fin the provinces. The trade in British cep and mutton has been as depressed as that for beef has been buoyant. The cause of the decline, after having been a puzzle to many, is now gener- ally admitted to be the enormous supplies of frozen mutton from Argentina, Australia, and New Zealand, its consequent lo)v price, and the way it has been pushed by retailers, who have been reaping great profits. The fol- lowing are the 'monthly averages for prime Downs at the metropolitan market during the year: January, ss. lld. per 81b. stone; Feb- ruary, 4s. 11d. ; March, 4s. lOad. ; April, 4s. 111 d.; May, 4s. 111 d. ; June, ss. hl. ; July, 55.; August, 55. lid. ; September, 4s. ,9d.; October, ss. • od.; November (first two Iseeks), ss. 3d. Approximately these prices are about is. 'per stone, or 11d. per lb., lower than they had been for a considerable time before the great fall which commenced in April, 1908, and this means about 10s. per head, which gives us a very good measure of the loss to, sheep farmers through the successful competition of frozen mutton. It, should here be said, how- ever, that .i.he increase of about 1,500,000 in two years to our own stocks has also assisted somewhat in keeping down values. During the late autumn. prime Scotch mutton" was selling at 3s. Bd. per 81b. in the Lor?don Cen- tral Market, which was only 4d. more than New Zealand frozen was fetching the same day; while excellent West of England ewe mutton mils only worth 2s. 2d., or is. 2d. per 81b. less than the best frozen! The trade for veal was about normal; but the pig trade, owing to the large decrease of about 16 per cent. of our stock, has been very lively all the year, and prices have steadily risen. /,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WOOL.</head>
        <p>WOOL.</p>
        <p>The advance in the price of wool has been some little consolation to the flockowner for the ,low price of his mutton. As most of the wool leaves the farmer's hands immediately after the completion of the clip, the great wool sales held in the summer are the best index of the farmer's receipts. These went off with great spirit, and values were some 2d. to 21d. per lb. higher than in 1908. This means, roughly speaking, that the improve- ment would be, on the average, about is. 6d. per fleece.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DAIRY PRODUCE.</head>
        <p>DAIRY PRODUCE.</p>
        <p>Of this section of the farmer's business we can speak with cheerful confidence. By far the greatest item is that of new milk, and this article has .met with a steadily increasing de- mand at remunerative prices. Abundance of grass means a good yield of milk, and dairy farmers have had a capital season for pro- duction. British cheese has also sold well, and the choicer sorts are now worth upwards of 80s. per cwt. As regards butter, foreign com- petition has not increased; but most imported butters have been cheaper than in 1908 with- out materially decreasing the value of British. Good milking cows have well , maintained their price, although the official returns show an increase of 34,455 head in Great Britain, the equivalent of 1.6 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>AGRICULTURE IN PARLIAMENT. '</head>
        <p>AGRICULTURE IN PARLIAMENT. '</p>
        <p>If we except the Development Bill which has now been through both Houses and awaits the Commons' consent to the Lords' amend- ments, the long session of 1909 has been quite barren of results for the agricultural interest. There are of course, possibilities under that measure, but its provisions are almost entirely permissive, and there is nothing to show that grants when applied for will be granted. The old excuse of "no funds to spare" will still be atrailable, and the benefits to be de- rived are entirely in the air. Milk Bills were introduced' both for England and Scotland, and an order was issued by the Board of Agriculture for dealing with tuberculosis under them. As both the bills were dropped this order was suspended from coming into force, pending, it is supposed, the passage of similar measures in a future session. Bills on the marking of foreign meat and the colouring of margarine were also introduced, but failed to get beyond the preliminary stages. The Agricultural Rates Act is in- cluded in the annual bill for the continuance of certain expiring Acts; but as at the time of writing that, bill has not been reached, it is not possible to say whether or not the Rates Bill will be included. - DISEASES OF ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>The country may be heartily congratulated on a very good year in connection with the health. of live stock. To begin with, there were no outbreaks of foot-and-mouth disease up to September. Those of sheep-scab de- creased from 647 to 476. The number of animals attacked with glanders fell from 1932 to 1515. The cases of anthrax increased from 109'2 to 1311. The outbreaks of 'swine fever show a satisfactory decrease, falling from 1,593 to 1,323. The number, of animals slaughtered, on . the other hand, shows a con- siderable increase, the' total being 11,984, against 9,400. This 114 accounted for by the change of policy on the part of the Board of Agriculture, and their more strenuous efforts to stamp out the disease.. The Committee on Abortion in Cattle have issued the first part of their report, and give some important directions and advice to breeders as to the proper things to do when a case occurs. In the early portion of the year the cattle trade was still in an anxious and uncertain state owing to the dispute . between butchers and farmers on the question of giving war- ranties. After many abortive 'meetings and .conferences nothing in the shape of. a general understanding was found to be possible, and so the matter was left to settle itself. The result has been that where the farmers have, by organisation, been able to present a united front, very little has been heard of the de- mand,/ while in a few districts the butchers have, to some extent, had their way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WORK OF THE SOQIETIES</head>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE SOQIETIES</p>
        <p>In a year like the present, when party politics have been of such an exciting nature, and agricultural legislation has been thrown into the background more than usual, the Central And Associat-A Chambers of Agricul- ture could scarcely -be expected to show a long list of achievements. It must be remem- bered that the chief object of these 15odies is to watch over the interests of the industry in Parliament., the Government departments, and those bodies which, as local authorities, possess powers Which affect the sale of pro- duce and • control local expenditure. Yet, although no new measures of any moment have been placed on the Statute • Book, bills have been introduced into Parliament which have kept the Chambers busy, and much of their work has been aimed at the prevention of legislation . which they have considered inimi- cal. The Daylight Bill , was opposed on the ground that it would inflict serious injury on farmers in hay-time and harvest, be extremely inconvenient for dairy farmers, and greatly interfere with fruit growers. The Milk Bill entailed •many meetings and long and anxious discussion in order to improve it, while no less than six reports have been drawn up and issued by the Central Chamber in connection with the Budget. On the whole, the guiding spirits of the "Farmers' Parliament " con- sider that they have • had a very arduous year's* work, successful in a way, but with little definite or immediate result.</p>
        <p>Good but quiet. work has been done by other soi!ieties such as the Farmers' Club, the Agricultural Organisation Societtr. and the ventral Lana • association, espec:any Llliir. ter, which, under the able, secretaryship of Mr. Charles Bathurst, has Worked hand-in- nand with the Centril Chamber, and has ren- dered invaluable ztssistance in Parliament..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>itritho</head>
        <p>itritho</p>
        <p>A series of tableaux numbering 20 scenes of Dicken's "Christmas Carol "was presented on Monday evening to a large audience in the Lecture Hall adjoining the Church by the members of the Guild. Mr. Frank Norman was the reader and the scenes were realisti- cally descriptive of the story. It is almost invidious to mention names, but Mr. C. Sinclair deserves a vt ord of praise for the manner in which he represented Scrooge.</p>
        <p>.:- ' &gt; . -..=--- •= ,a - .....-",-- " N ---- --"-- "•••,_ - , - • ' i ... Ou Ct til i: 7 ' '; 6 1. 14' ~ ** - tt - t CI „. ~..: , .h " si ,</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesley Guild.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Carol Service at Legh-Street.</head>
        <p>Carol Service at Legh-Street.</p>
        <p>A Carol service as held at Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday night which consisted of choruses, anthems, solos and carols, and proved enjoy- able to all present. Miss Alice Bridge who was in good voice gave capital interpretations of the recits "There were Shepherds, Szc." and the aria "I know that my Redeemer livetb," while the choir sang with good effect tie choruses "And the glory" and 'Glory to God," and anthems "Lend Kindly Light" (Buck), and "Oh how Amiable" (Faning). The congregation very heartily joined in singing a number of ieil-known carols. Rev. J. B. Buglass gave an address, Mr. A. W. Clatwortby presided at the organ and Mr. A. Spruce conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Bold street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>'rho Rev. Thomas Miller occupied the pulpit on Sunday night, and gave a short address on ‘„‘ Home Pfe." He appealed for more inity between the altar and the hearth. The latter portion of the service was devoted to selections from 'The Messiah." Mr. Harry Berrey acquitted admirably himself for his solos "Comfort ye,"" Every Valley," "Behold and see" and "But Thou dids't not leave."Mr.H.Plumpton sang effec- tively "The people that walk in darkness " ; Miss E. Taylor and Miss C. Hastings in ". He shall feed his flock" and "Come unto him" deserved high praise, and Mrs. Booth sang "I know that my Redeemer liveth" in her usual brilliant style. The choir excelled in the choruses "And the Glory " and "The Hallelujah Chorus."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A, Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A, Society.</p>
        <p>An open meeting 'v as held on Sunday lest, Ent very few attended in consequence of the very inclement weattler. Mr. J. W. Slack presided, and gave a few hints as to the secret of making others happy at Christmas time. Mr. John Harrison spoke on "A Merry Christmas." There wes, he said, a merriness which led to misery, but there was a merriness which tended to real happiness end solid joy. Christmas time was a season when %Ne could experiment and prove which was the best way. This was the season when we all liked to make our gifts or presents.. It is more blessed to give than to receive. He exhorted all to try it this Christmas time beyond one's own family, and they would realise the joy of it. The Co-operative Male Voice Choir sang "The Crusaders "and "Perfect in Christ," which were splendidly executed, under the leadership of Mr. A. row. Mr. Bennett and Mr. Fair- hurst were the soloists. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ. The Rev. .1. Yonge led the prayer and read the lesson. The Chairman and the speaker wished all a Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year. The old Christmas hymns "Christians Awake" and "While Shepherds " were heartily joined in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>• OUR MISCELLANY.</head>
        <p>• OUR MISCELLANY.</p>
        <p>A correspondent .in a remote corner, to whom our page has penetrated, writes to us in reference to our article on Henry Cort, the inventor of puddling.</p>
        <p>"I am gr",teful tci read so interestinc, and well written an account of a much neglected Englishman, and am glad that some effort is being made to lift him to a rightful niche of fame in the annals of his country. In 1904 Mr. Pierpoint Morgan placed in Hampstead Church a bronze tablet to Corts' memory. Never, surely, was a sadder case of ingrati- tude brought home to the nation than when it was left to an A. merican to do this small act of homage. It is true that Mr. Morgan owes to Cort's patents his immense wealth, and only did what common gratitude Would have dictated, but what a pity that the country of his birth should have been so blind and ungrateful. I have endeavoured to find out what recognition has been paid to the heroic genius of Henry Cort. Beyond the tablet of which you gave a picture and the one, at Hampstead—both given by Americans—l can find nothing save a Hamp- stead County Council scholarship and a rail- way engine bearing his name."</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>The neglect is the more lamentable and inexcusable on the part of our Government when wel remember that it • was by the thoughtl'ess injustice of a Government department that he was ruined, and that the same Government profited to the extent of several millions by his patents. By his patents, Cort made England the greatest iron producing country in the world. Alas that he should have had to die broken- hearted! Alas, too, that he should have had to wait so long for even a feeble word of praise!</p>
        <p>Since writing our notes upon Peter Slynehead. of Sankey, we have found the following reference in an article by Dr. Kendrick contributed to the " Guardian " in 1881.</p>
        <p>"Poor Slynehead in his earlier and more prosperous days appears to have had a turni for rhyming, as the following curious lines discovered by Mr. Samuel Broadhurst on one of the interior walls of the old granary still standing at Slynehead's Green, will show. Mr. Broadhurst found on close examination that the plaster covering the interior walls was mixed with powered glass which will explain the allusion in the lines. All you Rats and Mice. Now you may plainly see T 11,4 it is good to take advice For here you shall not Bee.</p>
        <p>For if you into this Place do Fall The walls are all fenced so That you must eate the Glass so small And that will be ioure overthroo. Man walketh like a Shade And cloth • In Vain Himself Annoy In Getting Goods and cannot tell Who shall the same enjoy. Nov. the 6th, 1688. It would be interesting to know whether Slynehead Green still retains its name and whether the granary still stands there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Field Club.</head>
        <p>The Field Club.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the club on Friday Dr J. Conte Illbbett, medical officer of health, delivered a lecture entitled " A short sketch of the birth and growth cf bacteriology : Some practical applications of the science." Theta was a good attendance, pre- sided over by Mr. %V. Webster. The subject was illustrated by a fine series of lantern pictures showing many of the minute though deadly creatures whose presence in cur tissues and circulating fluid a produce such diseases as typhoid, scarlet and other fevers, diphtheria, or consumption, to say nothing of diseases now rarely known in Europe, such as cholefa and bubonic plague. In addition there were slides of these gems shown under the rniscroscope and numer- Mis mysterious tubes. containing culture of the lecture itself, which was • a singularly clsar and tunder- standahle presentation of a difficult hut most import- ant subject. A lively, and prolonged discussion followed, and a very hearty vote of thanks was accorded to tLe lecturer. A strong opinion was expressed that it was desirable, if it could be al ranged, that do admirable a lecture should Le delivered to a larger and mote popular audience.</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND' (Anise4d, Senego, Scion 'foto, ecc.), voids Iniluenza.—Adrt.</p>
