      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>6 it 6 1111 arta 11.1111LeTIC</head>
        <p>6 it 6 1111 arta 11.1111LeTIC</p>
        <p>r</p>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>Warrington and Siuntz took about twenty-five pounds' worth out of Barrow on Saturday, and the account may now be considered settled. Warrington put on 30 paints to 7 against the Barrovians, and Stuntz pierced the defence on three occasions ;jest to show that he was worth the fuss which has been made about his final deitination,</p>
        <p>Though there was a 5,000 "gate " to bear Churchill in the Parr Hall and Hippodrome. There was nearly as big a crowd at Wildetspool, and the receipts actually exceeded the average returns for the Barrow match. The game is certainly booming in Watrington, and the enterprising policy of the Committee has been fully justified by results. But there are no signs yet of the mighty forward who is required to bring the scrummagers up to A 1 staldard. I note that Halifax have put such well-known men on the transfer list as Swinbank and Mallison. A change of air might make all the difference to them, and the respective merits and the prices of these two class set ummagers are worth considering. The date for Cup tie registra- tion is not a long way off!</p>
        <p>The form of the Warrirgton men was very satis- factory on Saturday, considei ing that the opposition did not call for the highest endeavour. Barrow are a smart and improving side, but they are not powerful away from their own pitch, and the only questi n was the size of Warrington's sco-e. Three goals and .eight tries on a wet and wicky turf may be considered a good afternoon's work. Most of the Warrington men gave brilliant callibitions, and only Dickehson and Brooks of the back divi,ion wc re "off c,ilour." The forwards were in fine form and seem to be pining for hard work. Thit they should gct in plenty to-morrow.</p>
        <p>So far, this season, I do not think we have seen the best that the Warrington men can do. Wet weather does not suit their style of play, and once we get a firm turf and a dry ball the combination ought to be of a brilliant character. The three-quarter line is the speediest in the Union, and the form of Fish and Stunts on the wings leads one to expect prolific scor- ing. They captured fire tries bEtween them on Saturday, acd both proved gluttons for work. What odds Fish being included in the Australian touring party? It will not be complete without him. That is Warrington opinion anyhow !</p>
        <p>Warrington still hold tight t) the second position in the League, and the table up to date is as follows :-- For. A gst._ Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage Oldham ll 12 2 0...32 GO 244...11 13 67... 85'71 Warringtonls 11 3 1...28 61 218...16 18 86... 76'06 Wakell'dT. 15 11 4 0...35 52 226...12 27 106... 73.33 Salford l5 11 4 0...31 47 209...19 25 113... 7333 Huddersfl'dls 10 5 0...25 46 188...21 25 117... 73'33 Hull 17 12 5 0...36 60 252...17 33 133... 70'59 Wigan ll 9 4 1...29 53 217...11 19 79... 6785 Leeds l5 10 5 0...33 45 201...23 30 136... 6666 Runcorn I3 8 4 1.-11 26 106-.20 29 127... 6537 Kei ghley 11 9 5 0...25 38 164...21 29 129... 6L28 Hull K.R 18 11 6 1...45 40 210...21 35 153... 6388 Halifax l5 9 6 0...31 33 161...23 28 130... 60 00 Hunslet l4 8 6 0...19 31 143...27 23 121... 59'14 Leigh l3 6 1 3...13 23 95...14 13 67... 57'69 St. Helensl4 7 5 2...33 49 213...28 46 191... 5714 Ebbw Valel3 6 6 1...11 22 88...18 20 96... 5000 Batley 11 6 9 0...29 26 136...20 25 115... 4285 Swinton l3 4 7 2...13 25 101...17 32 130.- 3846 Rochdale 13 5 8 0...10 21 92...21 36 156... 38.46 Dewsbury 15 5 9 1...23 23 115...25 38 158... 3666 Widnes l5 1 8 3... 9 16 66...18 33 135.- 36.66 BradfordNl6 5 10 1...18 16 81...26 41 175... 3500 Broughton.l4 4 10.. 23 29 133.. 37 56 242... 28'57 Barrow l3 310 0...11 20 66...29 57 229... 23.07 York l6 3 13 0...24 21 111...35 53 229... 18'75 Merthyr Tl 4 1 12 1...11 14 64...27 49 201... 1071 Bramley -.13 1 12 0... 9 13 57...33 56 211... 7'69 Treherbertlo 010 0... 3 12 12...26 41 181.- 100'00</p>
        <p>To-iirrrow's engagements are as follow :- 10 8 Batley v. Leeds. Bcclidala Hornets v. Bradford. 12-2 S Bra!nley v. York. 3-1-1 Brought/1n Rangers v. Salford. 8— 6 Wakefield Trinity v. Dewsbm y. 8— 5 Hunslet v. Halifax. 20— 6 Hull v. Huddersfield. 19— 8 Oldham v. Hull Kingston Rovers. 6 5 Leigh v. Warrington. 21— 5 Runcorn v. St. Helens. 27-11 Wigan v. Swinton, Warrington have a stiff engagement at Leigh to-morrow, and if they fulfil expectations they should go a long way towards making sure of the champion- ship of the West Lancashire League. They should be encouraged by the fact that the last time they were at Leigh on Christmas Day—two years ago—they triumphed by over 20 points. Warrington people expect them to win again. On current form they should have something in hand, and personally I am sure that if. they play the open game and rely on speed and combination they will prove too good for the Mather-lane men. Much will depend:on the forwards, who if they can get possession of the ball, will pave the way to certain success.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire table is as follows: Points. P. W. L. D. For. Agst, Pts. Warrington Leigh Runcorn NVidnes St. Helens 4 1 2 1 -. 62 45 -. 3 Wigan 4 1 2 1 -. 15 -. 20 -. 3</p>
        <p>What has come over the" A" team? Two successive defeats, even on the grounds of such strong clubs as Leigh Shamrocks and Wigan 11i,ghfield, were unexpected. Moreover they imperil the position of the champions, who will be hard pressed by Oldham and Broughton Rangers before the season ends. On Saturday, they were again beaten tn. the pack, and found the narrow field against combination. Never- theless a mistake was made in following the Highfield</p>
        <p>example of Lick and rush. Pdssing and running is the methol which has w n the Warrington second string its long sequence of victories, and it must be adhere I to if the honours are to be iet tined. To day's match with Leigh "A " should provide a safe two points if the ground can be got into playing order.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Combination table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. Pts. Warrington Oldnam Wigan H ... 8 ... 3 ... 0 ... 16 Broughton R ll ... 8 ... 2 ... 1 ... 17 Wigan H. lO ... 8 ... 2 ... 0 ... 16 Runcorn l4 ... 7 ... 7 ... 0 ... 14 Widneq Rochdale H l2 ... 6 ... 6 ... 0 ... 12 Leigh Shamrocks l3 ... 5 ... 7 ... 1 ... 11 Salford St. Helens.— l2 ... 2 ... 8 ... 2 ... 6 Pemberton ll ... 2 ... 8 ... 1 ... 5 Leigh Ince ll ... 1 ... 9 ... 1 ... $</p>
        <p>The Leigh directors are unable to place their strongest team in the field owing to the fact that Neville and Dal ies have not sufficiently recovered from their injuries. Dan Ish4rwool will play on the left wing and Bolewski will go the centre. Leigh—Clarkson (captain) ; S. Johnson, T. H. Johnson, Bolewski, Isherwood; Battershy,Whittaker ; Gallop, Neill, W. Winstanley, Woods, Marsh and Lee. Referee, E. H. Snail k, Wigan.</p>
        <p>The team for to-day's engagements will be found on page 12.</p>
        <p>, ~) J _</p>
