      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Warrington Overwhelm Barrow.</head>
        <p>Warrington Overwhelm Barrow.</p>
        <p>Barrow paid the return visit to Warrington on Satur- day, and brought a strong team, but were no match for the powerful combination which Warrington put in the field. Though soft, the ground was in good playing order and a crowd of 4,000 spectators watched a very interesting game.... The teams and officials were</p>
        <p>Warring-toil :—Taylor, back; Fish, Dickenson, Frawley and Stun; three-quarter backs; Brooks and Jenkins, half backs; O'Malley, O'Neill, Thomas, Boardman, Shugars and Belton, forwards. Barrow :—Treloar, back; Bamber, Brown, Gifford and Dobson, three-quarter backs; Rushton and Dicken- son, half backs; Morgan, Cheesdale, Little, Hannah, Brown and Taylor, forwards. Referee, Mr. King, Salford.</p>
        <p>Warrington started, and soon opened the scoring. A weak kick by Barrow put Stuntz in possession, and the Australian flyer sprinted grandly across the field before parting to Dickenson. The latter exchanged cleverly with Fish, and three minutes from the start the skipper was over behind the posts, Fish adding a goal. Barrow responded in active fashion and foreei Wr rrington back. Gifford tried to place a penalty goal from a wide range the ball falling short of the posts, but a better chance came a few minutes later, and this tune the angle was gauged well, so that Warrington's lead was reduced to three points. Restarting from centre the locals made splendid headway as the result of superior back play, Fish failing to hold when the opposition bad almost been overcome. Jenkins next gave Fish a forward pass when the Barrow defence was hopelessly beaten. The attack, however, was maintained, and after an ineffectual attempt by Frawley to set his brother colonial on the move, the ball was worked to the popular side, and after Brooks and Fish had tried to get through, Jenkins outwitted the opposition and scored. Thomas missed the goal. The Warrington forwards were playing a fine game and repeatedly menaced the Barrow goal,the defence qualities of Gifford and Treloar many times saving the visitors from disaster. A clearance to midfield was followed by a smart series of transfers between Brooks and Fish, the wing man scoring finely. Thomas failed at the kick. The right wing next had a chance of shining. Stuntz receiving from Frawley, brushed aside Barnber. and scored at the corner. The visitors showed a slight improvement as the interval arrived, and Rushton on one occasion made a good opening for Dobson. The defence of Treloar was exceptionally good, and after Bamber had broken through and Taylor had averted danger at half-way the interval was announced. Warrington leading by one goal four tries (14 points) to Barrow, one goal (two points). The visitors were early on the aggressive when ends were changed, Gifford showing rare dash and causing Taylor anxiety. Thrice in as many minutes the Barrow men made dashes for the line, and it was indeed remarkable bow they were kept out. Warrington speedily rallied and the ball was next handled with precision, and Fish being as elusive as ever, Barrow were placed in arrears. Thomas added the extra points. The Furness men were now being overplayed and another hot raid on the railway goal saw Brooks dive over near the posts, but the appeal for a try was negatived. The Warrington supporters had not long to wait for a score, for smart passing • placed Stuntz in possession, and ho dashed over at the corner, irrespective of the attentions of Dobson. A brilliant movement towards the left placed Fish once more in possession and he cleverly outwitted a host of opponents, but slipped when victory was in his grasp. With rapidity the ball wrs now worked across to the right and Frawley, accepting from Jenkins, handed on to Stuntz, who scored his third try. Barrow rallied well and the outcome of a hot raid on the Warrington line ended in Rushton scoring, and Gifford landed a goal. The home forwards maintained a fast pace in the closing stages of the game, and Warrington showing brilliant form behind, Barrow were outclassed. After Stuntz had been pulled up for stepping in touch, Jenkins started a passing movement which travelled on to the Australian. Being challenged by Treloar, Stuntz returned to the open, where Frawley passed to Jenkins, who put on the finishing touch to a really fine movement. Thomas converted and the result was as follows :—Warrington,</p>
        <p>three goals eight tries (30 points) ; Barrow, two goals one try (seven points). EBBW VALE WIN AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The 1,0)0 spectators at Widnes had a surprhe on Saturday, when Ebbw Vale secured the points. Con- sequent upon a heavy ground and a greasy ball, the game was of a very poor description, and was confined to the forwards. The home backs were very disappointing after their recent displays against Runcorn and Wigan. Bath teams indulged in far too much kicking, but at times the visitors showed some pretty combination, and on ore occasion Llewellyn, after a smart run along the touch line, was only brought down at the corner flag. At half-time nothing had been scored. Early in the second half E ibw Vale were penalised close in, and George Aspey failed to place a goal from an easy position. Towards the finish Ebbw Vale had slightly the best of the game, and Llewellyn scored a try after a good run. Davies failed to improve. Result :—Ebbw Vale 1 try (3 points), Widnes, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN'S MISFORTUNES AT SALFORD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN'S MISFORTUNES AT SALFORD.</p>
        <p>This game, at Weaste, was played on a greasy ground, in the presence of 4,000 people. The ball was difficult to hold, and against accurate passing. Salford were the better team, and won deservedly by 20 points to nil. In the opening half the play was very even; and the only score came to Salford through Curzon charg- ing down a kick uy Richardson, and enabling Warwick to score under the posts. Lomas kicked a goal. Run- corn were several times aggressive, and Moran, Watton, and Hughes came very near to scoring, but at half- time Salford still led by 3 points to nil.—ln the second nalf the" Linnets" lost the services of Wattou and Rees, who both retired injured. Preston quickly augmented the home score with an unimproved try, whilst Lomas also dropred a goal. In the closing stages Salford were all over their opponents, and Lomas each scored tries, both of which were improved by Lomas. Result—Salford 4 goals 4 tries (20 points), Runcorn nil.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AGAIN OUTCLASSED BY OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>St. Helens opened very well in their match at Oldham and after a strong attack on the line they were the first to score. Barton ran from his own quarter well into the home end, and punted past G. %V. Smith , when challenged. Briers was close up, and following on he dribbled over and scored after seven minutes' play. The visitors, however, failed to maintain that form, and they did not add to their sc)re until the last two minutes of the game, when Barton intercepted a pass in his own" 25," and, jumping clear between the centres, he had the field open. Still, he had to run three-quarters of the length of the field. Between these two tries, Oldham bad scored eight tries, five of which were converted, and G. W. Smith had dropped a goal. The tries were scored by Anlezark, G. V. Smith, A. Smith, Cook, Avery, Tyson (2), and Fergusson. Wood placed one goal and Jardine five. Cook was given a trial in place of Deane, who was suffering from a cold, and he surprised everyone by his fine play. Result : Oldham, 6 goals, 8 tries (36 points) ; St. Helens, 2 tries (6 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A's " SECOND DEFICAT.</p>
        <p>Warrington" A" sustained their second defeat of the season against Wigan Highfield on the Wigan ground and imperilled their leadership. Warrington were fully •represented, but the home team did some fine scrummage work and overplayed the champions. War- rington were early aggressive and fine prising between Lloyd and Bradshaw ended in the Warrington captain scoring, the try not being converted. Wigan responded well, and though the visitors retaliated aud Smith had hard lines near the corner. Highfleld again bore down on -Warrington's citadel and from a penalty Smith (Wigan) placed a goal. Near half-time Smith crossed for a try. In the second half Warrington attacked without result. Wigan scored through Arnold, and this was the only tangible point recorded. The visitors failed near the line repeatedly, and many passing move- ments were negatived by the narrowness of the playing area. Final : Wigan Highlield, one goal two tries (eight points) ; Warrington "A," one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
