      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>BY B. BBNNISON.</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED BY JACK DODWORTH.</p>
        <p>When a crowd of some 60,000 people rush bolter - skelter to see a League 'batch, as they did to Stamford Bridge, where Chelsea and Tottenham Hotspur played a week ago, one is profoundly impressed by the dramatic leap to popularity the game has made in London. I doubt whether there have been any scenes exactly like those one taw at the great rendez- vous in the Wea End of the metropolis in the history of the League. To the student of char- acter there was more life and colour, more thrill .in the mighty congregation of folk than in any period of the match. Except at the Cu!) Final, which is more of a happy holiday than a serious encounter to the majority of the on- lookers, there could be nothing ' like it. The crowd shot up on the great banks of earth were as four human walls; the sight appealed irresistibly to the imagination. Here were people tightly wedged in the most wonderful of all club grounds, with little or no thought of anything but a poseible victory of their favour- ites: one felt that real football counted nothing to them—they were out because of promised fury; every man was steeped in local prejudice.</p>
        <p>London's Football Derby. But there was E °mottling more than the wild enthuoiasm of this and that club's follower; something more than the fact that one was pre- sent at a London foutball Derby. One saw, as one has seen before that if London possessed a team as clever, say, as the Aston Villa of old, or an Everton, a. Newcastle, a Manchester United team, or the Sunderland eleven of years ago, -ould Irobably not be</p>
        <p>60,000 spectators witnessed the Chelsea-Tottenham game at Stamford Bridge.</p>
        <p>even that of Chelsea, which conies near being the last word in ctrelndi:—llicr Qnons,2a to. areonl-</p>
