      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WORMAITS METAL POLISH</head>
        <p>WORMAITS METAL POLISH</p>
        <p>nioaate We public :*—And I wonderOPAietfier Lon- don folk will continue their patronage so lavishly as they have done, and now do, if the same dead level of mediocrity is maintained; in- different or ordinary football must sooner or later make the "public indifferent. The Lon- doner, and the Southerner generally, knows better what high-class football is than he did seven op eight years ago. Neither Chelsea, nor Tottenham, nor Wool- wich aro proving worthy of the public backing they receive. Perhaps Chelsea will soon im- prove; it is only here and there they need strengthening. The Portsmouth amateur Wil- hams promises to be a decided acquisition to the forwards, and when Woodward returns—l was very glad that he did not turn out against his old club; sentiment is not yet quite dead in mo- dern football—Chelsea should really have about one of the best forward lines of any club in the kingdom. But, like Tottenham, the defence is in need of additional strength. Tottenham are worse off than Chelsea, and now Bolton Wan- derers, who in Adamson, late of Everton, have acquired a very good player, have commenced to win they will find it tremendously hard to avoid relegation. They would, at all events, not be foolish to coddle themselves with the belief that their present team is good enough to avert the threatened disaster.</p>
        <p>Ben Warren's Illness. All footballers will be very sorry to bear that .Ben Warren has been seriously ill, and that he will probably. be out of the game some time. I do not know of a better half-back than the Old Derby County player. Warren at his best is very old-fashioned; and one likes him best be- came he belongs to the old school; he plays so hard. It is his hardness, the telling shoulder. charge, his manifest joy in being strong that</p>
        <p>Klrkiey's goalkeeper has been suspended for IK..</p>
        <p>has always commended him to me. I like the player who is virile. Nicholas John Ross was a hard fighter; it was always woe betide the man who could not return charge for charge when he ran up against the old Preston North Epd man. I suppose that some people nowadays would hold such another player as Nick Ross to be, too vigorous, if not exactly rough; they would look down their noses if a modern Walters came to life, but there are very few people who are able to discriminate between rough and hard play. Certainly many referees do not. I haye seen a back floor a forward who has threatened to score in a perfectly legitimate way—that is, by means of a stout shoulder- charge—have a penalty-kick given against him when if he had not made the most of his 'strength he would have been sadly lacking in his duty. Really, sometimes one may- be pardoned for doubting whether it were wise for a player to resort to the old-fashioned charge. At any rate, the amount of hacking and tripping is on the increase. In these times what is known as "ankle rapping" is a fine art, and it should be put down with a bold hand. I have referred to rough play because the Football Association, by a recent action, have shown that they will not tolerate it at all. What the precise facts laid before them in connection with a game between Great Yarmouth Town and the Kirkley club were I do not know, but they have Auspended the goalkeeper of the latter team sine die, which means, for football purposes, "suspended for life." One iA very sorry that such a sentence should have been passed; it hits the game so hard; but no measure can be too drastic When it in proved, as I suppose it was in this case, that a player had broken down all the canons' of fair play. The severity of the punishment cannot help but have a wholesome effect. The League Leaders. There has been no violent change in the posi- tions of the various League clubs as the result of Saturday's game, but it may be noted that in the First Division .Newcastle came an awful</p>
        <p>cropper at Bramall-lane; that Manchester United have returned to winning form; that Aston Villa won well against Liverpool; that Notts County came near being a paragon of consistency, and that Bradford City are liv- ing up to the reputation of being one of the most improved teams in the country. In the econet Lague the race tor top place remains as exciting as ever; one little slip by Glossop, and they will lose the leadership; it is still touch-and-go. But I say, what a: falling-off there has hews in the play of Leicester Fosse!</p>
        <p>oiT XL IVK</p>
