      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>GROCERS</head>
        <p>GROCERS</p>
        <p>3E3"1:3" Fr 9L 1 W,:i Ft IV' ZEJEt. JOE 31IE 0110 S UV.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS TO LOCAL NATIONALISTS. Vote of Confidence.</p>
        <p>A demonstration promoted by the Warrington Branch of the United Irish League orGreat Britain was held in the Royal Theatre of Varieties on Sunday afternoon. There was a crowded attendance and the utmost enthusiasm prevailed. Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P., attended to give his views on Home Rule, and his appearance on the platform, accompanied by Mrs. Crosfield, was the signal for a great outburst of cheering which continued for several minutes. The principal speaker was Mr. Stephen Gwynn M.P., (Galway) who delivered a very able address on various phases of the Irish question. Dr. Liston presided and was supported by the looal leaders of the party. The CHAIRMAN referred to Mr. Gwynn's literary and oratorical gifts and pointed out that although he was a Protestant he had been selected to represent the Catholic constituency of Galway. (Cheers.) lie (the speaker) was proud of the old country for giving such proof of a liberal spirit in the matter of religion. It disposed of the argument that under Home Rile the non-Catholic minority would he made to suffer because of their religion. The Chairman proceeded to extend "100,000 welcomes" to Mr. Crosfield and said it would only be necessary for him to make a declaration in favour of Home Rule to receive their support at the coming election. (Cheers.) They regardei his presence as a gracious act of friendship towards the Nationalist cause. (Cheers.) With regard to the prospects of their cause the fact that the Boers had succeeded in their demand for Home Rule was an encouraging sign for the people of Ireland. They had been fighting for 110 years without securing national legislative independence but they were not discouraged for they believed that before another ten years had passed they would obtain the object of their desires. The Prime Minister, Sir Edward Grey, Mr. Lloyd George and Mr. Winston Churchill had all declared in favour of Home Rule. (Cheers.) They hoped that in the next Parliament Liberal, Labour and -Nationalists combined would be powerful enough to tarry Home Rule and he did not think their old enemies the House of Lords would venture to throw the measure out (Cheerr.) Conm id; r OWEN TOOLE moved a vote of confidence in the Irish Parliamentary Party,and pledging the meeting to give ffect to the advice contained in the manifesto of the National organization by</p>
        <p>SECURING THE TRIUMPHANT RETURN OF MR. A. H. CROSFIELD</p>
        <p>as the Member for Warrington. (Cheers.) He referred to the great measures which the Liberal Government had passed for the benefit of Ireland, including tbe Irish Labourers' Act, the Land Act, and—greatest of all —the Irish University Act. (Cheers.) The latter measuro gave them a democratic university in which there was representation of all denortinstions. (Cheers.) With re: ard to the Member for Wairington, every promise that Mr. Cosfleld made to the Nationalists before the election had been</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FAITHFULLY CARRIED OUT.</head>
        <p>FAITHFULLY CARRIED OUT.</p>
        <p>(Cheers.) The country was now faced with one of the greatest crises in its history. The Nation-lists could not ferget that tbe House of Lords had been no friends of Ireland but, on the contrary, had opposed ever} thing that was for the good of that country. In the prssent issue tl e Nationalists joined with the democracy of England in their determination that the will of the people must p•eyail. (Cheers). Mr A. M. CROWE, in seconding. said that in the days at en Labour was not directly represented in Parliem• lit the Trish representatives worked solidly for ref aur and helped the Liberal party in their fl2ht for progress. (Cheers). " Down with the Lords" had been the cry of the Irish for many years when the Englishmen hal not been much conce:ned about it. The leaven of Irish opinion had now made the democracy of Eng'and sufficiently strong that 'ere long they wouid either end or mend the House of Lords. (Cheers The res 4 lution was carried unanimously. _ _ _ . • ..</p>
        <p>Mr. STEPII RN GNV YNN, M.P., said ilia skies were bright for Ireland. From what he had seen in England, be was convinced that the democracy were thoroughly in earnest in the great struggle that lay ahead of them. Mr. Crosfleld was an English Liberal who bad got</p>
        <p>A TOUCH OF IRISH BLOOD in his veins— (cheers)—and, what was mora, he had the faith of an Irishman—the faith of a man who when he gave his word was prepared to stand by it. (Cheers). To-day there was hope and confidence in the hearts of Ireland's representatives in Parliament, for the great cause for which they had wrought and struggled so long, was at last coming to pass. (Cheers). Twenty years ago the Commons of England passed a measure to give to Ireland Home Rule. Who stood in the way o‘ the peace and prosperity of Ireland on that occasion? —the House of Lords. From that day to this the Liberals had, wrongly he believed, attributed the great disaster which befel them at that time to their advocacy of Home Rule, and the difficulty of the Nationalists had been not that the democracy of England opposed Home Rule, not that they could not convince the "predominant partner," but that they could not persuade the Liberal party to undertake a</p>
        <p>FIGHT WITH THE HOUSE OF LORDS upon a purely Irish issue. Their trouble was with the apathy of England, not with its hostility. What they hoped for was the day when some issue would arise by which the cause of Ireland would be knit with the cause of English democracy. (Cheers). That was where they stood to-day. The two issues were inseparably united. The people of England could not lift from off their necks the yoke that the House of Lords sought to put upon them, without at the same time removing the shackles that bound Ireland. (Cheers.) When they said " Down with the House of Lords," they meant "Up with the freedom of Ireland." (Cheers.) " Here to-day we have a clear issue" continued Mr. Gwynn "and every man who has a vote to give has no right to be in any doubt as to the way he shall give it, if he is a man who is for the freedom of Ireland, if he is a man who is for freedom at all in any shape or guise. Don't let anyone persuade you that the Liberal party are bragging about a thing that they will not be able to do. This is the</p>
        <p>FIRST AND MOST IMPORTANT PLEDGE OF THE LIBER&amp;L</p>
        <p>PARTY. Mr. Asquith says: 'lf you return us to power we pledge ourselves publicly in the face of the world that we will never again take office unless we are given power to limit the veto of the House of Lords.' (Cheers.) Don't let anyone persuade you th It Mr. Asquith has not the power to make good his promise if you give him the majority he asks for and that W 3 the representatives of Ireland, ask you to give him. (Cheers.) Mr. Asquith can go to the Crown and bay Either you give us the power that we ask or we stop the whole government of this country. We stop the supplies. We vote no money for army, navy, and civil service until we are given power to deal with the House of Lords.' (Cheers.) And what power have the Lords to resist? After all, in the last resort things do measure themsehes more or less by the counting of heads and right hands—with swords or even sticks in them. Who are the 500 in the House of Lords that they are to stand in the way of the Commons of England? They are not as formidable as they look and there is a simple way of dealing with them. You don't need to take them seriously. You can kill them with ridicule. What the Liberal Government can do if the Peers insist upon their prerogatives is to say We will make Peers so plentiful in the land that no man would trouble to pick up a marquis if one were lying in the street. (Laughter). We will send them to your gilded Chamber-500 or 600 people who can be trusted to vote straight upon this single issue and say that no longer shall an oligarchy have power to control the destinies of the country.' " (Cheers). Whenever the Irish party bad persuaded the Commons to pass a measure thlt would do justice to Ireland by settling the land question, the House of Lords had interfered, because they were landlords, and thwarted the attempt. The House of Lords was manned from Ireland for the most part by the representatives of those who held titles that were earned by the betrayal of the liberties of Ireland. (Cheers.) Mr, Crosfleld was one of the Liberals—and there were many —who during the last Parliament had never tried to shirk the Home Rule issue, but had endeavoured to help the Irish party in their work of converting the "predominant partner." (Cheers.)There were a certain number of Liberals who thought it good policy to say nothing about Home Rule at this election, but he (the speaker) believed the Liberal part 3 would be wise if they took example by Mr. Crosfleld and Cal. Seeley— (cheers)—and said to the country " We are going to do in Ireland what we have done with credit and glory in South Africa." (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>HOME RULE WAS NOT SEPARATION.</p>
        <p>For these islands there must be one -foreign policy directed by the Imperial Parliament. The military control should also be one and undivisible. Lastly it had always been assumed by those who spoke with authority that England would retain control of the customs. Home Rule would not mean setting up a hostile tariff against English goods in Ireland. At present there was a nominal union between England and Ireland, but in reality they had separation as deep as the sea that rolled between them. The speaker strongly protested against the action of Lord Hugh Cecil, who in a letter published last week left untouched no old sore or lurking rancour that his pen could quicken into virulence. Controverting the old assertion that Home Rule would mean Rome Rule, Mr. Gwynn pointed out that although he was a Protestant he represented a constituency where there were 95 Catholics in every 100 of the population. In the Irish party of 80 there were 10 Protestants—all returned by an exclusively</p>
        <p>Catholic vote. In the distribution of public offices and patronage in the most Catholic parts of Ireland, Protestants always- received fair play. (Cheers.) If they made Ireland a normal country with a Govern- ment like that of other countries the power of the Church would take on its normal, natural and legitimate position. (Cheers.) With regard to</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION QUESTION in England it was absolutely necessary that there should be a final settlement which Catholics could accept as fair to their convictions, but they would never get that settlement unless they also did justice to tho Noncon- formists. (Cheers.) They would probably be asked as Catholics to vole for the Tories. He telieved in all sincerity that if they did so they would endanger their schools because their organization would be weakened by division. That was a suicidal policy even from the point of view of those who recommended it. Above all it must be perfectly plain that in this election there was one clear issue fot Irishmen : "Every vote given to the Tories is a vote against the freedom of Ireland." (Cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P., who bad a great reception, said it was a little difficult for a mete Saxon to have to folio three speakers, all of whom were gifted with the Celtic fire and eloquence which they looked for amongst their friends from over the water. Mr. Gwynne, in his most ibteresting, argumentative and instructive speech, had alluded to the fact that he (Mr. Crosfield) had a little Irish blood in his veins. " I only hope " added Mr. Crosfield "that the Irish blood may pull me through."(Cheer.)Probably everyone present knew that so far as Home Rule was concerned it would be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NO _DEATH_ BED REPENTANCE</head>
        <p>NO _DEATH_ BED REPENTANCE</p>
        <p>ABOMINABLE OLD -CRY</p>
        <p>ENGLAND'S ADVERSITY 'WOULD BE IRELAND'S OPPORTUNITY. _</p>
        <p>iIcbC)7STIMIe. •Sz Clo'Mir,</p>
        <p>AND PROVISION MERCHANTS,</p>
        <p>for him—(hear, hear)—because as a matter of fact the very first time it was his privilege to take any part, in politics in Warrington, his introductory essay was an attempt in a very hunrbte capacity to back up the great movement that was begun in this country when their old Chief made his splendid effort in 1886. (Cheers.) He remembered quite well spending a good deal of time in the years tbat immediately followed 1886, while he was writing letters for that excellent paper the" Warrington Examiner," trying to investi- gate dispassionately the different cries that were raised and the methods by which it was attempted to excite the prejudices of English people against Home Rule. He could only say that the great arguments that were advanced by Mr. Gladstone and Lord Morley, as well as by the patriotic chiefs of the Irish party, to dispel the fears of the timid end ignorant, were just as valid now as they were at the time of that great controversy. Not the least interesting part of Mr. Gwynn's speech was that in which Le once again exposed the</p>
        <p>that in case self-government was granted to Ireland the large number of the Protestant minority would be oppressed or mal-treated by their Roman Catholio fellow-countrymen. (Cheers.) He (Mr. Crosfield) could assure them be never believed for a second that generous-hearted people like the Irish race would Listen to such a thing or would view the oppression of any section of the community %Aith any- thing but contempt and indignation. (Cheers.) In the old d 53 s he thought that from a tactical point of view a mistake was made in not laying sufficient stress on the great advantages not only to Ireland—though it was natural that a gathering like that should first consider the interests of their own beloved land—but to the Imperial Prirliament at Westminster, to Britain and to the whole British Commonwealth that would accrue from a sensible measure of devolution of Irish Govern- ment. (Cheers.) When they were faced with the congestion of business in the House of Commons which became more pressing with the increasing complexity of modern life and the extending ramifications of the problems they had to consider,how could it do otherwise than assist good government at. Westminster if they bad a certain devolution of Irish business to a Parlia- ment in Ireland, with an executive responsible to it? (Hear, hear) Ile contended that it, must</p>
        <p>CONTRIBUTE TO THE ADVANTAGE BOTH OF IMPERIAL,</p>
        <p>ENGLISH AND SCOTTISH LEGISLATION. Mr. Crosfield pointed out that it was not a co-ordinate but a sub-ordinate Parliament which the Government intended to establish in Ireland—and they were going to do it this time—(cheers)—and dealing with the argument that they might at some future date have to grant a similar concession to Scotland, he said that if increase of business or other considerations made it advisable he believed that would strengthen the Empire too. (Cheers). A most remarkable example of the healing power of responsible self-government was afforded by that act of wisdom and statesmanship which on the advice of their late beloved leader. Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman —(cheers)—had been carried out in South Africa. (Hear, hear). Equally striking results had followed Lord Durham's policy in dealing with the problem in Canada— the similarity between which and the problem we were faced with in Ireland was remarkable to a degree. The consequences of self-government in Canada were precisely those which he was absolutely certain would follow the adop- tion of that policy in Ireland. (Cheers). During the battle in the eighties, Home Rulers were frequently told that "England's adversity will be Ireland's oppor- tunity." He (Mr. Crosfleld) repeated that statement in all seriousness to-day.</p>
        <p>but in the opposite sense to what the crities of Home Rule meant. (Cheers.) Discussing the situation in its bearing on European armaments, Mr. Crosfleld said, it was not true—in fact it was an abominable party cry and a red herring trailed across the path—that the Government were leaving the country undefended, when they were spending £105,000,000 a year on Imperial defence. They were bound to do it as things stood, but there he drew the line. It was not true that Germany could come and land her forces in our country and conquer us. If Germany attempted to do so, she would have a pretty warm time of it, he could assure them. (Cheers.) It would be possible for Germans to raise a scare about the condition of their own sea-borne commerce, which was only second to ours and which at the present time was at the mercy of our fleet if we chose to have a" kick up." (Laughter.) But then we did not want and did not intend to have a" kick up "and—what was more-990 Germans out of 1,000 did not intend to. have one either. (Cheers.) He had discussed the question with influential men in the German Reichstag and he entirely refused to believe that at the back of the minds of responsible German statesmen there was</p>
        <p>ANY SUCH WILD CAT IDEA as the conquering of Britain. (Hear, hear.) If ever the day did come when England was in adversity—and they would do their utmost to prevent that—it was not by denying the responsibility of self-government to their friends across the channel but by giving Home Rule that they would have that union of the Celtic fire and enthusiasm of the Irish with the tenacity of the Saxon which would give us a combination that would be invincible before the world. (Cheers.) Having dealt with other aspects of the question, Mr. Crostield said he was still prepared to stand by the old principles which were rooted in English history that liberty and the responsibility which it involved were best for a country and for national character.(Cheers.) He denied altrgether that representative Government in Ireland, since the wretched Act of Union was passed, had been anything but. a mockery and a sham. (Che3rs.) In con. elusion, Mr. Crosfleld said : I must express the pleasure it has given me to stand on the same platform with such a distinguished patriot, so gifted and so literary, as Mr. Gwynne. (Cheers.) Perhaps some of you kuow I come of a Quaker stock. I am not ashamed to say it. (Cheers.) I have always been proud of the old, sturdy, God-fearlng north-country Quaker stock, those simple country folk among the mountains and rivers, the woods and heaths of Westmoreland and Cumberland who for many generations,even for centuries, have dwelt there and from whom en my father's side, I claim descent. (Cheers). But I always remember with equal pride and affection the fact that my mother came from Ireland. (Cheers.) Many of my earliest recollections are connected with Ireland— old Carlingford Bay and the mountains at Rostrevor— and thee,later on,often bevel spent happy days amongst the blue hills of Wicklow, and admired those old monu- ments of the ancient civilisation of Ireland, mellowed by the hand of time which Softens down the hoar austerities Of rugged desolation and fills up, • As lwere anew, the gaps of centuries ; Leaving that beautiful which still was so, And making that which was not, till the place Becomes religion, and the heart runs o'er With silent worship of the great of old, Those dead but sceptred kings who From their urns still rule our hearts. I can feel with Irishmen in their pride in the record of the grand old civilization of their own country. And when I have gone west and seen the stern grandeur of that desolate but striking coast scenery that you get in the far west of Ireland, even amid the desolation I have always felt the beauty of the country and thought it might be well pictured in the words that were applied a century ago to another favoured spot of nature : Thy very weeds are beautiful, thy waste More rich than other clime's fertility; Thy wreck a glory and thy ruin graced With an immaculate charm Which may not be defaced. Therefore it is not merely as a politician, but with a deep souse of personal satisfaction, that I find myself able to stand here looking, as Irishmen are all looking now, to the time which, as Mr. Gwynne said, is not 110 years distant—(cheers)—when this great meaeure of wisdom and statesmanship will be carried out, and when having got rid of that obstacle which all sections of the progressive party are determined to remove, we shall be able to give Ireland that chance to which the great patriot statesmen of your country have looked forward for so many years. (Loud cheers). The CHAIRMAN announced that Mr. James Dodds had written regretting his inability to attend and stating that he was a life-long Home Ruler. (Cheers). The other speakers included Councillor Burke, of Liverpool and Mr. J. Howes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WARRINGTON INTERVIEW</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON INTERVIEW</p>
        <p>Do the stairs seLnl long when YOUR back is bad? Does YOUR housework drag? Do YOU soon get tired? Perhaps YOU have kidney or bladder trouble.</p>
        <p>There are many women who suffer continually from nervous headaches, a constant worn-out feeling, dragging pains in the loins and back, and spells of dizziness and faintness, and who do not understand what makes them ill. It is the kidneys that are to blame they are weak or diseased, and fail to filter the kidney poisons out of the blood--and you can never be well un- til they do. "HOW CAN I GET WELL ?" Your neighbour here points out the way—and there's nothing like a neighbour's help when you are ill. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills gently restore strength and activity to weak kidneys and bladder, and help. them to drain gout the kidney poisons that cause the above symp- toms as well as dropsy, rheumatism, and urinary troubles.</p>
        <p>Over seven years after Mrs. A. Farrington, 31, Mersey-street, Warrington, made the statement about the cure of her kidney trouble by Doan's pills, she said :—" I am stilt well. I've used Doan's pills on several occasions since, and they have always done me good." Mrs. Farrington's original statement is as follows :—" For two years I suffered agonies with pains in my back, caused by my kidneys being out of order. The secretions from the kidneys were unnatural, there was a puffiness under my eyes, and I used to come over giddy. My rest at night did me no good, and I grew- very feeble. "A neighbour advised me to try Doan's pills, and I did so. With the use of the pills I gradually got better until I was quite myself again. My kidneys were acting properly, and the pains, and other symptoms had, gone. (Signed) Mrs. A Farrington."- WRITE FOR ILLUSTRATED BOOKLET. POST FREE. • 2/9 a box, 6 boxes, 13/9; of all dealers,or plat free direct from rFoster-McClellan Co., 8, IVells-strect, Oxford-street, London, W. iar LIKE Mrs. FARRINGTON HAD</p>
