      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>COMMERCIAL TRAINING.</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAINING.</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A.'S NEW DEVELOPMENTS.</p>
        <p>BY CHARLES T. BATEMAN.</p>
        <p>All parties in the State are united to-day in recognising that one of its most important duties is to 'provide an adequate education for Its young people. This does not necessarily Imply the seven standards of the elementary rode. It may include less or more. At any rate, most commercial people will agree that we need specialised instruction for the young than who has left the ordinary school and is about' to enter business life. America and Germany are far in advance of us in this re- spect. Therefore, in the developments for the future we must meet this deficiency. I am glad to state that the Central V.M.C.A. executive, with Lord Kinnairel as President, have formulated an excellent scheme on the lines indicated. This is pro- posed in connection with their new building now in course of erection on an island site in Tottenham Court-road. Here they intend to preside classes for young men desirous of be- coming proficient in their trade or profession. If they find a demand for technical instruction on these lines they will endeavour to meet the supply. Mr. Clarence Hooper' the secre- tary, who has devoted so much, time and thought to this part of their programme, tells me that where they find the need they will arrange classes and a lecturer. Supposing a number of men in the paper trade in. the tity petitioned for instruction upon paper manu- facture, the Y.M.C.A. would undertake to ap- point a master of his craft as lecturer, and provide the necessary class experiments. The Y.M.C.A. does not seek to make a profit out of the transaction. They will be quite con- tent if pnvents and receipts balance. As expressed to me by Mr. Hooper, their object is to equip young commercial men in order that they may become still more proficient in their craft, and thus assist in maintaining London as the great trade metropolis of the world.</p>
        <p>One of the- most valuable portions of the new building in the Tottenham Court-road will be that devoted to education. Here the committee propose to place in charge a well- qualified director of studies, whose experi- ence and training will enable him not only to provide the necessary arrangements for such classes, but render him competent to deal with young men who have not yet decided their profession or calling. Suppose, for in- stance, a young clerk who has entered a com- mercial house finds that all avenues of ad- vance are closed along that line, and yet he is anxious to study, he will be able to consult with the director as to his wisest course, and receive from him expert advice. This will in- clude not only that relating to the choice of a calling, but the best procedure necessary to aualify in it.</p>
        <p>Clarence Hooper, in answer to my queries, has pointed out to me that the dif- ference between the work of the Y.M.C.A. and the Polytechnics consists in the fact that the latter invite the attendance of the artisan. The Y.M.C.A. is endeavouring to cater for the educational requirements of the young commercial man. "We want," said Mr. Hooper, "to make it possible for the young man in the silk warehouse to be able to take a piece of silk and analyse its con- tents with accuracy and speed. We hope to deal also with other textile manufactures. At the same, time, in our languages department we shall teach French and German, in order to embrace the commercial terms; so that, lcr instance, a man in the Manchester De- partment may be able to speak and write to French or German corresponants in the commercial language they understand. We shall teach him the language, not .for literary purposes, but for his business. In a word, we are anus that he shall be as well'equipped as his French or German competitor. W have already done considerable work along these lines; but,in our new buildings we shall possess greatly enlarged opportunities for development." In Exeter Hall the educational cla3seB were naturally circumscribed for space, but at Tot- tenham Court-road a whole floor will be de- voted to educational purposes. The total cost of the enterprise will Le about £175,000, and there is, roughly speaking, £63,000 to be raised by the Committee. Mr'. Hooper has obtained the official figures of the number of young men in the metro- polis. He finds that there are 800,000, or more than the. Whole population of.-.the . next largest</p>
        <p>city in ureat rintain. Form then Imo regi- ments, he remarks, and they will extend in column formation all the way from London to Leicester. Two hundred thousand of these live in lodgings. Now these are notable facts. With a little imagination we can perceive something of their meaning. The new head- quarters of the Central Y.M.C.A. will not only provide educational facilities, but also a residential home and a social club. Young men will be able to attend classes there, and if so disposed—according to the accommoda- tion—obtain the use of a comfortable bed- room at a moderate rent. To the young man just entering business life in London this will be a considerable boon. Certainly there does not exist at present in the _metropolis any other institution answering so many needs— religious, intellectual, and social—for tho young man. When the building is finished the member 3 will be able to go from the class-room to the gymnasium, and then to the swimming bath, ending up with a quiet half-hour in the read- ing-room or a turn or two in the games de- partment. The programme to be adopted in the Tottenham Court-road premises—to be completed in 1911—caters for what is best in a young man's life. The executive are above all things anxious to train the strong, sane, strenuous Christian young man.</p>
      </div>
