      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Mr. GROSFIELD ADOPTED AS LIBERAL CANDIDATE.</head>
        <p>Mr. GROSFIELD ADOPTED AS LIBERAL CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>ENTHUSIASTIC MEETING AT THE CLUB.</p>
        <p>"The Winning Cause."</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. is to be the Lib.ral standard bearer in Warrington in the coming momentous battle for the people's rights of self- ;government. He has served Warrington well in Parliament (hiring the past f(.ur years and he was unanimously adopted as candidate on Tuesday evening at one of the best attended and most enthusiastic meetings of workers ever held at the Liberal Club. The large billiard room was filled to over- -flowing and there was a ring of earnestness and confidence about the proceedings which augurs well for another glorious triumph. Mr. C. E. Parker (president of the Liberal Association) presided and amongst ethers present were : The Revs. E. B. 'Keeling, R. E. Letheren and H. Wall, Alderman Tinnnion, Captain Crosfield, Councillw s Wilkinson, Locker, Howard and Hal li well, Messrs. J. Howes, J. E. Warren, S. . Wright, James Shaw, W. Clegg, J. Unson, E. England, G. Weaver. J. Heywood, G. Wallington, J. F. Awhery, P. Peacock, Jos. Owen. J. Critchley, E. Wynne Jones, Jos. Wilkinson, G. Walker, E. Lloyd Jones, E. V. Walker, W. H. Sherburn, A. Cook, W. Ford and T. Hewitt.</p>
        <p>Letters regretting inability to attend and strongly supporting Mr. Crosfield's adoption were received from the Hey. John Yonge, Rev. W. J. Roberts, Dr. 'Liston, Mr. T. Pemberton and Mr. Andrew Rathbone.</p>
        <p>The Rev. John Yonge, who had an engagement out of town, wrote :—Had I been able to be present, I</p>
        <p>Judging from the enthusiasm and interest already shown, they need have no fear as to the result of tht corning contest. When the figures came to be announced they could, to parody the song which went so well on Saturday, "bet your bottom dollar Arthur's there"— at the top of the poll. (Cneers). His fidelity to his wont in Parliament aad his interest in the gre it (pies- tions of the day were proof thit he vies a " fit and proper person" to represent the borough. Every ward com- mittee should place before itself the lofty ambition of securing 1,000 votes and then the result would be all that they could desire. (Cheers). Alderman TiIsTNION (chairman of the Liberal Club), supported and said that as President, Mr. Crosfield had probably done more than any other individual to support their organizations. (Cheers). The resolution was then put to the mc3ting and carried unanimously and heartily. Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield then entered the room, aad were greeted with rounds of cheering and the singing of." For he's a jolly good fellow." rhe PRESIDENT, in informing Mr. Crosfield of the decision of the meeting, said : You have kept our flag untarnished—(cheers)—and although at the bidding of the House of Loris yoh have had to lay down the standard I am proud to be able, in the name of the party, to band it bick to you. (Cheers.) We place it with confidence in your hands. and ask you to become our candidate. ( Loud .cheers.) MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH. SOME TORY HARES.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD, who was again warmly cheered on rising to address the meetinc, said that. ever since he had the great and prized honour of being chosen to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, THE LIBERAL CANDIDATE.</head>
        <p>MR. A. H. CROSFIELD, THE LIBERAL CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>•should have cordially supported the nomination of Mr. Crosfield as M.P., for Warrington in the nf.xt, Parliament. I sincerely trust that our Party in War rington will use every possible legitimate effort to win a great success. Do not let any one count upon any representations as to the certainty of success, whether they be the sanonine hopes 01 friends, or the wily prophecies of certain defeat which the opponents of Liberalism may profess to be in store for themselves. I understand many Conservatives are telling our people that they have no hope of -victory. Probably they have not But I don't believe them because they say so! We shill need -every vote that we can properly seenre, and if we can score a bigger majority than in 1906, let us do s The nation has never, in our time, had a graver .crisis, and it behoves every lover of freedom ad progress to do his duty." The PRESIDENT said that's Mr. Crosfield had so frequently addressed them his views were well-known and it was therefore unnecessary that he should state his 'opinions on political questions before they adopted him. If anyone present was doubtful on a particular point, questions would be welco ued. No questions were forthcoming. Councillor WILKINSON (cnairman of the Executive Committee) moved: "That this meeting considers Mr. Arthur Henry Crosfield to be a fit and proper person to represent this borough in the forthcoming Parliamentary Election. It sincerely thanks him for his services in the past and we pledge ourselves to do our very best to return him at the comihe contest by an increased majority." (Cheers). He said that for yeara ago they won a greit battle by a splendid majority and they had now been thrown into the thick of a contest again by the action of the Lords. He hoped that at the forthcoming election they would RALLY ROUND TE-IE STANDARD OF LIBERALISM and again returilMr. Crosfield who had been a con- sistent supporter of the principles of Liberilism in Parliament. He had always been found in the right lobby. (Cheers). Mr. E. WYNNE JONES (President of the Progressive Labour League) in seconding said he did not think they could get a batter ouriidate than Mr. Crosfield to fight their battle. (Cheers.) The Tory party in Warrington were well. aware of that fact. rhey 'thought their power over this borough would,' like the brook, go On for ever, but they had a rude shock at the last election when Mr. Crosfield was returned by a magnificent majority. (Cheers.) If Mr. Crosfield by himself could win 1?y 1,500,</p>
        <p>NOTHING LESS THAN 3,000 ought to satisfy him this time—which would allow 1,500 for Mrs.Crostleld as wall. (Laughter ani cheers):</p>
        <p>[Elliott &amp;. Fry.</p>
        <p>represent their ancient borough in Parliament four years ag ), it had been an invariable pleasure to come down and meet all one's constituents, without dis- tinction of party. He need hardly tell them how particularly glad he always was to come into con- tact with friends of the progressive cause, of every shade of opinion, and it was a very sincere pleasure to him at that moment to be able to return his very grateful thanks to everybody for the extreme kindness and consideration and the unvarying courtesy he had experienced at their hands. They had been blind to one's main deficiencies—deficiencies to which he was quite as much alive as anybody else ;n the room. Hiving thanked the members of the exe iutive and all the leaders of the party, Mr. Cr )sfield expressed his cordial acknowledgments to the esteemed and respected president of the association. (Cheers). Continuing, he said that no man accepting, as he was naturally proud to accept, the handsome invitation they had given him, could fail to be °the-, vise than greatly encouraged by the spirit, the kindry -a and warmth of the reception that they had given to what Mr. Parker had said. It was a great thing to have</p>
        <p>A WINNING CAUSE to fight for (cheers)—and it was certainly quite as fine a thing to have a good cause—(bear, hr ar)— but when they had both a winning cause and a good cause to maintain in the coruing struggle he could well unierstand the spirit that was perrocating the Liberal party from John-o'-Groats to Lands' End—the kind of spirit of which' be had seen the most splendid evidence in Liverpool that afternoon. (Cheers). He had the privilege of attending the luncheon at the Reform Club and of hearing a masterly speech by the Prime Minister—(cheers)—in which incidentally be took the opportunity of tracking dos\ .1 one more of those hares which were being started by a party that was in a tolerably parlous condition in the hope—and it was a vain hope—(cheers)—of distracting and diverting public opinion from the main issues. They had all to be on their guard against the kind of machinations which they would have to face between now and the end of the election. Probably the sporting element in Warrington and other places would have an interesting time in seeing so many hares started and satisfactorily tracked down by the Liberal greyhounds which were in waiting. (Cheers.) They remembered the first great scare raised for party purposes in March and April of this year. Next they 'had the piteous appeals made throughout the country with the object of exciting their compassion for the unfortunate landlords of England—(laughter)—and also for the brewers. The Tories tried first one tack and then another, just like the man who was !eau:ling to ride a bicyle and who wobbled first ou one side and then on the other, without any straight course or policy, finally coming a cropoer.(Laughter.)So in the last fort- night they had seen the same little game played again,</p>
        <p>and whether it be Lord Charles Beresford or Alr.Blatch- ford—all was fish that came to the net of their opponents and the different cries were used for party purposes. Now, in answer to that pitiful work, which ought really to be beneath the contempt of a great historic party like the Tory party, they had the</p>
        <p>• STRONG DECLARATION BY THE PRIME MINISTER that since the days of the great war, culminating in the battle of Waterloo—the result of which his (Mr. Crosfleld's) grandfather read out to his fellow-townsmen in Sankey-street in 1815—(cheers)—and, good old Quaker that be was, rejoiced in the victory as much as any other Britisher—the defensive farces of this country, naval and military alike, had never been in a better condition to fulfil the objects they had to serve —and, thank Heaven, they were not aggressive objects. (Cheers.) He (Mr. Crosfield) had never indulged in inflammatory talk on the question of invasion. He had ' had the opportunity this year of discussing it with men of different nationalities, including members of the German Admiralty and the Reichstag, who were well acquainted with the state of public opinion in that country, and they told him that the Germans scarcely believed that any great nu:nber of English people could be so silly as to think that either the Germans or their rulers were so mad as to dream of carrying out what we were told was an impending invasion of England. Still he (Mr. Crostield) was glad that our defences were in such an efficient state, because it was mainly a question of diplomatic authority. He was firmly convinced that the mission of our great Commonwealth in the world was a good one. and he wished to see the authority and influence of Brttain and the Empire continued through the ages. (Cheers.) Another hare which had been startea during the past tew days was the old</p>
