      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>INVIDIOUS-ATTEAIPT TO STIR UP OLD PREJUDICES</head>
        <p>INVIDIOUS-ATTEAIPT TO STIR UP OLD PREJUDICES</p>
        <p>—rank racial prejudices—between the English and the Irish. Discussing the Home Rule, question, Mr. Crosfleld contended that a measure of devolution would be conducive to the increased efficiency of the Parlia- mentary machine, just as a sensible division of power in the case of some great and expanding business, often taking the form of the opening of a branch establishment, teaded to enhance its success. A subordinate Parlia- ment would be a good thing for Ireland as welt as for the English Parliament and would once and for all put an end to the differences between the two countries, so that if the hour of adversity to England should come it would indeed be lreland's opportunity, not to strike but to help us. (Cheers). While he was thankful to think we had no reason to anticipate any such dire calamity as a Euxopean conflagration, which would be nothing less than letting lose the powers of hell, he respectfully submitted to every thoughtful man in Warrington that from the standpoint of imperial defence, if ever the evil day did come, we should be ever so much stronger if we followed the same policy that, bad built up the British Empire by giving a proper measure of liberty and responsibility to our fellow countrymen across the water. (Cheers). It was unnecessary for him to ask a gathering like that whether they approved of the Lords' attempt to s•riFut REPRESENTATIVE GOVERNMENT</p>
        <p>in England. He very much doubted if there were a score of intelligent artisans in Warrington who, having thought over the question, really in their hearts agreed with the usurpation of the powers of government in this country by a few hundred men who happened to be the sons of their fathers. Such an action on the part of the Second Chamber was not only preposterous but intolerable. (Cheers.) It struck at the self-respect and dignity of the British citizen, (Cheers.)—ln conclusion, Mr. Crosfield said: I will not on this occasion traverse the facts which make the case for Free Trade impregnable. I hope we shall all put our shoulder to the wheel, expose all the fallacies as they are trotted out, and settle the question this time in such a way that we shall he able to get on with that steady progressive legislation, that remedial social reform which will an build up the health, the physique and 'happiness of the people of our country that, while contributing to the welfare of all, it will also add to the stability and strength of the nation and of the whole Empire. (Loud cheers.) •</p>
        <p>OTHER SPEECHES. The PRESIDENT" after referring to matters of organization, said that was one of the greatest coustitu- tional tights they hid had for the past sixty years. He hoped that when their children looked back upon the memorable struggle they would be able to say that their fathers took a part in an election when the power of the House of Lordr, which had long menaced the liberties of the people, was utterly smashed. (Cheers.) Mr. J.HowEs delivered a stirring address. He said that at the last election they inaugurated a new order of thirito in the borough. Mr. Crosfield had been a splendid progressive representative, and they had a record of his work whicu they would not be ashamed to display. Above all, they had the facts of the present situation to stimulate them to renewed energy. He wished to impress upon them that it must be intensified energy. They did so % ell last time that they could not afford to go back on it. They wanted to increase the majority and they could increase it. (Cheers). Every sane man must adinit that the representation of Warrington, in Mr, Crosfield's hands, was much better than it was in the old days of Toryism, and every friend of humanity would admit that the votes given by Mr. CrostieldNvere worth a thousani given by his opponent. (Cheers). Mrs. A. H. CROSFIELD rose in response to repeated calls end made a neat little speech. After referring to the unusual position in which she found herself, as the only lady in such a large gathering, she said : I don't know whether you want me to thank you for having selected my husband as candidate. You haven't selected me. (Laughter). If you had, I don't think there would be any doubt, at all as to the fate of the Lords. (Laughter and cheers). Perhaps that sounds like blowing my own trumpet, hut that is not what I mean. It is that we women generally get what we want. (Laughter). Perhaps it comes through practising it in our own households. (Laughter.) All I have to say is to thank you very much indeed for the very hearty welcome you have given me. (Cheers). Mr. E. Lloyd Janes said that • if ever there was an occasion when every man should put his hand to the plough. it was the present crisis. Never was there such enthusiasm in the Free Tradp cause as WPs manifesting itself throughout the land to-day. (Cheers.) The Rev. E. B. KEELISCr said it was something absolutely new for him to speak on a strictly political question. He had always understood that there were two distinct parties among those to whom he had to address himself and he therefore felt it his besiness to keep fairly clear of party polities. He had had tolerably strong political convictions mil they had been getting stronger of late years. (Cheers.).. He used to feel that if the Tory party went in we should have pretty hum- drum times domestically, and they might be rather vigorous in foreign matters. But the last Government taught him that there was more danger from the Tories in home matters than there was even in foreign affairs, end turned him round hopelessly—(laughter)—so that in his old age he was positively becoming a wild Radical. (Laughter and cheers.) A friend once told him that if a man began to work bard for the uplifting of the fallen and the reaching of the masses, two conse- quenr3B were certain to follow—one was that he would become a fanatical teetotaller and the other that he would become a Radical in politics. He (the speaker) was afraid he was verging very far .in that direction and when he heard of what had been attempted by the House of Lords he said" Whatever the cost, everybody shall know on which side I are." (Cheers.) He felt</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION WAS ABSOLUTELY VITAL TO THE COUNTRY, and whatever influence he had would be used on the side of liberty. (Cheers). Mr. GEORGE WALLINGTON said it would not do for the people to allow the Lords' claim fpr one moment. They might as well have no vote at all—it would be valueless if they let the action of the Lords go without resentment and overwhelming defeat. (Cheers.) They were proud of their candidate, who had already won his spurs by his work in Parliament. (Cheeo.) The Rev. R. E. LETHEREN said the caaes for which they were fighting were so absolutely certain to win that they might be disposed to think the victory would come without effort. They must, however, remember that the forces arrayed against them were very strong. Behind the advocacy of tariff reform and the opposition to the Budget lay the selfishness of private interests. which was difficult to fight. In the coming struggle the liberty of every common man was at stake. (Cheers.) Mr. E. J. GUEST said the House of Lords were</p>
        <p>endeavouring to put us back many years. They were Seeking to wrest from us the rights and privileges we had long enjoyed, and we could not allow them to do it. (Cheers.) Liberals must exert themselves to the utmost to checkmate them. The House of Lords had really thrown themselves into the hands of the poop*. They had been heaping up a considerable amount of iniquity for a long time, but by rejecting the Budget they had sealed their own fate. (Cheers.) The meetings held throughout the country appeared to be enthusiastically in favour of the Liberal party, but they must be careful not to despise the enemy. The Tories had a great amount of money behind them, and they also bad the backing of "the trade." Air. Crosfield had passed through his apprenticeship in the House of Commons, and had done his work well. They would now send him back as a iourneyman—(laughter and cheers)—believing that he would serve them still better in the future. (Cheers.) Mr. P. O'BstrEs spoke in favour of Home Rule and said that Mr. Crosfield had voted for all measures, irrespective of questions of nationality, which were for the well-being of the whole. (Cheers.) The PRESIDENT said he believed much of the luke- warmness and apparent antipathy of the Irish towards the English was the fruit of the treatment which bad been meted out to Ireland in the past. Self-government in South Africa, where there was even more cause for mistrust of England, had dispellei all bitterness and be believed it would have the same effect in Ireland. (Cheers.) Mr. J. UPSON said he deplored the shatneful.attempt that was being made to cover up the lack of a case on the other side by the exploitation of Mr. Blatchford in the series of articles attacking Germany. (Hear, hear.) He believed that the democracy of Europe had so much in common that, above everything, they could not possibly go to war. The man who would intenstify racial bitterness and make possible racial strife was a traitor not only to his country but to his race. (Cheers). The'Liberals were sure to win the coming struggle because •</p>
        <p>THE APPEAL WAS BEING AtADE TO THE GREAT _ HUMAN HEART OF ENGLAND _</p>
        <p>, and that appeal had never been made in vain. The appeal was made when we held thousands of slaves and those slaves were set free. It was made when women and children were practically enslaved in our factories and the conditions under which they then laboured were for ever rendered impossible. It was made when the agricultural and industrial workers were suffering terribly through the heavy corn taxes, and the appeal was again successful. To-day the appeal was made to England's heart on behalf of the toil worn man who, having served his country as well as any Cabinet Minister had done, by arty years of labour, was to be saved from the workhouse; on behalf of the man whose health was giving way or who had lost his employment. The appeal was made to the highest and noblest instincts of Englishmen and it would not be made in vain. (Cheers.) Captain G. R. CROSFIELD, in moving a vote of thanks to the President, said he was more than ever convinced that Free Trade would make for the welfare of the masses. The more 'he looked into the question of the land the more was he convinced that Mr. Lloyd- George was perfectly right in his proposals—(cheers)— and he believed the Budget taken as a whole MADE FOR SOCIAL WELFARE AND WA'S NOT SOCIALISTIC</p>
        <p>(cheers). It endeavoured to give equal opportunities to everybody and to relieve, as tar as possible, the misfor- tunes under which so many of our own people laboured through 'no fault of their own. Perhaps the feature which impressed him most about the Liberal party to-day was their courage over the licensing question. (Cheers). They had in that matter put party advan- tages on one side and dealt with it from the point of view of the State. All credit and success to them as a party ! (Cheers). He nas obliged, in view of what Mr. Upson had said, to deal with the question of the danger to this country. He (Captain Crosfield) did not like to hear them applaud the condemnation of Mr. Blatchlord. He looked upon Mr. Blatcbford as a patriotic man. They knew Mr. Blatchford had been to Germany and studied the ques- tion, and in his articles he wAs most careful to point out that it was not the German nation that was hostile to us. The people were perfectly ftiendly to us. but Germany was not ruled like this country. The G, rman Government had far more power. Let them read German history, see how the Bisraarckian system worked, examine the phenomenal way in which Germany was building up its navy, take the evidence Mr. Blachford had put forward, and then ask them- selves "Is there nothing in it ?" (Cries of "No.") He must differ from them. "I say" continued Captain Crosfield" that a storm is brewing and the ship is not ready, and it never will be ready until every man in this country is trained so that he may defend his country. I look forward to the time when you as a party will take the question up and once more put the State before party interests." (Cheers). Councillor LOCKER seconded and said the party bad reason to be thankful for the active support they received from their President. He agreed with Captain Crosfield to a graat extent as to the need for the youth of the country to take a greater share in its defence. it was quite as reasonable to expect their sons to learn to use a rifle as it was that that so much time should be spent in watching football matches. He agreed that there was the friendliest feeling on the part of the German peeple towards this country. (Cheers). 'Mr. W. H. SHERMIEIN in supporting said he hoped Warrington would again give the Knutsford division a good lead by returning Mr. Crosfield by a great majority. -The vote was enthusiastically accorded and Ithe meeting closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE CANDIDATE.</head>
        <p>THE CONSERVATIVE CANDIDATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Pierpoint Adopted.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held at the Conservative Club on Thursday evening Mr. Robert Pierpoint, who represented the borough for fourteen years, previous to his defeat at the last election, was again selected to contest Warrington in the Conservative interest. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., who presided, said the other side were leaving no stone unturned to get, back into power. They meant to get there by any means they could think of, and he did nOt know that they were so jolly particular how they did it. (Laughter and applause.) But the Con- servatives of Warrington meant to make a good fight. (Applause.) Sir Gilbert pro- posed that Mr. Pierpoint be adopted as the candidate. Mr. W. Peter,Rylands seconded, and Mr. Pierpoint's adoption was heartily aggreed to. Mr. Pierpoint expressed himself in favour of the maintenance of a second chamber, while not averse to some change or reformation of the House of Lords. He spoke in 'favour of religious education and fair and just taxation, along with Tariff. Reform. He condemned Socialism and Home Rule proposals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>£lO5OOO FOR MR. CHURCHILL</head>
        <p>£lO5OOO FOR MR. CHURCHILL</p>
        <p>To Promote Free Trade.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF MR. CHURCHILL'S LANCASHIRE TOUR.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. K. Caird, manufacturer, Dundee, has sent a cheque for £lO,OOO to Mr. Winston Churchill, to be used by the Liberal party to promote their Free Trade campaign. A similar amount has been given</p>
        <p>concerning the Peers. It is just as well to.know more about them. . Mr. JOHN BRIGHT:</p>
        <p>"Many of the peers are the result—l had almost used a more unpleasant word—but they are the result of the darkest periods of our history, for I believe about one-half of them were created and placed in the House of Peers between the accession of George 111. in 1760 and the rear 1830, which we may call the era of the Great Beform Bill. They were the result of the creation, many of them the spawn, of the plunderer, and the wars, and the corruptions of this dark age of our country." Mr. E. James Hall is giving a homily on the history of the House of Lords and the other night he is reported to have made the following remarkable statement:</p>
        <p>MR. E. JAMES HALL: "Born in the infancy of their race, adapting themselves to the changing centuries as they went by, the Peers of England bowed to none in pride of race and love of freedom, and might be trusted to lead the people on to fresh conquests and yet nobler achievements, for they were one with the common people in this that they loved England dearly and longed for her welfare. Now let us see what the absent leader of the Pro- tectionists has said.</p>
        <p>THE RIGHT HON. JOSEPH CHAMBERLAIN, M.P : The House of Lords courts investigation into its past history. Investigation it shall have. And speaking of it so far as regards the majority which dictates its policy—for I admit that there is a minority of able, distinguished, and patriotic men in that assembly—but speaking of what the majority have made it, I say it is a stronghold, and always has been, of Tory prejudice and Tory obstruction. During the last 100 years it has never contributed one iota to popular liberties or popular freedom. It has never done anything to advance the common weal, an 4 during that time it has protected every abuse and sheltered every privilege. It has denied justice and delayed reform. It is irresponsible without independence, obstinate without courage, arbitrary without judgment, and arrogant without knowledge."</p>
        <p>_ For anyone to attempt to champion the cause of the present House of Lords, it is simply 'madnoss. There if a tendency on the part of some speakers to smooth over its defects. It would serve their interests better to admit at once, like Lord Newton did the other day when speaking at the old Kent-road Baths, that it "contains a certain number of people who have got no special qualification for politics, and to be quite frank, I. would be content to do without them." The time has come \for the. people to say, once and for all, whether they are to be rtiled by "people who have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>to the managers of Dundee Infirmary to enable them to deal with tubercular disease. The cheque sent to Mr. Churchill, it is understood, is to be applied towards promot- ing the objects of the Budget League, of which Mr. Churchill is president. In his letter to Mr. Churchill. enclosing the cheque Mr. Caird states he has been induced to give the money as the result of reading the speeches of Mr. Churchill in Lancashire. Mr. J. K. Caird is one of the largest jute manufacturers in Dundee. He is a strong Free Trader, but has never taken any active interept in politics, although on one occasion, several years ago, he was approached by the then president of the Dundee Liberal Associa- tion to represent the city. This honour, however, he declined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HOUSE OF LORDS: ITS HISTORY.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE OF LORDS: ITS HISTORY.</p>
        <p>A FEW NOTES CONCERNING THE</p>
        <p>PEERS.</p>
        <p>;CONTRIBUTED BY M. CHARTA.]</p>
        <p>During the past few week,certain prominent members of the Conservative Party in Warrington have been exercising their wits and have endeavoured to show that, after all, the House of Lords is a kind of Heaven- sent assembly for the good of the community. One gentleman's peroration is worth repeating on account of its sublime distinction. SIR GILBSRT GREENALL : The constitution of the House of Lords hut been made up for centuries and canturies of .the most outstanding intellects and the best men in Parliament, and to say they were imbeciles and fools was absurd, and neither ownmon-sease nor argument tale ordinary English brain. Let us see what certain other gentlemen have said</p>
        <p>got no special qualification," and who, under the pre- sent system, can govern them for ever, or whether they will have representatives who will legislate for the good of the many and4not the few.</p>
        <p>It has been stated that a man cannot argue well or his knees. The House of Commons is the chosen agency of the democracy. It must not be tt ifled with to serve the private ends of any man, or any body of men. The people's will must rule. The time for b wing and scraping has passed by. The wishes of voters must be met honestly and earnestly and their representatives must not bend the knee to old and worn out effigies.</p>
        <p>EARL GREY, in the Reform Debate, 1832, said : "If a majority of the House of Lords should have the power of acting adversely to the Crown, and the Commons, and was determined to exercise that power without being liable to check or control, then the Constitution is completely altered, and the Government of this country is not a limited monarchy. It is no longer the Crown, Lords and Commons, but a House of Lords, a separate oligarchy, governing absolutely the others."</p>
        <p>It has been said that "Anarchy is a horrible calamity; but it is less horrible than despotism. Where anarchy has slain its hundreds, despotism has sacrificed millions upon millions. It is to despotism that anarchy is indebted for its sting." It may seem only a small matter to some people for the House of Lords to pretend to submit the Budget to the judgment of the country, but we may depend upon it that if the power is given to the Peers to refuse to pass it the social problems of the future will receive many severe shocks from their aristocratic hands. The people will be tolling their own political death-knell, For what were all these landed patriots born?</p>
        <p>To hunt, and vote, and raise the price of corn. Safe in their barns, these Sabine tillers sent Their brethren our, to battle. Why? For rent! Year after year they voced cent, per cent.; _ _</p>
        <p>Blood, sweat, and tar-wrung minions. Why ? For rent! They roared, they dined, they drank, they swore they</p>
        <p>meant To die for England. Why then live? For rent! And will they not reinv the treasures lent? _</p>
        <p>No ; down with evei:yehing, and up with rent! Their good, ill, health, wealth, joy or discontent. . .</p>
        <p>Being,-end;rtim., religion—rent: rent, rent!</p>
        <p>M. CHARTA.</p>
      </div>
