      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>eroslield for the eommons. Pierpoint for the Peers.</head>
        <p>eroslield for the eommons. Pierpoint for the Peers.</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Sir W. J. Crossley Unanimously</p>
        <p>Adopted.</p>
        <p>Sir William Crossley, M.P., on Tuesday night accepted the unanimous invitation of the General Council of the Altrincham Division Liberal Association to contest the seat. He said he did not object to a Second Chamber, but he maintained that it should not be allowed to stand in the way of the declared and maintained will of the people. The issue in the Contest was between the reformed House of Lords and Free Trade on the one hand and a curtailment of the rights of theliouse of Commons and the adoption of Tariff " Reform "on the other. He was in favour of a strong navy. All the talk of their opponets as to the navy being weak was so much bunkum, used to create a scare and stir up bitterness. Speaking on education, he said he most earnestly desired to see religious education carried out in' their schools. He wanted the greatest amount of freedom that they could give for each, and he did not se b why, supposing each denomi- national was willing to pay for it, it shohld not have the power to give the religious teaching it desired. He 'hoped a bill would be introduced which would remove the injustice under which Nonconformists suffered. He was in favour of giving Ireland the management of its own local affairs subject to the control of the Imperial Parliament. He was willing to support the demand of women for the franchise. Sir William was promised the unanimous support of , the Association. His proposer was Mr. G. Faulkner Armitage, seconded by Mr. Frank Milne, and supported by Mr. R. H. Gotobed. Mr. J. W. Robson was appointed election agent.</p>
