      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <p>After a flood of impor- BEFORE THE tant speeches there BATTLE JOINS. will now be compara-</p>
        <p>tive rest until the holidays are over, when we 43ha1l be at once plunged into the turmoil of a General Elec- tion. We say comparative rest advisedly, because the dishonest and reckless attempt to create a naval scare will no doubt be con- tinued with the same vigour as before, not- withstanding Mr. AsQurrifs assurance, based on personal investigation and close know- ledge, "that the Navy to-day is able to main- tain, not only this year, but in the years that lie before us, our supremacy at sea, and should necessity arise, to guarantee the integrity of our shores, the protection of our commerce, the inviolability of our Empire." To 'place agalnst this we have the random statement of Lord CAWDOR, an ex-First Lord .of the Admiralty, who thinks that Belfast might be made a naval base for Germany if Ireland obtained Home Rule, that we are ' sitting still with our hands folded and doing nothing." A man who can say this after the detailed statement made by Mr! MCKENNA the other day might be expected -to say anything. The electors will be called upon in the coming election, not merely to decide 'between one party and another, or one policy and another, but between representa- tive institutions and permanent govern- ment by the House of Lords. This is so 'very apparent that in all constituencies there are some clear-seeing men who, never having voted for a Liberal candidate -in their lives, intend on this occasion to support -the Government. It is to these that the recent letter of Lord ST. ALDWYN was addressed. Just as Lord CROMER hopes to persuade the Conservative •Free Traders to vote for the Tariff "Reform" party, so the only financial authority on the Conservative side aims at inducing those who share his own views about the action of the House of Lords, but who, unlike him, carry them to their logical conclusions, to return to their party. He may be successful in some cases; but we shall venture to hope that there are many Conservatives who are first of all Englishmen and lovers of liberty, and that these will, in an unparalleled crisis, -vote against the attempted establishment of ,a new tyranny. It does not appear, on reflection, that the ,elections Will be interfered with seriously by the decision of the House of Lords in the trade . union case. Undoubtedly a grave situation has been raised; but it is one that does not immediately require to be dealt with. The Labour candidates and Labour Members will go to the poll just as if nothing had happened. 'There are, as it appears, very considerable funds in hand. Mr. KEIR HARDIE, indeed, goes so far as to say that -there is in hand or in sight money enough to pay election expenses and the salaries of members for some years to come. Where a special difficulty preients itself is in such cases as that of Mr. CHARLES FEN WICK, who is not a trade union official, and who receives his salary from the union wholly in the ,character of a Member of Parliament. Such -instances are few—probably not half-a-dozen in all—and the trouble created by the House .of Lords' decision will, no doubt, be sur- mounted by means of voluntary contribu- -t ions.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield and THE CANDIDATES. Mr. Robert Pierpoint will once more be the candidates for their respective parties at the Warrington election on Monday, January 17th. There have been rumours that the 'Conservatives contemplated a change in -their standard-bearer, but they have apparently acted on the principle that it is not wise to swap horses when crossing the stream. Personally we would as soon beat Mr. R. Pierpoint as anyone else. In Mr. Crosfield the *Liberals possess a candidate who enjoys their full confidence and who has strengthened his position enormously since he was returned as member for the Borough four years ago. His occasional interventions in debates in the House have always been timely, and indioative of his deep interest in the welfare of the working classes and the progress of the nation. On Committees of the House, too, Mr. Crosfield has rendered service which has not caught -the public eye, but which has lacked nothing in value on that account. In his own con- stituency Mr. Crosfield has always been ready and willing to personally support -every deserving object, and he has been instrumental also in conferring a boon upon the public which is apt to be -overlooked. We refer to the supply of dis- tinguished politicians for public meetings in -the town. Warrington has been exception- „any favoured in this respect,and the privilege of hearing so many of the leading men Non the Government side is largely the result of the close personal touch Mr. Crosfield has kept with the leaders of his party at West- minster. • The constituency has had free</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>political education pf the highest class, and the result shonld be obvious when the poll is cast. Liberalism has everything to gain and nothing to lose at the hands of an enlightened electorate, and we cannot anticipate anything but the triumphant return of Mr. Crosfield, for the second time, as Member for Warrington.</p>
        <p>Another record has ML' CHURCHILL'S fallen to .the Liberals VISIT. of Warrington. They previouSly held the record for the largest Parliamentary poll and for the largest election, majority in Warrington, and on Saturday they easily. secured the record for the greatest and most impressive mass meeting—or meetings—in the borough. There is one more record ' desired, and that is the surpassing of the previous best in the Liberal majority on January 17th next. Mr. Winston Churchill created the newest record on Saturday when he did Warrington the honour of selecting the ancient bordugh for the closing meetings Of his memorable Lancashire tour. Two thousand one hundred people—there were tickets to this number collected' at the doors—assembled in Parr Hall to hear his address on the vital issues of the coming struggle between democracy and hereditary privilege, and an even greater 'gathering filled the Hippodrome for an over- flow meeting. Liberalism, liberty and pro- gress were upheld by an assemblage of about 5,000 persons, mostly voters in the Parlia- mentary , borough of 'Warrington. Mr. Churchill stated his case—the case of a united party, or union of parties—and met with no dissent in the vast crowd. The flower of the electorate demonstrated their firm faith in Free Trade, in the social programme of the Government, in the sanity and righteousness of the Budget, and in the inalienable right of the people of this country to govern them- selves. There was evidenced a moral earnestness which bodes ill for the party which has flouted the democracy and attempted to raise a bulwark of selfish interests against the flood tide of a people's will.</p>
        <p>THE SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Churchill spoke, as he always speaks</p>
        <p>with a statesmanlike gravity, a ' characteristic touch of irony, ' and with the directness of a man whose beliefs are rooted in the bed- rock of conviction. His grasp of affairs, the width and foresight of his outlook, were remarkable, and no politician of a recent generation has given utterance to sounder or more serious propositions for the advance- ment of the nation, and the amelioration of the lot of the poorer classes ,who form the bulk of our population. Right at the out- set, Mr. Churchill tackled the problem of unemployment—the greatest curse of our time—with a courage and skill which, appealed to every thoughtful mind. He has no ready-made panacea for this vast and growing evil of present-day civilization, and he spoke the truth when he said that no one else possessed. a remedy. "If any man tells you he has the ready-made solution of this great problem, you may be quite sure he is a rogue "—an undisguised and telling hit at the speciousness and falsity of Tariff Reform. But Mr. Churchill did not leave the trouble where it lay. He pointed out the effective lines on which the Government is working to lessen the evil—through the Development Act, labour exchanges, and national insurance for the workless. Herein lies a policy bright with hope for the struggling masses of unemployed or partially employed, and the great audience was quick to appreciate the fact that there was not only something promised, but something done.</p>
        <p>Then followed a critical examination of Tariff Reform proposals, and an exposure of their nebulosity and cruel deceitfulness. "Taxing the foreigner" means essentially the establishment in this country of trusts, and addition to the cost of living.; the increase of indirect taxation, which is taxa- tion of wages, rather than direct taxation, which is taxation of wealth. The plan of the Budget is to tax people directly in pro- portion to their ability to pay; and to liberate the land from the barbed fence of greed and selfishness, so that the people shall gradually but surely resume possession of their God-given birthright. The arrogant usurpation of privilege by the Lords was put in concrete, telling form. "The people," he said, "must depend upon themselves and not on the House of Lords." They had only to stand firm and they would gain the day; the stability of society would be strengthened by a better social organisation and we should all "march forward in the might- of a free healthy and contented people; freed from the abuses of monopoly and from the restraints of hereditary privilege."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>French Engineer's Design</p>
        <p>Recommended.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Bridges Committee previous to the Council meeting yesterday, Mr. John J. Webster, the well-known Bridge Engineer, submitted a report upon the com- petitive designs received from contractors for the reconstruction of the bridge. Twenty contractors, he stated, sent in tenders, but as many of the. contractors had tendered upon the same design, the actual number of designs was 10. In considering the merits of the various designs, two main points only had to be investigated, the architectural features of the design and the details of the construction. The main thing to be ascertained was that the details of construction were properly designed to withstand the stress to which they would be submitted under the taxed condition loading, all other considerations being of secondary importance. A very careful consideration of the tabulated results of his investigations, reported Mr. Webster, left the 'designs of Messrs. Christian and Nielson, and of the Considere Construction Co. for final consideration. Although, continued Mr. Webster, the design and the constructional work of Messrs. Christian and Neilson were excellent and had been sub- mitted in masterly manner, the advantages of the two systems were in favour of the design submitted by the Considere Construc- tion Co. He, therefore, recommended that this system be adopted. The Bridge Com- mittee after an hour's discussion, decided to invite Mr. Webster's attendance at a meeting next Wednesday. M. Considere, whose design is recom- mended for adoption, is one of the highest authorities on the design and construction of re-inforced concrete work and many of the formulas which are now used in designing such work are based upon the results of his investigations. He was for many years Inspecteur Generale des Pouts et Chausses for the French Government. All designs for public work had to •' be submitted to and passed by him.</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists 0n1y.914,1/1-1„2/9-Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>• The polling day for the Borough of Warrington is expected to be Monday, January 17th. * *</p>
        <p>Warrington Liberals have a winning cause and a winning canclldate. The latter has, in addition, a winning wife. The combination is believed to be irresistible.</p>
        <p>* * Mr. Crosfield's adoption as the candidate on Tuesday was an event signalised by the wildest enthusiasm of the party chiefs and captains. In Free Trade, the Budget and the taming of the Lords they hold a " hand " which they believe cannot be beaten by any combination on the other side. * * *</p>
        <p>We are delighted at the good fortune of the Infirmary, which has benefited by a cheque for £l,OOO from Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., through his firm, Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Co. The brewers certainly do owe something to such institutions as the Infirmary and Dispensary!</p>
        <p>* *- Mr. Charles E. Parker has been honoured with an invitation to contest the neighbour- ing borough of Widnes in the Liberal interest, but has declined owing to the fact that he feels his public and political work should be identified with the town of War- rington. Nevertheless, the compliment to the esteemed President of the Warrington Liberal Association is one which has been appreciated by the members, who fully realise the deep interest Mr. Parker takes in the duties devolving upon the position. The Widnes Liberals have succeeded in prevail- ing upon Mr. L.C.Bernacchi, scientistexplorer, to stand in their interests, so Colonel Hall Walker will not have a walk-over.</p>
        <p>The Liberal meetings in Warrington con- tinue to be splendidly supported by the rank and file, while those on the other side are tame and uninspiring. Contrast the 5,000 who gathered to hear Mr. Churchill with the five or six hundred who attended the rival demonstration in Parr Hall on Tuesday. The Warrington Conservative Association has fallen upon evil times. Neither its policy nor its peers are wanted in these democratic days, , * * *</p>
        <p>There was one section, and one section of the electorate only, that -Was well represented at this anwmic demonstration of full-blooded Toryism. The Church of England clergy were there in full force and graced (?) the platform which, like the rest of the building, had plenty of space to let. Whatever else befalls, we do not think- our Conservative friends need fear that they will be deserted by the clergy: The faithful and subservient devotion of the "cloth" in Warrington to the lords and the landlords could be vouched for. It has become positively touching of late yearS. * *</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert made one of those touching little speeches for which he is, famous, and which get right down to the heart of things. He had a lord to introduce to the meeting— Lord Deerhurst—and he introduced him with great pleasure" because they both went to school together." ' (Cheers). He had further good testimony on behalf of his lord- ship—he was the son of a lord,Lord Coventry, who in 1863 and 1864 "won that great Lahcashire trophy, the Grand National,with two own sisters.' (More cheers). Sir Gilbert also made a passing reference to Mr. Winston Churchill—still more cheers— whereat the Knight of Walton looked surprised and pained. His aristocratic friend and school-fellow from the back- woods then addressed the meeting and remarked that it was very easy to throw mud. Before he sat down he had demon- strated the ease of the operation. For instance, men . who were now members of his Majesty's Government "were willing at the time of the Boer War to sell their country to the Boers. They did not care. Any enemy of England was a friend of theirs." This kind of mud sticks only to the fingers of the thrower. No wonder there were cheers at what seemed the wrong places to Sir G ilbert and. his friend. Even a small and select Tory audience has its sprinkling of honest politicians. * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Winston Churchill had a wonderful reception on Saturday when he made his first visit to Warrington, and the two great gatherings of men cheered him to the echo. As a speaker he does not possess the fire of Lloyd George or the weighty deliberation of the Premier, but he is astonishingly effective in a quiet way. The young statesman's personality is magnetic, and his utterances betoken not only the man of thought and knowledge, but the possession of a positive genius for national affairs. His critical, satirical faculties are abnormal, and after taking his hearers through the tortuous maze of the unemploy- ment problem he switched off to a disquisi- tion on Tory manners which was full of delicious irony. The allusion to Lord Curzon's following him round "like a dog— I mean with the fidelity and persistence of a dog" brought down the house. We note,too, that it touched the hereditary pride of his lordship, for he could not refrain from a reference to the hit at a subsequent meeting in I3urnley, 'where he was once more follow- ing in Churchill's footsteps. The dog is likely to tire of the tour before the, leader, who has left a thorny pathway behind him. * * _</p>
        <p>Tariff Reform means higher prices. Mr. Churchill proved this to the hilt by a wonder- fully lucid illustration. He took two glasses and stood them on the famous despatch box before him. One was supposed to be German and the other English. "If" said he " the two glasses now cost is. each, and the foreigner was driven out of the market by a tariff on imported glass what would be the result ?" He hardly needed to explain. A combination between the English makers who held the monopoly at once suggested itself to every mind. The price of glasses would obviously rise. To what level? Mr. Churchill had already supposed the cost of the German glass, plus the tariff, to be is.' 6d., and the audience jumped at the answer to the ques- tion. There was a unanimous shout of' one and fivepence three farthings" which showed that the logical reasoning was as clear as the glass. It was hardly necessary to point out further that the foreigner would not be pay- ing the tax, but the purchaser at home. With glasses, so with other homely utensils, and so with adjuncts to the processes of manu- facture. Mr. Churchill capped the argument by saying that if the foreigner paid the tax we couldfall sit tight and presently retire on our ill-gotten gains. Tariff Reform was laughed out of court.</p>
        <p>* * It is a very strong proof of the complete demoralisation of• the Party which has stak edits political existence on the defeat-7- by any means, it matters not what—of the " Social Welfare" Budget, that it has actually fallen back upon a cordite " scare," not because it had 'the slightest ground for supposing that there was any deficiency in the Admiralty's cordite reserve, but because</p>
        <p>Fairfield Branch Technical School. Miss Dorothy Ashley has been appointed super- intendent of the Fairfield Branch Technical School in place of Mii3s Margaret Edgar, whose resignation has terminated this week. • . New Curate at the Parish Church. Mr. Thomas Vickers, 8.A., of Bishop Hatfield's Hall, Durham, was ordained deacon by the Lord Bishop of Liverpool on 'Sunday and licensed to the Warrington Parish Church. • Musical Successes. The following pupils of Miss Schofield, L.A..A..M. Priory School have been succeisful in the recent music examinations held at Liverpool• Centre : Dorothy Percival, Trinity College, Local \lnterme- diate Division : Clarice Ball and Edith Allen, Grade I. Incorporated Society of Musicians. MILLER &amp; Co., 10, Sankey at., Jewellers. Xmas Presents, great variety, from ls.—Advt. Presentation to Mr. S. W. Jeffrey.</p>
        <p>he teachers of Wycliffe school na-ade a presenta- tion yesterdsay'afternoon to Mr. S. W. Jeffrey of a set of fish knives and forks, in recognition of his services as teacher in the school for the past 25 years. Mr. Corney, headmaster, made the presentation and voiced the esteem in which Mr. Jeffrey was generally held and his appreciation of his services as colleague. Mr. Jeffrey suitably acknowledged the gift.</p>
        <p>PEAT FOR HOUSE FIRES, as. 9d. per 100 blocks, delivered free..—J. S. GARLAND" Kitchen Range</p>
        <p>and Stove Warehouse, 51, 53, g 55, Bridge it., Warrington.—Advt. Royal Court Theatre. Next week at the Royal Court Theatre there will be produced for the first time in any theatre T. Edward Ward's new play, the Company including the brilliant young actress, Miss Ward. The play is entitled "His Real Wife" and is a production in which emotion is well to the fore. With a new play and new scenery it is expected that there will be large audiences each night. The scenery is said to be of an exceptionally beautiful character.</p>
        <p>MILLER .tt Co. (opposite Trinity Church), for Whist Drive Prizes. Large Stock.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>" Dick Whittington " at the , Shakespeare Theatre.</head>
        <p>" Dick Whittington " at the , Shakespeare Theatre.</p>
        <p>As is ,nsual at this time of the year the Shakes- peare Theatre, Liverpool gives a seasonable produc- tion, This time it is" Dick Whittington and his Cat "the ever poptilar Christmas pantomime. It will be given on Tuesday. Wednesday, 'Thursday and Saturday of next week both afternoon and evening and twice every Monday, Wednesday, and Saturday until further notice. There will also be the ordinary evening performances. The well known artistes Miss Carrie Moore, Mr. Harry Randall and Mr. Tom Foy are supported by a brilliant company who are sure to interpret the story in a manner which will delight the public. FOR WHIST.DRIVE PRIZES you cannot do better than see MILLER &amp; Co.'s selection.—Advt. Local Railway Staff Changes.</p>
        <p>Several important changes in the district staff of the London and North Western Railway Company are about to take place. Mr. F. A. Sargent, who for the past three years has been district goods manager, has been promoted to Garston in connection with' the Company's extensive additions to the dock accommo- 'dation there. His assistant, -Mr. J. B. Parkhouse, has been promoted to the position of assistant district manager at London-road, Manchester. Mr. Sargent will be succeeded by Mr. E. Wharton from London-road. •Mr. Sargent has been in the service of the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Company 36 years, and during the time he has been in Warrington the company's business has considerably increased. His I removal and that of Mr. Parkhouse will be generally I regretted in the town, while they; will be heartily nougiatutitedlupon their well-earned promotions.</p>
        <p>AEROPLANES for the Boys.—Prices is., Is. 6d., 2s. 9d., Bs.6d, up to 30s. each.— J. S. GARLAND, Ironmouger, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Crampton. The many friends of Mrs. Crampton, wife of Mr. Thomas Crampton,nrovision dealer, Bridge-street,will regret to bear of her death which took place at her resi- dence,Viciotia-road,Gra ppenhall, on Tuesday morning, The deceased lady,who was the eldest daughter of the late Mr. litichard Roberts of Bridge-street, had been ailing for-some time. She underwent an operation in a nursing home in Manchester a little while ago, and when she returned home she rallied and was on a fair way to recovery, when five weeks ago a relapse. occurred and the case became hopeless. Mrs. Crampton, up to the time of her illness, was an active worker at the Wycliffe Congregational Church, and her loss will be felt very much. The funeral took place yesterday afternoon at tbe Warrington Cemetery in the presence of a good number cf people, who assembled 'to show their respect to the deceased lady. The Rev. John Yonge officiated. The mourners were :—Messrs Thomas Crampton (widower), Percy Crampton (son), P. P. Crampton, W. Crampton' and Rev. J. Davies (brothers-iti-law), W. Roberts, F. R. Roberts, H. Roberts (brothers), S. and T. Hildage, H. G. Roberts, and R M. Roberts. Amongst others present were Messrs. George Wallington, Linnmus Greening and B. Fairclough. The large number of wreaths included the following :—" Husband and children," Mr. and Mrs. P. P. Crampton, Mr. and Mrs. W. Crampton,Rev. and Mrs. Davies, Mr. and Mrs, W. Roberts, Mr. and Mrs. F. It Roberts, Mr. and Mrs. Henry Roberts' Mr. and Mt EL S. Hildage, Mr. and Mrs. T. Hildage, mr. L. Greening, Mr. and Mrs.. Hodgkinson, and the Misses Field house. XMAS PRESENTS. Large selection in silver and geld. Jewellery, Eleotro-plated and Silver Articles. —MILLER 84 CO., Sankey st.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Ellen Vanilla Disaster. . The funeral of one of the Warrington victims in the above disaster,Master Ernest Allen,took place at Liver- pool on Monday in the presence of a sympathetic gather- ing. Prior to the interment at St James' Cemetery a service was held at St. Luke's Church, one of the hymns sung being Mrs. Allen's favourite hymn, "Thou art coming, 0 my Saviour." The officiating clergy were .the Ven. Archdeacon Madden and the Revs. R. F. Winter, G. W. Swainson, H. D. Morgan and T. W. Dearnley, The coffin was of plished panelled oak, the brass shield bearing the following inscription :--" Ernest Allen, born 14th October, 1894. Died 3rd December, 1909." The Ven Arch- deacon Madden delivered an impressive address. The chief mourners were Mr. John Allen (father), Master Luther and Mr. Herbert Allen (brothers), Mr. William Gandy' Mr. John Gandy, Mr. Thomas Gandy, Mr. Henry Gandy, Mr. Elias Allen and Mr. Thomas Allen (uncles), Mr. Thomas Gangly, Mr. Percy Gandy, Mr. John 'Gandy, Mr. Harold Gandy, Mr. Harry A. Gandy Mr. Douglas Gandy, Mr. Sydney Allen and Mr. Thomas Allen (cousins), and Mr. E.' D. Philips and Mr.' F. 13roOk. Messrs. W. Atherton. J. Bibby and R. Lees represented Emmanuel Church, Warrington. Floral tributes were sent by the family, the teachers and scholars of St. Luke's day schools, his Sunday school class, Pleasant-street evening school, "Uncle, Millie and Dora," "Grandma and Aunt Ellen," Messrs. Allen's employes at Liverpool and Warrington, Mr. W. Gowan and family, Aunt Allen and family (Warrington), Mr. and Mrs. Heyes, "Elsie, Pollie and Edith," Miss Spinks, Miss Guinan. XMAg GIFTS of every description. See window display.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Wycliffe Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>In accordance with the well estabtished custom at Wycliffe Church OD the Snnday previous to Christmas day selections were given by the choir and friends last Sunday evening. There was only a fair attendance, but those who braved the elements were well repaid as the performance of the work was most impressive. The instrumentalists were ;—First violins: Messrs. W. Brown' T. L. Frain, A, Kuowles ; 'Cello .slr. H. Bond ; Skond Violins : Mr. W. trough, Mrs. H. Haviour ; organ, Mr. T. F. Wallington and BIM, Mr. R. Whitfield ; conductor, Mr. W, H. Payton, Mus. Bac. The soloists were Misses J. Armstrong, B. Hayes, Messrs. A,- Griffiths and F. 'Brown. A collection wits made in aid or thecboir funds.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND with warm water is an excel- lent gargle for sore throat, Colds, Coughs, &amp;c.,—Adv'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LIBERAL WARD CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WARD CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>MR. GROSFIELD ON TARIFF REFORM FALLACIES.</p>
        <p>The Liberal campaign in the Wards was continued on Wednesday evening when a largely attended and successful meeting was held in the Hamilton-street schools. Mr. James'Shaw presided and was supported on the platform by Mr. A. H. Crosfield M.P. And Mrs, Crosfield, Mr. C. E. Parker, Captain Crosfield, Coun- cillor Locker, Mr. P. Peacock, and Mr. J. Howes. The CHAIRMAN referred to the many changes of front on the part of the Tories and,pointed out that even some of the Lords themselves now professed a desire to amend that Chamber. When the Lords began to " mend "it was indeed proof that the Upper House required reforming. (Hear, hear), The pro- gressive forces were closing their ranks and all sections —Liberals, Free Traders, temperance workers, Free Churchmen and Nationalists—were uniting to pass the Budget and curtail the plwers of the House of Lords. (Cheers).__</p>
        <p>-Mr X. H. CROSFIRLD, M.P., who had a hearty recep- tion, said he was sore the great earnestness of feeling that one saw throughout the democracy in this struggle was due to the fact that British citizens realized how much there was behind the great controversy that was being fought out. The outcome of the Constitutional issues which the electors were called upon to decide would have its effect and influence in DEMOCRACIES ALL OVER THE CIVILIZED WORLD who were watching what England was going to do to-morrow. "I venture to say" continued Mr.,Crostield "that there is not in the wide world a friend of ascendancy or of exaggerated privilege who will not exult if the Lords are allowed to succeed in the attempt they are making to usurp power lathe State, and I am equally certain that there as not a friend of freedom who will not be discouraged and dejected if the British democracy are not true to themselves in this hour." (Cheers.) Discussing the fiscal question Mr. Grosfleld submitted the following</p>
        <p>SEVEN REASONS WHY TARIFF REFORM WILL NOT BEAR</p>
        <p>INVESTIG ATION':</p>
        <p>(1). It would artifi-Call-i--r-a-i-se Prices of the simple necessaries of life..</p>
        <p>(2). The increase of prices would mean that people would buy less and therefore Manuflsoturers would have to make less.</p>
        <p>(3). If the manufacturers make less, there must be less employment. (4). The imposition of tariffs would increase the cost of manufacture and strike a blow at our sea borne commerce.</p>
        <p>(5). Tariff "reform " would mean more unemploy- ment and inevitably produce industrial strife. (6). ft would encourage the inefficient manufacturer and place shackles upon the capable and enterprising manufacturer.</p>
        <p>(7)6 It would introduce into the politics of England the corruption which was rampant in the United States.</p>
        <p>Continuing, Mr. Crostleld said the Tariff Reform League was spending money like water and making lavish promises. But Protectionist promises reminded him of the familiar story of " the spider aud the fly." (Laughter.) So many tempting little bits were offered to us if we would only enter the Protectionist parlour, that one wondered why the Continental nations did not enjoy those morsels more than they did. (Hear, bear). It was instructive to compare the rates of wages paid in Protectionist countries with those which our own workpeople received. A Board of Trade return gave the following particu- lars of</p>
        <p>WEEKLY WAGES ?AID IN ENGLAND, PRANCE-AND GERMANY</p>
        <p>in the year 1905 : Trade England. France. Germany. s. d. s. d. a, d. s. d. s. d. s. d. Bricklayers Carpenters Plumbers Painters Builders' Labourers 23 6to 27 0 15 10 to 19 10 19 6to 24 0 Engineers' Fitters Another important point was that an English workman living in Germany as he had been accustomed to live in England would find his expenditure on rent, food and fuel, leaving out of account local taxation, increased by 19 per cent. or roughly one-fifth and in Prance be would find it increased by 14 per cent. or one-seventh. Thus, while there was such an enormous difference in the wages paid in Britain and other countries, the purchasing power of the wages in Britain was much greater. The hours of labour were also longer in Protected nations. He did not say that there was no room for improvement in England in that respect, and if he was again returned to Westminster, —(Cheers and a voice: "You will be ")—he wonld do his utmost in support of any effart to curtail the abominably long hours which many shop assistants bad to work. (Cheers.) Mr. Crosfleld also dealt with the Budget which he said was very truly called _ _ A PEOPLE'S BUDGET.</p>
        <p>(Cheers.) It had been carried through the House of Commons by an immensity of labour, without unfair- ness to one single class, and it was entixely in the: interests of the. nation as a whole. (Cheers.) Mr. Crosfleld was discussing some of the details of the measure when a member of the audience remarked "Never mind my bacca, don't speak about that." (Laughter). Mr. Crosfleld rdmarked : "I see my friend takes the tax on his tobacco like the artisan classes of the country as a whole have taken their share of the Budget—with a spirit of good humour 'and manli- ness which might with great advantage have been imitated in other quarters." (Cheers). Referring to the violent attacks on the licensing proposals, Mr. Crosfield said that when they considered the much higher duties levied on licences in New York as com- pared with England, they must come to the conclusion that Mr. Lloyd-George's proposals were ntoderate, especially in view of the long time which that monopoly had run on without the State getting anything like a fair share of the value. (Cheers). The land taxes did not inflict a scintilla of injustice on anybody and they were .to be supported not only for the revenue which they would produce but for the social consequences—the bringing of more land into the market, and the relief of con- gestion in the over-crowded centres of population. (Cheers.) Mr. Crosfleld concluded by saying that Liberals would go into the battle with a good heart, determined that none of them would be shirkers but that all would unite to carry the cause to victory. (Loud cheers). - -</p>
        <p>Captain CROSFIELD sail he was thototigly convinced that FREE TRADE WAS THE BEST THING FOR THE MASSES of this country. Dealing with the glass trade, be pointed out that the exports of glass from this country during the past four years had increased from £423,000 to £500,000. From personal experience he knew that labour exchanges had done an immense amount to pre- vent unemaloyment in Germany, where they had worked out the syßem very fully. (Cheers). Mr. C. E. PARKER said the Budget proposals would tend to give the poorer classes a rightful position and a greater stake in the country. With regard to our position in the commercial world, the exports from England during the past. five years had increased by £81,004,000; from the United States by £75,000,000; from France by £24,000,000; and from Germany by £58,000,000, Therefore</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WE STILL STOOD AT THE TOP OF THE TREE</head>
        <p>WE STILL STOOD AT THE TOP OF THE TREE</p>
        <p>and were not only holding the blue riband of the trade of the world but were gaining in our position. (Cheers). It, was all the mote remarkable when they considered what a Small country England was and how much less our population was than the population of the great competing countries. The exports from England were equal to about £9 per head of the popu- lation; United States £4 per head; Germany £5 per head. Consequently. we sent out for every unit of our population as much as the United States and Germany combined. We had increased our output per head of the population during the flye years by £2 per head; United States by £1 per head; Germany £1 per head. Mr. Parker emphasised the fact that trade was barter and the goods that came into this country must necessarly bepaid for by the goods that went out. As a rule there was far more of the labour element in the goods that went out than in those that came in. To tax the food of the people and thus make the lot of the poor harder was a cruel policy to put before the country. The Budget was big with hope and it was something worth fighting for. • (Cheers). Mr.J.HowEs dealt at some length with the question of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>COAL TAX.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TAX.</p>
        <p>Hesaw that Alr.Edwin NVoods bad stated that when the tax was on we exported 40,000,000 tons of coal and after it had been removed we exported 66,000,000 tons. Ther- efore, if we had kept the tax on the 66,000,000 tons it would have trought in £3,250,000 revenue. How was it, added Mr. Howes, that the exports went up to such a grent extent when the tax was taken off? It looked as if the tax prevented them going up. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Howes showed bow the imposition of the tax caused a reduction in wages to the miners of Notting- ham and Derbyshire and also deprived many coal trimmers of the opportunity of gaining a livelihood. The Government abolished the tax because they knew what an iniquitous thing it was. The CHAIRMAN was thanked for presiding. on the proposition of Mr. P. PEACOCK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>St. Paul's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Church.</p>
        <p>A good congregation attended St. Paul's Church on Sunday evening last when selections were given from the "Messiah." The choir was assisted by members of the congregation and friends. The principal vocalists were: Sopranos, Miss Gladys Toft, Miss E. Wilkinson ; con- tralto, Miss Nellie Anderton ; tenor, Mr. Levi Ridhard- son ; and bass' Mr. E. Walker. As the music proceeded short explanatory observations were made by the Vicar bearing on the solos and choruses. All the soloists sang with taste and expression but a special word of praise is due to Miss Anderton, The choir rendered the choruses in admirable style especially the "Hallelujah" Chorus. Mr. E. Wilkes ably presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. 90.6;1/11. Of Chemists only:Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD AND TORY "SCARES."</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND TORY "SCARES."</p>
        <p>Last evening a meeting was held in Fairfield Schools., There was a large attendance and splendid enthusiasm was manifested. Councillor J. T.. Locker presided and was supported on the platform by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.. Mr C. E. Parker, the ,Revs. C. Harvey-Cook and H. Wall, Messrs. G. Walker, J. Critchley, P. Peacock, E. Wynne-Jones, B. Farrar and E. England. Mr. CROSFIELD said he had read with a great deal of amusement and some astonishment the statements made at the recent Conservative meetings. Mr. Pierpoint had, in common with many others, associated himself with the attempt that was being made by the Conserva- tive party to revive the same scare that was raised last March and April, and at a meeting at Latchford he made the following statement about the conduct of the Governmnt with reference to national defence They had practically abandoned the old-fashioned notion that this country should have a navy equal. to any two countries in the world. But they were actually in danger at this moment of losing even the one power standard. At another meeting Mr. Pierpoint stated: They knew very well in their heart of hearts that unless a draßtic cnange was made, they would not be up to the one power standard let alone the two power standard." He then went on to say that the Navy ought not to be made a party question. (Laughter.) Warrington had also had a visit from one of the scions of nobility. (laughter and a VorcE : "They did not tell us he was coming ")—and this was what Lord Deerhurst had to say :—__ _</p>
        <p>This country was becoming dangerously near not only losing its supremacy as to the two-power standard, but of being outstripped by Germany altogether. Mr. Crosfield said , HE DIRECTLY CHALLENGED THOSE STATEMENTS. It was not the fact that we were in danger of being out- stripped by Germany in our naval defences, or that we were in danger of ceasing to maintain the one-power standard. On the contrary we had at the present moment a two-power standard and a great deal, more. (Cheers.) He bad only one qualification to make about that: Was America to be counted in? He had always held that it was the business of Britaln to maintain the full two-power standard with a margin beyond it, but that standard was to be reckoned as against the next two strongest powers in Europe. He called that reasonable, but .if they were going to reckon in not only the 66,000,000 of Germauy but the 88,000,000 of the United States and say that if the U.S.A. chose to take an aggressive line in naval expendi- ture we were bound to maintain our navy at a point equal to the strength of Germany and America, he told them frankly that that was a principle to which he had never subscribed and to which he was not prepared to subscribe. The ablest and best statesmen in London. would tell them that the time when war could be con- templated between the U.S.A. and Britain had passed and he believed that any question which might arise in the future would be settled by arbitration. (Cheers.) Having referred to the significant signs of the times in rapprochements with France and Russia, , Mr. Crosfield said he hoped that before many years were over there would be a much better understanding with , Germany. If he was again returned to Parliament ' —(cheers)--he woul dcontinue to vote fdr what was, necessary to maintain the unchallenged and unchalleng- able supremacy of Britain on the high seas. (Cheers.) To deal with another topic, when he (Mr. Crosfield) remembered how old age pensions were dangled before the constituencies by the Conservatives in 1895, then shunted, and trotted out again in 1900, he was surprised that Mr. Pierpoint should have told his hearers that "Lord Wolverhampton had said that THE MAN WHO MOST POPULARIZED OLD AGE PENSIONS, was that great statesman Mr. Joseph Chamberlain. (Laughter.) There was a great virtue in the word "popularised" but Mr. Pierpoint would have been nearer the mark if he had said that MraiChamberlain and the party associated with him were content simply to tickle the ears of the people with their promises. (Hear, hear.) Then he turned to what had been said by Mr. H. H. Field, one of thebreeziest, cheeriest,and most straight-from-the-shoulder men he knew, but who was just a little rocky on one or two politicallquestiorut. After describing the excellent• remedial legislation which the Government bad in hand as a" Socialistic botch-potob," Mr. Field, dealing with the fiscal ques- tion said</p>
        <p>He spoke simply as a business man and the prin- ciple which he thought should govern the country was discourage importation and encourage exportation. Did Mr. Fietd, or the firm with which he was associ- ated follow out that policy? They all knew very well that very large quantities of manufactured goods were IMPORTED BY MESSRS. RYLANDS BROS. and were of the greatest possible benefit to that great concern. 'lf Mr. Field told him those were only partially . manufactured goods, he (Mr. Crosfleld) would ask nim straight to give him a definition of manufactured goods and aisle definition of raw material. He (Mr. Crosfield) included a great deal in the term" rav? material "and he had yet to learn that for the work- ing class population of Lancashire food was not one of the most essential raw materials in the world, (Cheers.) Mr. Crosfield concluded by s tying that the case for Free Trade and the Constitutional issue on which the Liberals were fighting the election was impregnuble. (Cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FREE TRADE LEAGUE CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>FREE TRADE LEAGUE CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>The Free Trade League held highly successful meet- ings on Wednesday evening and last evening. The meeting on %i ednesdsy evening was at Thewlis-street schools, the chair being occupied by Mr. R. G. H.nshall in the absence of Mr. F. R. Roberts. The speakers included Mr. S. Rigby Armitage, Mr. .lames Ball, Mr. A. H. Rathbone, Mr. H. J. Leech (Manchester), Mr. E. Wynne-Jones and Councillor Hawthorn. . Last night's meeting was in Silver-street Scbools.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S MEETINGS. Next week there will be meetings on Wednesdayin the National School, Church-street, and on Thursday in St. James' School, Latchford. Further particulars are given on our front page.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>PEOPLE'S COLLEGE CONCERT.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE'S COLLEGE CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The teachers and scholars of People's College are to be congratulated upon the splendid success of their annual concert which was held in the Parr Hall last evening and worthily maintained the high standard set by its predecessors. The hall was filled with an appreciative audience and the children acquitted themselves in a way that spoke volumes for the excellent training they had received. Musically, the most interesting item was George Rathbone's setting of Longellow's poem " Vogelweid, the Minnesinger." This was an ambitious undertaking for a children's concert but full justice was done to the beautiful two-part singing and the effect was wholly pleasing. The chorus of 200 voices also acquitted themselves well in a number of well-chosen patriotic and old English songs and a Christmas carol. As usual solos were a pro- minent and enjoyable feature of the concert. They were given as follows :—" The little Dutch tile " by W. T. Wilkinson, "The Longshoreman " by Llewellyn Jones," " The little coon " by Morris " Lighterman Torn "by Harold Eccleston. There was also a 'cello solo by Edward Roy. There were several exceptionally attractive items by children in picturesque character costumes, including the gavotte from " The Gondoliers" and "In days of long ago (written by Miss E. Brown and &amp;imposed by Mr. W. H. Payton). There was a capital military swing about "Terrors Territorial " by a contingent of lads under the leadership of Harold Warburton, and great amusement was caused by the grotesque antics and wierd " music" of " Macnamara's band" conducted</p>
        <p>'by Sydney Pemberton. "The Bosun, the gunner, an! me" was given in capital style by Gartland, Grundy and Leigh. . _ . . _</p>
        <p>Specia—l praise is due to the following members of the staff who were responsible for the training of the boys and girls who figured in the character songs. &amp;c. —Miss E.Brown, Miss A.Wallington and Mr.l. Bowen. Mr. W. H.Payton,Mus.Bac., also performed invaluable service in regard to the musical instruction of the scholars and presided at the organ with his usual taste and skill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenell Gives .E !,000.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbe.t Greenall, Bart, has throur:h his firm, Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., brewers, given. £l,OOO towards the liquidation of the debt of £4,000 on Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary. The Infirmary stands in need of .E. 4,000 the accumulated debt of the last few years, brought about by. the additional cost of maintenance Since the magnificent extension was made at a cost of £15,000 the accommodation provided has been more than doubled.</p>
        <p>A million penny fund has been inaugurated, with Mr. J. H. Smethurst as chairman and Mr. Henry Collin as secretary, and it is towards this that Sir Gilbert has made the gift of £.1,000.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL CLUB'S GENEROUS DONATION.</p>
        <p>Last night the Committee of the Warrington Foot- ball Club decided to make a contribution of 5,040 pennies (£2l) to the fund. .</p>
        <p>SPECIAL COLLECTION TO-MORROW.</p>
        <p>The householders of Warrington and district are kindly requested to note that a collection will be taken by means of envelopes left by either a member of the Church Lads' Brigade or Boy Scouts in uniform on Christmas Day ' Morning and called for on Bank Holiday, December 27th. We hope the festive season will have the effect of putting the townspeople in a generous mood, and so helping on the big snowball of pennies for the Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>MR. CHURCHILL AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MR. CHURCHILL AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>TWO RECORD MEETINGS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>5,000 Voters Cheer Free Trade and</head>
        <p>5,000 Voters Cheer Free Trade and</p>
        <p>Free Government.</p>
        <p>Warrington was honoured with a visit on Saturday from the Right Hon. Winston S. Churchill, M.P., President of the Board of Trade, who addressed to great meetings in the Parr Hall and the Hippodrome. Ever since it became known that the Warrington Liberal Association had been fortunate enough to induce the eminent young statesman to include Warrington in his Lancashire tour, extraordinary interest had centred in the meetings. The demand for tickets was immense, applications pouring into the Liberal offices night and day, and but for Mr. Churchill's kindly consideration in agreeing to address an overflow meeting, some thousands of people would have been disappointed. Satur- day was indeed a memorable day in Warring- ton's political annals and Mr. Churchill's visit has undoubtedly done much to stimulate the enthusiasm and determination of the progressive ranks in the fight for popular liberties. At the Parr Hall the audience numbered over 2,000 and at the Hippodrome Mr. Churchill addressed another splendid gathering of about 3,000 men. A magnificent recep- tion was accorded to him at each meeting and the brilliant speeches in which he tore to shreds the Pro- tectionist arguments, exposed the fallacy that unemployment is to be remedied by food taxation, and stated the Constitutional position in calm yet forceful and telling style, were listened to with keen interest and freely punctuated with cheers. The arrangements for the meetings were admirable and reflected great credit upon the Liberal agent, Mr. J. Howes, jun. The elaborate precautions taken to guard against the intrusion of the militant suffragists were entirely successful. A large force of police under the direction of the Chief Constable, guarded the approaches to the buildings, and only those ladies who bad tickets, issued upon an under- taking not to disturb the proceedings, were allowed to enter the Parr Hall. Ladies were not admitted toi the overflow meeting. During the morning police officers found two 'suffragettes' from Manchester hiding in the Hippodrome. The ladies, who st once admitted that they were waiting for Mr. Churchill, capitulated without demur, and on giving their names and addresses to the police were allowed to go. • Both meetings passed off without untoward incident and will rank as probably the most successful ever promoted by the Liberal party in Warrington. The Parr Hall was filled with a vast and represen- tative audience. Around the building appeared the watchwords of the party. Above the platform was a streamer bearing the words "Peers or People, who shall rule," and other mottoes included " Our trust is in the people" "Remember old age pensions:" "Do justice to yourself and stick to Free Trade." To while away the time of waiting for the meeting to begin, the assembly sang with great gusto 'a number of Liberal songs, Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac. playing the organ accompaniments. All went with a fine swing but " When the Lords shall get their whacking," sung to the tune of "When the roll is called up yonder" was easily first in popularity. When Mr. A. H. Crosfield, entered escorting Mrs. Churchill, followed by Mr. Churchill and Mrs. Crosfield, the audience rose en mese and cheered tumultuously. "Auld Lang Syne " was sung and the scene was characterised by a fervour and enthusiasm never excelled at any previous meeting in the town. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., presided, ,and amongst those present on the platform were :—The Right Hon. Winston S. Churchill, M.P., and Mrs. Churchill, Sir W. J. Crossley, Bart., M.P., and Lady Crossley, Mr. A. J. King, M.P., Mrs. C. E. Parker and Miss Parker, Captain Crosfield, the Revs. John Yonge, Harvey Cook, J. B. Buglass, H. Wall and E. J. Ga wne, Mr. and Mrs. Ziba Armitage, Mr. and Mrs. James Dodds and Miss Dodds, Dr. Markel, Mr. F. W. Monks, Aldermen Tinnion, Pierpoint, Burton, and James Evans, Councillor C. Jackson Holmes and Mrs. Holmes, Mr. and Mrs. T. Pemberton, Mr. J. B. Aitken (president of the Widnes Liberal Association) and Mrs. Aitken, Messrs. R. IL Vosnett and A. R. Norman (Runcorn), Councillors Wilkinson, Locker, Birtles, Cunnington, Dickenson, Halliwell.Toole and Howard, Mr. and Mrs. E. Lloyd-Jones, Mr. and Mrs. E. V. Walker, Mr. and Mrs. G. Guest, Mr. and Mrs. J. G. Withinshaw, Mr. and Mrs. J. Longland, Mrs. Walker, Miss Walker, Mrs. Osborne, Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Locker, Mrs. F. Magin-n, Mr. and Mrs. W. Furness, Mrs. Griffiths, Mrs. Garnett, Miss Bessie Dutton, Messrs. A. Browne, J. Upson, T. Locker, J. A. Ransome, E. Wynne Jones, S. W. Jeffery, J. Jeffery, F. A. Harris, J. C. Holland, P. Peacock, W. Roberts, F. R. Roberts, A. Mounfield, Jos. Wilkinson, J. Critchley, J. A. J Morton, J. S. Hill, T. Critchley, Joseph Owen, P. L. Richardson, P., Winskill, G. Wallington, E. Wallington, J. Darbyshire, J. W. Bouchier, J. Bailey, J. Elwell, jun., T.Barnes, B. Farrar, W. Haigh, W. Bibby, J. T. Barton, W. Beswick, G. J. Brown, -.N. Cook, A. Cook, R. W. Cook, W. Clegg, A. Conley, A. Rathbone, W. Clatworthy, W. H. Sherbum, A. M. Crowe. P. O'Brien, J. Griffiths, T. Dale, A. Downes, J. B. Isaac, E. England, H. Gray, C. Healey, R. Henshail, C. H. Hibbert, J. Jarman, E. J. Mitchell, F. E. Penketh, T. Smith, A. Tilling, jun.' G.Walker, S. Wright and W. Wright; J. D. B. Smith and R. H. Gotobed (Lymm) and J. Howes, jun.</p>
        <p>questions of vast importance to the community. They decided four years ago that they</p>
        <p>WOULD NOT TOLERATE PROTECTION,</p>
        <p>because they did not approve of artificially raising the prices of the fir 4 simple necessaries of life to the millions of people in the county Palatine and in the country, and because they also realized that if they made everything dearer and people could buy less the demand upon manufacturers for goods would be reduced and there would be less employment for the workers. If they threw away the primary foundation of our stupendous over-sea trade—the priceless possession of low cost of manufacture—tbat trade must inevitably suffer to a substantial extent, and they would therefore add to the greatest curse of our time--,the curse of unemployment. Further, he did not believe the electors wanted those who had the privilege and honour of representing the nation in Parliament to breathe that</p>
        <p>POISONOUS ATAIOSPUERE</p>
        <p>generated from the corrupt and objectionable systems which unfortunately they saw in the United States and in so many other countries that were afflicted with Protection. (Hear, hear) They refused to have anything to do with Protection, preferring to go ahead with the social reform which so largely represented the sense of civic responsibility and social duty, the awakening of which was one of the most encouraging signs of the times. (Cheers.) The Government had undertaken that work and if War- ringtonians appreciated the honour of the visit that day—(cheers)--they appreciated it in no slight degree because Mr. Churchill was one of the foremost men who had helped on the great cause of social reform. (Cheers.) He (Mr. Crosfield) had the privilege of serving under Mr. Churchill on the committee which considered the question of sweating and they hoped by the institution of Trade Boards to do much to fight that evil of industrial and social life. (Cheers). In tackling the problem of unemployment they had to face two amongst other causes—immobility and lack of proper knowledge as to where work was to be found—and those factors were dealt with by the Labour Exchanges which they owed to Mr. Churchill. (Cheers) Those were illustrations of the remedial work with which the Government were proceeding when they were arrested by those irresponsible people in the House of Lords. (" Shame.") In conclusion Mr. Crosfield said : This is a red letter day in the political history of Warrington. (Cheers.) We honour Mr. Clinrchill for the brilliant work he has accomplished and we have all followed with admiration his splendid campaign, not only memorable ,in Lancashire but ' already ranking amoug the greatest political campaign in English history. (Cheers). We have read his speeches—fall of-originality and energy, packed with knowledge and close reasoning, lit up with irony and with positive genius, and when the issue conies to be I settled, Lancashire in that hour will not forget the admirable work which Mr. Churchill has done. (Cheers.) We know the spirit that prevails among the North country people, and we are confident that Lancashire is going to play a very great part in helping to wate a great chapter in the glorious history of England. (Cheer.) We hope that in. that day, for the name and fame of our old town, it may be given to Warrington to play a foremost part in Lancashire. (Loud cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>The CHAIR3IAN, who was greeted with loud cheers, said it was almost four years since they entered upon what many of them then thought was the greatest Constitutional struggle that they were likely to have to tackle in their life-time. Looking back on the records of English history and contrasting the condi- tion of our land before the introduction of Free Trade with what at least they might call the com- parative blessings, the great increase in prosperity and the well-being of the people which followed that great fiscal change, they felt it was an amazing thing that they should be asked to fight over again the. battle that was won for them by their great Free Trade statesmen over half a century ago (Cheers) There was, however, a still greater surprise in store, for they were now face to face not only with the old struggle in defence of Free Trade over again but with the even farther-reaching issue of whether they were to retain the inborn right of British citizens to govern themselves (Cheers). They all remembered what was said during the "RAGING AND TEARING PROPAGANDA"</p>
        <p>by a very able and powerful Conservative statesman— one of the many men of weight and character in the Conservative party who would have nothing to do with the fallacious and dangerous attempt to re-intro- duce Protection—who described that, campaign as "a gamble with the food of the people." Now, in addition to' the gamble with the people's food, there was a gamble with the liberties of the peoplt. (Cheers). The situation was well summed up by Sir Edward Grey —(cheers)—when he said that in fighting on that Constitutional issue they should go into the battle with their whole strength. (Cheers.) He (Mr. Cros- field) could not remember any other great political controversy in which there had been so pplendid a I common measure of agreement between all shades of opinion represented in the different branches of the I progressive party, and such united determination to carry the issue to success as happily characterised the present important crisis in our history. (Cheers.), Let them remember that it was not merely the atal mistake, the mistake of their lives which he Lords had made, that they had to con-. sider. They had to deal with a long record— (cheers and a voice : "Send them grouse-shooting ") in the course of which, time after time, •the Lords, many of them habitually absent, many of them quite untrained men, had gone down to their House and in two or three days had wiped out work which had cost five or six months' labour of the best brain and talent in the country in the representative House. (Cheers.) A telling article in the "North American Times" had thus Mated the consequences to our country if the present action of the House of Lords were condoned "It will mean that henceforward no Govern- . Ment will be able to remain in power which does not control the, Upper Chamber; it will mean that England, hitherto a supposedly democratic country,is really under the thumb of an oligarchy; it will mean that the Lords are the supreme power in the Constitutioh, able to dissolve Parliament and upset Governments as they please ; it will mean that the old principle that taxation and representation should go together has been destroyed." That was the dominant issue, but it involved ,other</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MR. CHURCHILL'S SPEECH. Solving the Social Problem.</head>
        <p>MR. CHURCHILL'S SPEECH. Solving the Social Problem.</p>
        <p>Mr. CHURCHILL, who - as received with loud and prolonged cheers and musical honours, said: Your member, our chairman for this afternoon, and soon to be your member again—(cheers),—has told us that ;unemployment is the greatest curse of our time. I think it is true that unemployment, which is the feature, the evil feature, of the growing and industrial character of all the great nations of the world, is one of the gravest and cruellest problems with which the rulers and the l'arliaments of every country are con- cerned. lam glad to think that unemployment in Warrington at any rate, has improved upon the condition at which it stood last year. The building and wire-weaving, at any rate, are better—so far As actual employment is concerned, althongh I am told there is short time in the wire-weaving trade—than last year, and there is an improvement generally, according to the trade unions' reports which are col- lected at the _Board of Trade, in the employment of this town compared with the state in which it stood at this time last year. CAUSE OF UNEMPLOYMENT. This grave problem of unemployment is common to all modern industrial countries. We see it in republics as well as in monarchies. We see it under the highest Protectionist tariffs as well as under the Free Trade system of our own land. The problem is one of special importance to us in England,- because perhaps more than any other country we depend upon manufactures for our wealth and our power. We depend less upon agriculture and more upon manu- factures than any other country for our wealth and power. If we did not depend upon manufac- tures we could not have reached our present great scale in the world of power and authority and because we depend upon this branch of industrial production so much, the special evil of industrial prodnction operates here with greater relative intensity. To seek the causes of that evil, and having found the cause to apply the remedy, must be one of tke main concerns of every thinking .man who occupis himself in public affairs. (Cheers.) • The causes are far -reaching and deep-seated, and they are in some respect obscure. We do not know enough yet of the phenomena of industrial production all over the world to be able to say that we have a solution of the problem. .If any man says he has a solution of the unemployed problem yon may be quite sure that he is a rogue. (Laughter and cheers). But certain main causes are discern;ble, and I have referred to them before in speeches which I have made in other parts of the country and also in the House of Commons when I have been trying to bring forward some of the contributory remedies which we have already begun to apply. The first evil feature I discern in our system of industry is that there is no machibery in the Government of the country or in the -industrial system for averaging the demand for labour so far as is possible. THE DEVELOPMENT ACT.</p>
        <p>The fluctuations occur, the demand goes up and then it goes down, very quickly, causing immense hardship to individuals and the families dependent upon them, and there is no machinery, such as we see in every steam engine, to regulate the speed and to moderate the fluctuations so that there shall be a steadier general level.—(Hear, bear.) Now we aro attempting in the Development Act which has been passed to begin to feel our way' towards something like that machinery, because this is an Act which enables public money to be spent on useful reproduc- tive- workfi, liko afforestation, canals, harbours, and roadmaking, which public works can be taken in hand and pushed forward very rapidly when unemployment is very bad, and which can be slacked off and not pressed forward as soon as things begin to improve.—(Hear, hear.) And in proportion as that system is applied more thoroughly we ought to be able in this country—not, indeed, to eliminate unemployment, not, indeed, to prevent the manufac- ture of unemployables, which is another point,—but certainly to give a greater measure of stability and sensibly to minimise and reduce the awful ups and downs by which modern production is characterised, and which inflict such hardships on the life of the ordinary industrial operative.—(Cheers.) '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>UNEMPLOYMENT INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>UNEMPLOYMENT INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>There are certain trades, like the building trade and the engineering trade, in which the fluctuations to which •I have referred are chronic and periodic. They recur from time to time. There is always a certain small percentage of unemployment, but every now and again there is a very sharp pinch of unemployment.. Those are trades in which, in addition to this other machinery of which I have sf3oken, there ought to be the machinery of insurance against unemployment. (Cheers.) The Government have decided that if they are responsible in another session of Parliament —(cheers)—they will introduce</p>
        <p>a bill dealing with this subject of unemployment insurance. That scheme has been occupying the attention of the Board of Trade for more than a year past. It is far advanced---(hear, hear)—the details have been worked out. We shall be able to provide for upwards of 21- millions of persons peculiarly subject to those fluctuations a system of State-aided unemployment insurance which, without injuring .in any way the trade unions which have already, done a 'great deal of work in this field—(cheers)—will extend to a much larger class than those who are at present able to qualify for trade union benefits something of the benefits which trade unionists at present enjoy. (Cheers.) We have been working it out for the last year, and I tell you frankly that I have not thought it worth while to stop the work which is being done. (Loud cheers and a voice, "You're a good judge,")</p>
        <p>CASUAL LABOUR AND THE EXCHANGES. Then there is another cause or evil tendency which leads to unemployment in our country, and that is the growth of casual labour—the system which makes it so convenient for each industry to have around it a certain fringe of ,workmen who are never fully employed except in times of great activity but who are habitually under-employed. In fact, I think you may go so far as to say that very few worthy citizens are altogether unemployed but a great number are under- eniployed.- They are employed only on certain days ,of the week or on certain months or weeks in the year, and although they may get good wages during the period of employment, if that period of employment only lasts for a small portion of the year or for an insufficient portion of the year they might just as well be receiving very low wages, for their high wages are practically taken away by the short time during which they enjoy the advantages of them ORGANIZING THE LABOUR MARKET.</p>
        <p>Now, the evils of casual labour are very largely to be limited by a better organisation of the labour market. In conjunction with these schemes of the Development Act and unemployment insurance there is a system of labour exchanges which will e4able us to pool all the available labour in 6ny particular industrial centre, and to see that, so far as it goes, it is divided fairly and equally among those for whom it is sufficient, a system which will enable RI man who wants employment to avoid the need of wandering to and fro in search of it, not knowing where to turn, a system which will give him the certainity of being able to find employment ir there is any employment for him, and save his wanderings if there is • no employment for him—that system must be, and has</p>
        <p>of the "nauseous cant of Winston Churchill."— (Laughter.) There was the Duke of Rutland, who said the Liberal party were a crew of political tatterde- malions.--(Laughter.) There was the Duke of Beaufort, who was reported good-humouredly to have said that he would like to see Mr. Churchill and Mr. Lloyd-George in the Middle of twenty couples of drag-hounds —(Laughter.) There was that patfiot, Sir -Grey Humberston Skipwith, of Leicestershire— (laughter),—who said that the Lord Adv( cate, my friend Mr. Ure—(loud cheers).—was one of the most unprincipled blackguards living, and that Mr. Lloyd- George ought to be shot----(laughter). and (continued this noble patriot) if there was an invasion of this country he would not only not help to repel the invaders, but would welcome them; he would hold out his hand to them, whereupon someone very pertinently said—" Do you think they would take it?" —(Laughter.) Sir A. Acland-Hood, the Chief Whip of the Opposition, said the members of the Govern- ment had faces like sausages or suet puddings.— (Laughter.) Do not imagine that I am making any complaint—(laughter)—or that I am seeking to excite your sympathy on behalf of those who are the object of the,e little courtesies.—(Laughter.) I recite these to you onlS7 that you may be able to judge of the qualifications that a leading representative of the Con-ervative party has to preach a homily on the subject of manners in politics. (Laughter) AUTHORITIES ON ETIQUETTE.</p>
        <p>I notice that Lard Curzon, having hoped that the Conservative party would never sully their fingers by anything in the nature of improper political methods, went on to say that with, every speech of Mr. Lloyd- George they had a new slander and in every speech of Mr. Ure they had an old lie. (Laughter.) Mr. Balfour, who is also a great authority on etiquette— (laughter),—introduced this controversy at the beginning of the Budget crisis when he made a speech in which he said he earnestly hoped that no meetings on either side should be interrupted or disturbed. I take the strongest possible view against all disturb- ances of public meetings. I think a shrewd inter- ruption, a fair point which occurs to anybody in the audience, made at the right moment is always justifi- able. I never object to this myself, and, on the contrary, I often make a bit out of it. (Laughter.) But anything in the nature of organised rowdyism or attempts to prevent speakers from putting their views fairly before an audience is an offence against demo- cracy of the most dangerous description. (Hear, hear).</p>
        <p>v 419?? 474 W 4"/ /</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>great nob!eman who comes to enlighten us in Lancashire and Cheshire made a little omission—l hope accidentally—from his programme. (Laughter.) THE BULWARK AGAINST POPULAR PASSION. Lord Curzon goes about the country telling is that the House of Lords is a bulwark against gusts of popular passion. (" Rubbish.") Surely nothing could be mare inconsistent than to say that at the present time. Why, the House of Lords is of opinion that the Budget is a thoroughly bad Budget, and yet we are told that if, in a gust a popular passion, the country returns a Government majority at the next _election the House of Lords is going to pass the Budget. (Cheers). I think that is a very prudent course on their part; but how about standing against gusts of popular passion? The chief charge which I bring against the House ,of Lords in my indictment which I have tried to make in the towns of this great industrial world in Lancashire and Cheshire—the main charge which I bring against them is that they use their power to play A PARTISAN AND A PARTY GAME</p>
        <p>—(cheers) that in regard to bills like the Old Age Pensions •Bill—(cheers)—and the Trades Disputes Bill—(cheers)—which they thought,and thought rightly had a gust of popular passion behind them, althougt. they declared that they believed they were injurious to the country, they let them through. But on the other hand, when there is a bill like the Licensing Bill, which I am quite ready to admit was not good electioneering, if eleptioneering were all you had to look after, which Ia&amp; quite ready to admit raised up powerful enemies for the Liberal party, although it attempted to grapple with a grave social evil—(cheers)—when there is a bill like that, no one is more quick than the House of Lords to try and exploit for the cheapest and pettiest purposes of party politics the advantages which may come to t in a gust of public passion. (Cheers). THE HOUSE OF LORDS A PARTY TOOL. So much for Lord Curzon's claim that the House of Lords stands against party passion.—(A Voice : "Hand him over to Kitchener.") If the Lords were to stand against gusts of public passion, whether those gusts came from Tories or Liberals, whether they were the gusts of passion which bring social reforms forward or gusts of passion which plunge us into a great war, if they stood fairly and firmly against all as an impartial second Chamber there would be something to be said for them.—(Cheers). It might not be a democratic instrument but it might</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>; • ------L----------_ _ -------_- ' ---="----r---L:-___---______ ____ _ _ ' l (I:1 4-'/A;#00:::::-----1' ,' , i ~ 7 _ :- . _ _ _</head>
        <p>; • ------L----------_ _ -------_- ' ---="----r---L:-___---______ ____ _ _ ' l (I:1 4-'/A;#00:::::-----1' ,' , i ~ 7 _ :- . _ _ _</p>
