        <p>( ____-...,</p>
        <p>_: - _</p>
        <p>:PRESSIONIST SKETCH</p>
        <p>been declared by Commission which sat on the Poor Laws to be• the first and the best method of dealing with the evil of casual labour. As that system comes to be developed it must increasingly tend—Ad I would commend this remark to the trade unionists present—to the standardisation of the conditions of life and labour in all classes and in all trades. (Cheers). EVILS OF BOY LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Lastly, there is the evil of boy labour, which is a very serious one indeed. There are any number -4 jobs open to boys, but a great many of them lead to nothing, and when the boy, having previously done men's work for boy's wages, grows up to claim man's wages for himself he very often finds that the trade has no more use for him. Very.often he haS changed his job four or five times and he finds that the years of youth have passed and he has learned no trade. His education has been neglected during that period; he has lost a good deal of what he learned at school at great expense to the State, and he has started out on life on no clear or definite line of advance. That is one of the reasons why such a large percentage of the unemployed appear to be drawn from young men in the prime of health and the main strength of their lives. That is another great evil with which we should deal by an educational process, a process which should make sure that every young man in the country during the years when character is being formed is learning as well as earning. (Cheers). TARIFF REFORMERS' "GLITTERING AND DELUSIVE CLAP-TRAP.'"</p>
        <p>You will admit that I am talking serious politics ,to you. (Cheers.) What a contrast it is to turn from these labourious and painstaking attempts which are being made by thinking men in every town, in every city in our land to . deal with the social evils which they see around them—what a contrast to turn to the glittering and delusive clap-trap of the Tariff " Reform " politicians ! (Loud cheers.) Turn to the man who is not facing any of these difficulties, who is not trying to look for the real cause or t find the real remedy, but is endeavouring to exploit the unhappiness of a section of our people in the towns, to turn their misery to his gain and profit, and by representing that every social evil results from the Free Trade system' is seeking to overturn that system and to set up in its place another system which, I have no hesitation in saying, will put money in the pockets of nearly all the intelligent men .who are advocating it. (Laughter and cheers). LORD CURZON'S LECTURE ON MANNERSO</p>
        <p>I am glad to see that my pilgrimage through Lancashire—(cheers) has led others to follow in my footsteps. I bee that no less a person than Lord Curzon—(laughrer)—has been selected to follow me about from place to place like a dog—(laughter) :—I mean with the fidelity and persistence of a dog. (More laughter). /‘ Last night Lord Curzon was addressing a meeting at Derby. He opened his speech with the usual lecture which the superior Conser,va- tive nobleman is always ready to supply on the sub- ject of good manners. (Laughter). He made •especial complaint that people use harsh language in politics. How deplorable ik is that the "manners of the mudlark ' find exhibition upon political platforms! Nothing amuses me more when I hear Conservative speakers indulging in this form of homily than to notice the extraordinary manner they contrive to be blind to everything they say themselves. (Laughter). A friend of mine in the House of Lords, a young member of the Government, Lord Denman—(cheers)-- made a very amusing speech in the debate the other day in which he reminded all the Tory peers present of the sort of things they said abotyLiberal politicians.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE COURTESIES,</p>
        <p>There was Lord Newton—(laughter),-."—who spoke</p>
