      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>OF MR. CHURCHILL AT THE GREAT MEETING IN PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>OF MR. CHURCHILL AT THE GREAT MEETING IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>MR. URE'S VINA)ICATION</p>
        <p>But it is all very well for Mr. Balfour to take this line At the time of the South African War, when those who protested against the war ran the risk of their lives, Mr. Balfour, so far from condemning the violent tactics a lopted by .the war party, endeavoured to excuse them. (Cheets). On more than one occasion too Mr. Ure, tile Lord Advocate— (cheers)—has been followed about the country by a peripatetic gang of Tory ruffians, who have invaded the towns where he has been making his speeches for the sole purpose of,breaking up his meetings or of interrupting him. I think this is a shame, because Mr. • Ure has made a certain argumentative charge against the position occupied by the Conservative party at the present time. He was challenged on the sub- ject .in the /most brutal manner. He faced the challenge, (Hear, hear.) He faced it in the House of Commons. I heard him, Be stood up in his place and made his case good before the House of Commons. (Cheers). Mr. Balfour was there and rose to follow him. He utterly failed—(cheers)--to justify the language which he had employed, and in the opinion of" the whole House of Commons he lowered his reputation in Parliament by not having the manliness to make a decent apology. (Cheers.) Now after having failed to meet Mr, tire face to face in the House of Commons and because they were unable to make their case good, where charges could be answered in proper form, you can scarcely descend to a lower level than to have Mr. [ire's meetings per- sistently interrupted by roughs and rowdies who are sent about from place to place. (Cheers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>TARIFF " REFORM " INCONSIS PENCIES.</head>
        <p>TARIFF " REFORM " INCONSIS PENCIES.</p>
        <p>So much for Tory methods and for Tory manners. Then Lord Curzon went on to deal with the fiscal problem and to tell us that the Tariff " Reformers " propose to raise the revenue by a uniform duty on foreign manufacturers accompanied by a preference on Indian I,nd• colonial produce. To begin with, that is an entire departure from what-Mr. Chamberlain and all the rest of the Tariff " Reformers " have always told us, because they have told us it would be an average duty on foreign imported manufactures—that is to say, lower than 10 per cent. on some classes and lower on others. But Lord Curzon tells us it is.to be amniform dead level over the whole area. But I pass these little inconsistencies by. (Laughter.) They are all fundamental to their scheme, but you never find two Tariff "Reformers " who have the same scheme. (Cheers.) I ask you to examine the, actual words which he used: "A uniform duty on foreign manufactures, accompanied by a preference on Indian and colonial pi oduce." I don't think it is possible to state the case for Tariff" Reform " in a more disingenuous manner. For this reason—that the whole essence of a preference on Indian and Colonial produce is that it must be- given upon Colonial produce by the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TAXATION OF FOOD,</head>
        <p>TAXATION OF FOOD,</p>
        <p>The taxation of food is no doubt a very awkward thing for a Conservative speaker to talk about. Lord Curzon did not yvish to bring it into his speech, but he wanted to talk about the advantage of taxing foreign manufactures-and Colonial preference, and he carefully uses a phrase which enables him to put forward his policy in what looks like an attractive form but to keep the essential part in the background. That is what I call disingenuous political argument. (Hear, hear.) I have no hesitation in saying that if the policy of Tariff" Reform " is unaccompanied by the taxation of food it will not raise the revenue needed at the present time and you will not be able to give a preference worth having to the colonies. What did Mr. Chamberlain say over and over again in 1903 and 1904?. "If you give a preference to the colonies you must put a tax on food.' Therefore this</p>
        <p>nevertheless be a useful -national instrument. But inste id of that the House of Lords is a part, tool and nothing but a party tool.—(Loud cheers.) Lord Curzon went further and said the members of the House of Lords had not got to make promises ; they were not worried with all this electoral business. (Litughter). Quite true. What do they care what the people say or think? It does not matter to them; they are not answerable to anybody. (Cheers). They are not, says Lord 'Curzon, forced to make promises which at ia subsequent/ period have to be most reluctantly redeemed. But then he proceeds, at Derby, to make some of the most remarkable, some of the most unscrupulous— •</p>
        <p>BARE-FACED AND UNSCRUPULOUS-PROMISES that have ever issued from the mouth of a serious and responsible politician.—(Cheers). TAXING THE FOREIGNER.</p>
        <p>He began by assuring us that we can raise the revenue necessary t meet the needs of the country by taxing the foreigner. (Laughter.) Now any - man who believes that must be a very simple and unintelli- gent fellow, (Laughter and cheers.) We know Lord Curzon is not either simple or mintelligent, and my conclusion is that he certainlidoes not believe that we can raise the necessary revenue for this country by taxing the foreigner. (Laughter.) However, he says so. (Laughter.) That is a pledge w hich I hope, if he should come into power, he will not be reluctant to redeem,' because I am very anxious to see how the foreigner is going to be taxed. (Laughter) It would be a very interesting thing if we could raise all our revenue I.)y taxing the foreigner. Why we could all retire. (Laughter.) 'There would be no need for us to work any more. (Laughter.) We should simply have to sit will and tax,the German and. the French- man, the Italian and the American, and all these good people would come and pour their wealth into our lap. (Laughter, and a Voice: "I don't think.") Then he tells us that Tariff " Reform " will stimulate home industries without raising prices. Now how can you stimulate home industries without raising prices? Cheers.) If Protection means anything,it means better prices. The argument for Protection is that we are under- cut by the foreigner. We are undercut, so we are told, because'he brings his goods here cheaper than we can make them, and we have to lower our prices to a level at which we cannot profitably produce. STIMULATING HOME PRICES.</p>
        <p>And what is the remedy they propose? To shut the foreigner out so that we can raise our prices. That is the Protectionist argument. They would raise your prices and certain advantages—and certain disadvantages—(laughter and cheers)—would follow. But Lord Curzon wants to have all the advantages and none of the disadvantages. (Cheers.) You are to stimulate home industries by raising prices, and yet you are not to raise prices by raising prices. (Laughter.) This is what we expected from these peers who are not at all swayed by the influence of the crowd, who do not have to bow the knee at election times, who never have to make promises which they cannot fulfil or which they reluctantly or do not redeem, like the promise of old age pensions. (Laughter.) Lastly, Lord Curzon assures us that Tariff "Reform," this</p>
        <p>WONDERFUL PATENT 3f EDICINE which is to cure all social and human ills besides raising revenue without raising prices, will also cure unemployment. I hope that in the few words I spoke about ungm- ployment I showed that it is not a subject to be treated with such cynical levity as that. But the fact that such statements are put forward by men of the highest eminence for party purposes at this juncture, in the hope of catching votes at this critical election, forces me to revert for one moment to the very ,simple pro-</p>
        <p>cesses of Free Trade argument on which the grand sim- plicity of that case depends. (Hear. hear.) Ask your friends in the mills, in the shops, in the streets or in your homes: How will a tax on food cure short time and unemployment? We are told that a)k our evils come from Free Trade. All .will be cured by the adoption of Tariff "Reform." Let us be told how the process is going to work. Short time is an evil. Dear food is an evil. Why one evil should cure another; what connection there is between one evil and the other I have never been able to see. A SIMPLE ILLUSTRATION.</p>
        <p>May I put it to you in a very simple illustration, which I have used before in Lancashire, but which enables people, I think, to follow the argument very clearly? You see these two glasses (indicating the two drinking glasses on the table). ThiS is not a conjuring trick, I may say. (Laughter.) This one is a German glass, we will assume, and the other is a British glass. We will assume, further, that the German glass can be made at is. and that-the British glass cannot be made under Is. id. Along comes Lord Curzon, or somebody like him—(laughter)--: and declares that he will rid the British producer of the competition of the German glass. "I will give him a tariff which will keep out the German glass," says Lord Curzon. We will assume that the tariff is a high one, and that a duty of 6d. is put on the German glass, xhich, with the duty, will now cost is. 6d. After "That duty has been put on, how much do you think you will have to pay for the British glass? (Several Voices : "One and fivepence three farthings.") Is not it clear that the British glass will rise in the market to the level at which it would again be cut in upon by the German article after it has paid the duty? You may reply, "No, because there is competition among the people who make glasses in our own country which beats down the price of the British glass, so that although the price of the British glass might rise, it would not rise nearly so much as you Ay. But see what follows. When the people who make glass in this country know that the German glass is out of the way and shut out, they put their heads together and say to one another, "Now we have got rid of the foreigner, why should we be such fools as to cut our own throats ?" And then you get the great combines. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>THEN COME THE GREAT TRUSTS, which we see in Germany and the United States, which are in many cases deeply detrimental to the welfare of the. country, and which inflict the greatest injustice upon the home consumers—great rings and corners organized among the capitalists in order to raise artificially the whole of the prices of articles within their own tariff protected country and to raise them much more even than the duties which the Government have imposed. This is the evil which we see in every country where a high tariff is in force. Behind the tariff comes the trust. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WEALTH AND THE COST OF LIVING.</head>
        <p>WEALTH AND THE COST OF LIVING.</p>
        <p>To those of you who are wire weavers,and who manu- facture things that are affected by foreign competition it would seem very nice to get rid of the foreigner and to be able to charge a little more for the things you make. It is to the interest of anybody manu- facturing things to get as much as possible for theta and to pay as' little as possible for what pverybody i else makes. But what s done for the maker of one article is not going to be done only for him. No doubt there will be a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>LOT OF LOG ROLLING</head>
        <p>LOT OF LOG ROLLING</p>
        <p>and some ugly rushes made, but broadly speaking a protectionist system must be applied to every trade, and while everybody will be able to charge a little more for" what he sells, everybody will also have to pay more for what he buys. You u ill have called everything in the country double the value and you will imagine you have doubled your wealth. (Laughter and cheers). You will have increased the cost of every article in the country, and you will imagine that you. have ,the the wealth of the population. The wealth of the population will remain absolutely unaltered in any beneficial degree, but what about the cost of living?' 'Everyone knows that, of course a tax on food Will mean a reduction in wages; but a tax on manufactures Will equally mean an increase- in , the cost of living. (Cheers). Let anyone go home and look round his parlour or his kitchen. Let him see how many articles the cost of which would be raised in price if it were possible for the manufacturers of those articles to make a- ring in this country and raise them against him, and let him consider how mall the pressure of life would be increased npon every class of the 'population. , Then I turn from the worker to the producer, the great manufacturer. We have been 'told to think of the producer and not the consumer. But the greatest producer is the greatest consumer,—(Cneers). The great Manufacturer ia the man who consumes Most and let anyone who is familiar with a great cotton mill or a great engineering shop go into it and con- sider what the price of the finiithed article would be if everything in the shop or the mill cost just a little more at every stage of its creation. lie would then be able to estimate wliat an enormous drag on our competitive power in the neutral markets and, what a tremendous drag on our consuming power in our home market would be the effect of this insane and meretricious system which Protection would involve. (-MEAT CLASS, SOCIAL, AND ECONOMIC ISSUES.</p>
        <p>I don't, of course, deny that the Budget raises the fundaruental issues which divide the historic parties in this country. I don't want particularly to embitter those issues, but I don't think we need to disguise them. They are great class, and they are great economic and social issues. Our opponents believe that the revenue could be better raised by a protec- tionist tariff. We are confident that FREE TRADE FINANCE AIi:ONE WILL STAND THE STRAIN (hear, hear),—and our proof of that will be when the Budget passes, as pass it will— (loud cheers),—the great revenues which it will raise in future years— growing and expanding revenues year by year—out of which we shall be able to finance a policy of social reform. Another proof is the total failure in Germany of all these protectionist measures of raising the necessary revenue to carry on the Governmeut. Our opponents believe that we ought to rely more upon indirect taxation. We believe, on the contrary, that the great object is to make the cost of living as low as possible —(Hear, hear.) The tendency of the last sixty years ,has been to lessen the proportion of indirect taxation, and to increase the proportion of direct taxation. If you look at the published sheet of direct and indirect taxation for the last sixty years you see that as the people have got more power, as more have voted at elections, as the franchise has been extended, as government has become more popular and democratic, so the contribution of the indirect taxpayer has got less and less and less and the contribution of the direct taxpayer has got gradually more and -more. We believe that it is a beneficial tendency. We believe that it is a_ profoundly democratic tendency. We believe it represents the</p>
        <p>DIFFERENCE 13ET1%7EEN THE TAXATION OF WAGES AND THE</p>
        <p>TAXATION' OF WEALTH (cheers),—for indirect taxation is mainly a tax on wages, and direct taxation. is mainly .a, 'tax on the accumulated profits of the capitalist. We believe that is.a beneficial tendency. We don't desire to see it reversed, as it would be reversed, by a system of Tariff "Reform." We desire to see it maintained and extended, until in the end, as I said in .the House of Commons, the burden of the State is reposed upon the superfluities of the rich, upon the monopolies of the means of production and distribution, and upon the luxuries of the whole people which don't in any way affect their physical strength.—(Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>METHODS, AIMS. AND OBJECTS</head>
        <p>METHODS, AIMS. AND OBJECTS</p>
        <p>And if the two great parties are disagreed as to the methods by which revenue should be raised, still more are they disagreed upon the methoOs, aims, and objects in which and for which revenv should be spent. I think both political parties are inclined to admit that we are approaching a serious period in the history of our country. We see new forces all around us, and they are not all friendly forces. We see new conditions in the world, and they are not all conditions which suit our national life. I think there is much to be said for those who hold that a very vehement effort must be made now if we are to keep the great place we have won and held so long in the world. (Hear, hear),</p>
        <p>But our opponents repde their faith so largely, almost entirely, upon the mere</p>
        <p>MULTIPLICATION. OF NAVAL AND MILITARY ARMAMENTS. They Chink that by simply' adding one ironclad ship to another they are going to succeed in making this country secure against all those dangers and difficulties and in maintaining its supremacy in the world. We take a broader view. (Cheers). We decline to measure the strength of great nations in ternis of physical force. (Cheers) We believe that . the health and the vigour of the people is the only measure of the abiding strength of a nation—(cheers) —we believe that the happiness of cottage homes is the only true standard of national glory—(cheers)--we think that .men and men only must be the test of national greatness and world security; we believe that if our country is to remain great and famous in the</p>
        <p>world we cannot allow the social and industrial diseases which we see at present to continue, with all their profound moral and physical reaction. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>THE BUDGET POLICY</p>
        <p>We come before yon with our Budget, unfolding a policy not merely of finance but of social organisa- tion. (Cheers.) It demands sacrifices from all classes, it affords security to all classes, and by its means we shall certainly arrest many processes of misery and waste which ne see at work in our country and which, if not arrested, must create serious, if not irreparable, dangers in the future. That is the policy of the Budget —(cheers)--those are the issues which the policy of the Budget raises (Cheers.) That is the policy which has been brought to a stands! ill, which has been cast out and shattered by the recent vote of the House of Lords.</p>
        <p>They had no right to do it. (Loud cheers ) They had no legal right to touch the question of finance at any point. Finance belongs exclusively to the House of Commons. (( beers.) Your fathers and your grandfathers Ivou!d never have borne that it should be intruded upon at any point by, a hereditary and unrepresentative assetAly, and the House of Lords tbemselvcs would never have dared twenty year; ago or fifty years pp, or a hundred years rgo to lay their' bands upon the whole financial system of the country in the manner in which we have seen them act in the last few weeks. (Cheers) THE DOUSE OF LORDS.</p>
        <p>We say to you that the time has now come to deal with the House of Lords. (Loud cheers). Their veto is an abuse. It has long been used in a class interest. At last it has been used in violation of the Constitution, in an invasion at once of the prerogative of the Crown and the rights of the Huuse of Commons. (Cheers.) We do not propose, if power be given to us, to leave them in the position to make these assaults upon free government in the future. (Loud cheers.) We ask you to give us the the power by returning the Govern- ment with an ample and overwhelming majority— (cheers)—to</p>
        <p>CARRY 'rim BUDGET UNALTERED AND UNMODIFIED in any\ respect--(loud cheers)-- ; to carry out the social policy dependent upon the Budget and for which the Budget is to pay ; to carry through the land taxes--(loud cheers) contained in the Budget ; and to resist the food taxes which are the only alter- native to the Finance Bill. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>But more important than all, we ask your support in abolishing for ever the veto of the House of Lords on finance, ard in so effectively restraining its veto over legislation that within the lifetime of a single Parliament the will of the House of Commons duly elected shall prevail.-- -(Loud cheers). Those are the issues before you at this most memorable election. Those are the specific and definite objects for which we ask you to fight. They embody all the main causes upon which the interests of democracy repose. They are, every one of them, causes which are in the full stream and forward march of the progresA of civilisation. They have with them the whole move- ment of the age, and behind them the real interests of the great population crowded together in these small islands.</p>
        <p>If you stand together at this juncture you are bouLd to win a great and memorable victory. The security of property will remain unimpaired. The stability of society will be strengthened by a better social oronisation. We shall march forward to meet the dangers and difficulties of this mod, rn age strengthened by the might of a free, healthy, and con- tented people *free from the abuses of monopoly and from the restraints of hereditary privilege.--(Loud and prolonged cheers). THANKINC MR. CHURCHILL. Speeches by Mr. A. J. King, M P., and Sir W. J. Crossley, Bart., MP.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. KING, M.P., in prorosing a vote of thanks to Mr. Churchill, slid tie President of the Board of Trade was one of the youngest, most earnest nail pro- gressive members of our splendid Cabinet. (Cheers.) With regard to his (Mr. King's) constituency, the Knutsford division, although the population was scattered he believed it was just as convinced and e .rnest in its determination to sapped this great Government and this gnat Budget as was the electorate of Warrington. (Cheers.) Mr. King contrasted the composition of the two Houses of Parliament, pointing out that the House of Commons represented in small compass practically every shade of opinion and every interest in the country, whereas the House of Lords was chiefly representative of the land and liquor interests. One reason which had been suggested for their rejection of the Budget was that they had mules bargain with " the trade"—that the liquor interest raid : "You shut out the Budget and we will find the money for your election campaign." He hardly liked to believe that, but if it were true, what a spectacle for a free country !—that the free House of elected representatives should be controlled by a hereditary Chamber which in turn was controlled by "the trade." The only other explanation be could think of was that the House of Lords on this occasion bad been advised by the same advisers as directed them ten years ago to enter upon another campaign. He could only believe that the result of this campaign would be as disastrous to them as in the former case. 'I he men who advised them before were Lord Lansdowne and Lord Milner. These same men were advising them to-day. They told the Government of that day that the Boers would not fight, that they were a degenerate race and had lost the spirit of their forefatbers. He could imagine Lord Milner to-day whispering into Lord Lansdowne's ear "The people of this country are a degenerate race. They have lost the spirit of indepen- dence of their forefathers. They are not the same type of men who won those liberties and they will not stand up to defend them." He (Mr. King) hoped the people would let the Lords know that they were as much mistaken on this occasion as they were on the lest. (Cheers.) We were told that the super tax on large incomes, the death duties and the taxes on land were £0 bi avy that they were going to destroy confi- dence and drive capital out of the country. (A voice ; "To Monte Carlo.") The interrupter was quite right (Laughter.) It was an easy thing for the House of Lords to postpone the Budget until after an election. It meant nothing for them but a nice and pleasant holiday, spent where they would, but for the House of Commons and the country it meant a burdensome, toil- some and expensive period. Dealing with the cotton trade, Mr. King referred to the present scarcity of raw material and said that when he thought of the immense amount of capital which had been expended in the erection of new mills during the past few years, he believed it might have been better for the Lancashire cotton trade to-day if that capital instead of being kept at home bad been sent to our Colonies, and spent in growing more cotton and raw materials. (Cheers). After all, our cotton trade was living very largely on the result of the export of British capital to other countries. Mr. King instanced the great continent of South America, a splendid, fertile, productive country, capable of growing any amount of produce, food and raw material. Twenty or thirty years ago that continent had only a comparatively small population. What happened ? Owing to our Free Trade prosperity we in this country had been able to save up enough capital not only for our own needs but to send out to Argentina and the other great countries in South America. We had built railways there, laid down tram- ways, developed waterworks and other undertakings and opened up the country to a large population. That capital did not go abroad in the form of golden sovereigns. It went, in the form of iron rails, wire fencing, railway engines, wnggons and all the material which was needed by that country and which was manufactured by our own people. When the capital got there, were the people who sent it content to have no interest on their money? Not at all, and the interest came back to this country year by year in the form of foodstuffs and raw materials for our trade. But that was not all. The capital had been the means of building up a thriving and prosperous commnnity whose requirements of manufactured goods increased annually and which we were able to supply. (Cheers.) Sir W. J. CROSSLEY, Bart., M.P., in seconding, said Mr. Churchill had done yeoman service in,lais tramp through Lancashire. (Cheers.) He had told them that he. wanted ti.big majority to back up their leader, and if he (Sir William) knew anything at all about the country be thought he would get it. (Cheers.) "I sometimes ask myself" continued Sir William "What deluded the House of Lords to take this step. Why have they dsie it? They have jumped into our arms. (Laughter.) . We were longing for it. We wanted them to do it. We wanted to go to the country on this great issue and we have very little doubt about the result. (Cheers.) Just think what has happened. Here we have been working six mouths on this Budget. In less than six days the House of Lords throws it out. We have been paying all the wages of the great Govern- ment departments, paying for shipbuilding, for the army and navy, and now they refuse to sign our cheque and leave us without anything to pay our men with (Laughter.) Such a system cannot be tolerated any more. (Hear, hear.) It is no use talking about the legality of it. They have done it and what we have to do is to be pra?tical and to prevent its ever being done again. (Cheers) We are out for business this time. (Cheers.) We all mean it. We are not going to surrender our liberties to the House of Lords. It is going to be a fight to the finish. It is vital for each side. If we lose, and if by the return of a Tory majority we condone the action of the i House of Lords they can go on doing t. On the other hand, if we win the House of Lords can never do that sort of thing again. (Cheers.) I don't think there is any fear of the result and what I cannot understand is why they have jumped into our arms in this way. I cannot help thinking the advice on which they acted vas the sort of condensed tittle-tattle that came out of</p>
        <p>the public houses, the talk of the rcen with a little drink in their heads will were so very brave, and was brought by the brewers to the ears of the House of Lords. (Laughter.) Mr. King blames it on Lord Milner—the man who said the Boers would bolt at the first shower of rain. Lord Milner is a dangerous ady'ser. We have had enough of him. We are not going to allow our food t,9 be taxed. In Lancashire we have an old saying If thou &amp;est 'o vt for nowt do it for thisen: (Laughter.) We are gting to give our votes for our own interest. We knqw too much of what putting taxation on the price of f oh means" Sir William don It at length with the queqi it of food taxation and pointed out that Pro!ection had c used Germany to pay for corn in 1905 £10,000,000 more than if that c rrn had bean bought it Mark Lane. Only .£8,000,C0 of that went int') the pockets of I.IIA G ivern- ment and the rest, wont to the farmrr, laudlords and others.</p>
        <p>- The vote wds enthusinstivillycarrled rni the met ting closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>(Continued from previous page).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>OVERFLOW MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE OVERFLOW MEETING.</p>
        <p>MR. CHURCHILL AND 'THE LAND MOVOPOLY.</p>
        <p>Speech by Mr Hamar Greenwood, MP.</p>
        <p>The overflow meeting was held in the Palace and Hippodrome and was attended by 3,000 men. Mr. C. E. Parker presided and others on the platform veye Mr. Hamar Greenwood, MP. (York), Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Councillors James Haliiwell and J. T. Locker, Messrs. T. Pemberton, J. Critchley, R. Lewis and E. Wynne Jones.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, who had a rousing reception, said he felt very happy when it fell to his lot to take the chair at an overflow meeting. (Applause.) Referring to the " Examiner," he said that liberals should support the paper which supported the Liberal cause. lie was delighted with that magnificent meeting, and at the magnificent meeting that he had just had a look at in the Parr Hall, which showed them that apart from the magnetic personality of Mr. Winston Churchill— (cheers)---the democracy cf the country and the democracy of this district were thoroughly roused in regard to the great constitutional issue. (Cheers.) The Lords had done their best to do the Budget to death and if they had not managed it they had banded it over to the people and asked them to bury it decently. The " corpse "of the Budget was however, not dead. (Cheers). It sat on the box with the driver of the coach who was the Premier, Mr. Asquith (loud cheers)— and the chief guest, now no longer a mourner, was Mr. Lloyd-George. (Loud and pro- longed cheers.) They were going to turn that hearse into a triumphant coach and put the Cabinet ministers inside with Churchill on the step. (Laughter and cheers.) If there w.,s not room inside for the man who would-be the member for War' ington they would put him on the top along with the member for Knnts- ford. (Laughter and cheers.) It would not be to the cemetery that the coach would go; they were not going to take the Budget to the sime place where the Lords had buried so many Bills in the past. That coadi would go straight to Buckinghatn Palace and Mr. Asquith would say " TIIIS BUDGET IS THE WILL OF THE PEOPLE (Cheers) and the King would say " The will of the people is my will ; I have no other will but the .will of my subjects " and he would endorse the Budget with his name. (Cheers). The Liberal Prime Minister backed up by those who led the party would take the Budget and would get the 8 Dreadnoughts and we would not have to wait. (Laughter and cheers). They would have enough money for old age pensions- -(cheers) —and they would abolish the disqualification of poor relief.(Cheers).They would also be able to carry out the great social reforms contained in the Budget. (Cheers). In the future they would look back upon the year 1909 in the same way as we did on the year 1832 when the great Reform Bill was passed and also upon the Vine of the Corn Law Agita- tion. Ile was rather proud of the fact that his grand- father took a leading part as president of a Liberal Association in the fight for the passing of the Reform Bill. (Applause). He was glad to say that his father took his part in the agitation in reference to the Corn Laws and he (the speaker) was glad to be able to take his part in this great struggle. (Hear, hear). And when his work would have pas,ed away and when he would have crossed the great River he hoped that his soon too would look upon the fact with some Pride that his father took a little part in the events of 1909 and be hoped that their (the audience's) children would have the same reason for pride that their parents had taken a part in the great events of 1909 and in returning Mr. Crosfield. (Cheers). Mr. GREENWOOD who made a very able speech, with- out the aid of a single note, and held the rapt attention of the audience for about one hour and twenty minutes until the arrival of Mr. Churchill, said that he was extremely pleased to be in the constitutency of a friend like Mr. Crosfleld, and it was also a pleasure to address a meeting and endeavour to hold its attention until Mr. Churchill arrived. (Cheers). We were going to have a General Election in the middle of January— one that hat been cast upon the country by the rejection of the Budget by the hereditary peers—a Budget wh!ch passed the House of Commons with a majority of 230. The first point to remember was that never before in English history had a Budget been rejected by the Upper Home. The second point to remember was that never before in English history bad a dissolution of Parliament been brought about other ' than by the wish of the King acting under the advice of his Ministers. On this occasion the House of Lords including</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>CZAR LANSDOWNE</head>
        <p>CZAR LANSDOWNE</p>
        <p>—(laughter and so-Me-ap-p-lause)—who ought to have been a Russian and not an Englishman—(cheers)—bad ignored the customary prerogative of the Monarcbry and without consulting the King bad rejected the Budget and had forced a dissolution.Therefore,they could understand that when the King in his last speech to Parliament,said "1 regret that the efforts of the House of Commons have been unavailing to provide for the year's expendi- ture," he meant what he said. (Cheers). In this con- test Mr.Crosfield stood for the Commons and the King— (chcers)--and so did all those who were Irishmen,Labour men and intelligent Conservatives. (Cheers). They were prepared to vote all the money the King wanted through the House of Commons; they were rot prepared to bow the knee to the hereditary upper Chamber and be dictated to by them, first as to when a dissolution should take place and second as to how taxes should be raised or spent. (Cheers.) The House of Lords was composed of over 500 Conserva- tives and there were about 60 Liberals and some of them were a bit gone at the knees—(laughter)— especially those who were the sons of tbeir fathers. (More laughter.) Whilst a man who went into the House of Lords by reascn of his own effort was generally a man of some capacity it did not follow that a son had any capacity. In this troubled world it seemed as easy to be born a fool as to be born a wise man—(laughter)—and, therefore the sons of their fathers did not necessarily inherit anything of the capacity of their fathers. But they almost invariably had a peculiar, way of looking at things—those men who never worked for a living, who never came in con- tact with the rough and tumble of life and who were spoon-fed from the cradle to the grave. (Hear, hear.) He paid every tribute of respect to a man whether titled or otherwise if by his capacity he merited respect. (A Voice : "Lord Morley is one of them.") He was speaking of the sons and Lord Morley had no son and he was a peer who ran no risk of casting his title on to an incapable son. (Cheers.) No man was more entitled to legislate than another just because he had been born. (cheers.) It required no capacity to be born, but Lord Lansdowne and 500 hereditary peers were rulers of this country and rejected the IBudget merely because they were born. (" Shame,") They had no more right to be rulers of this country than they (the audience) had. (Cheers.) Further, the 'House of Lords during the past 4 years had REJECTED OR MULITATED 16 PROGRESSIVE MEASURES.</p>
        <p>(Shame). "We are ashamed of You if you put up with it." replied Mr. Greenwood." The shame is on you if you stand it any longer." It was the same House of Lords that had not rejected or mutilated a single Conservative measure during the past 30 years. No man, sober and sane, could say that such a second Chamber was a judical and a wise body to have in the country. (Cheers). It was only a second chamber when a pro- gressive Government was in power. During the last four years the Upper Cbamber had been growing more presumptuous and now they had :Actually DARED TO DO WHAT NO OTHER HOUSE OF LORDS HAD DONE-</p>
        <p>to reject a Finance Bill. The excuse was this —that because of tha land taxes and because of the super tax on incomes of £5,000 a year and more they wanted to hear the voice of the people before the Budget became law. He hoped they were getting ready to let them hear their voice. ((heers). He hoped that their voice would be the voice of God—final. The Lords had rejected the Budget in the desire to rave their own pockets and to push the burden on to those less able to pay. It was a desire to throw the burden off the shirkers on to the workers. (Hcar, hear). Taking up his copy of the Budget the speaker remarked that if every man perused the Bible as he perused the Bill we would indeed be a theological race. (Laughter.) At this point a man from the gallery shouted. "Why did you take the coal tax off?" " Because " the speaker continued" the miners wanted It dr. We felt that their employment was jeopardised by the continuance of that tax—(cheers)—and as we stand for employment, and more of it,'we removed the tax." (Cheers.) If any man there agreed with that rainbow politician, Mr. Chamberlain, he might say that Mr. Chamberlain was in favour of removing the tax.</p>
        <p>(Cheers.) Referring to Old Age Pensi ms, Mr. Green- wood said that the Peers although they had pat-sed them had said they would destroy thrift. (Voice: "What about Lord Cross with his £2,000 a year ?'') "I call him Lord Joyful " commented the speaker. The Budget also provided .£650,000 extra for the Post Office officials. They wanted the Goveanment to be model employers. (Voice : "'Who called them blood- suckers?") 'lie is a neighbour of yours not mine. (Laughter.) Evidently you do not love your neighour as yourselves." Tariff Reform meant taxing bread and beef. (Voice : " Nothing of the sort.") Mr. Chamberlain said be was going to put</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TAX ON BREAD AND MEAT</head>
        <p>A TAX ON BREAD AND MEAT</p>
        <p>—(hear, hear,)—and not only those, but on butter, milk and cheese and every a-ticle they wore and used. Tariff Reform meant taxing the poorest people. ("No! No! ") They could not tax food and clothing without taxing the poor. (Cheers). In the Lloyd George Budget there was not a farthing put on the poor in the country. They asked the wage earners to pay a tax on tobacco and whisky. (Voice: "We will do it.") They asked them to pay on those to protect their wives and children in regard to the necessaries of life. (Cheers). They believed that the wage earners would rather pay on their luxuries than on the prime necessaries of life. (Cheers). Whfn the Bulget became law the rich would pay their share. THE I3UDGEr EQUALIZED TIE BURDEN.</p>
        <p>If we put up with the action of the Lords any longer we were not living up to the fighting spirit of our race, He said he welcomed any proper question while he was speaking because then people would understand more of their policy. (Hear, hear.) (A VOICF: : " Why is it that bread is dearer now than it was under a Conservative Government? ") Free Trade meant untaxed bread, therefore, nothing that loree Trade had done could increase the price of bread. But, if we taxed bread, up would go the price still more, just as the tax on tobacco raised the price. (A VOICE : " What is the use of taxing whisky if we do not get m are from it ?") Mr. Greenwood quoted the words of the Chancellor of the Exchequer when he told the House of Commons that the tax on whisky had not yielded as much as he expected,and added "As Chancellor of the Exchequer I am sorry, but as an Englishman I am glad." If a man did not spend his money in whisky he would spend it in food and clothing, &amp;c., which gave more employment in their manufacture than the distilling of whisky. (Cheers.) When the Licensing Bill of last year was passing through the House of Commons the brewers, who were hoping to get heavy compensation under any Bill, tried to make out that the profits averaged from 10s. to I Is. on a barrel of beer. Ths Government lost the Bill, but they remembered the pront—(laughter)--- and they were going to take 3d. on a barrel. (Cheers.) Why should not the brewers pay for Dre ulnoughts to pretec t them, because if the G rmans ever got into this country they would make first for the brewery. (Lsughter.) Mr. Greenwood asked them to vote Liberal in order that they might for ever destroy the veto of the House of Lords. (Cheers) The spcaker explained the land clauses proposals and also the death duties and his statements were received with enthusiasm MR CHUR/311ILL'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>When Mr. Churchill arrived he wig received with loud and prolonged cheering. The audience rose to its feet and to the ace imraniment of the orchestra sang " For he's a jolly good fellow." Mr. Churchill said : Ladies awl gentlemen —I am afraid you will welcome me with some d mbt because I are afraid I have inter- rupted the speech which Mr. Hamar Greenwood was delivering. I know him of old and Ikn ico what great powers of dealing with a public audience he has and his high gift of lucid, papular explanation, but I hope you won't owe me a grudge although the tail end of a speech is often more flourishing than the begitining. lam anxious as soon as possible to have a little nec:•s- sary rest. However, I hope you will be indulg,mt to rre for a few moments. I are very glad to come to Warrington to talk to you at this critical time. I think it is the most critical time which any or its can remember. At most general elections one of the parties stands on the defensive. It relies, like an army, on its entrenchments and waits for the other side to attac'z. That WaS th 3 case at the last generit election. 'lb. Conservative party remained in their entrenchments without much to say for themselves, and we were the party who came forward in attack and a very stmessful attack too. (Cheers). This election is more like the</p>
        <p>MEETING OF TWO ARMIES both of which are marching forward, end both attack- ing at the same time and both have positive non .iies and programmes. Therefore, to be defeated at this juncture would not mean merely that we should stay where we are, but would mean that we should be definitely forced back on to other ground which we have left in the past and on the other hand to win means of positive and decided advance in the future. Now let us see how we shall be moved back by a Conservative victory, It seems to me quite clear that we are con- fronted with a policy of organised and agres.ive reaction. I don't see how anyone can doubt, that the powerful classes with whom we aro confronted have definitely made up their minds to put the clock back and are deliberately attempting to put it back. It is the old story of food taxes which we got rid of sixty years ago, because it was found to be an oppressive policy to the working classes and a poor way of raising money for the State. (Hear, hear). You know perfectly well the objection to taxes on the necessaries of life like broad and meat. Every man has to spend a certain portion of his income on food, and the work- ing man spends a much larger proportion than the rest of the people of the country. That is why it is regarded as one of the grave injustices and that is one of the great reasons why we disagree with the main outlines of these things. What, is the proposal for dealing with the commerce and trade of the country ? It is proposed by the Conservative party to deal with them on the basis of taxing all goods which the foreigner sends here and shutting out these goods. Now what does that mean ? It means that you are not going to allow the people of this country to buy wEat they like, where they like, and whore they can get best served. It is absolutely necessary to have our hands free in respect of all the articles required to reduce the cost of living and to enable manufactures to be conducted with the most facility untrammelled in all respects. (Cheers). The remedy of our opponents is, definitely, to go back to an abandoned system uhich has often been tried before and given up because it has failed, and is still failing, in other countries. (Cheers.) I come to the most serious of all—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>CONSTITUTIONAL POINT.</head>
        <p>THE CONSTITUTIONAL POINT.</p>
        <p>Up till a few weeks ago no one ever thought the House of Lords would throw out the Budget. Fifty years ago it would never have been tolerateri, 100 years ago it might have led to civil war, but now presuming upon the powerful organisation of newspapers which come out by the million at, a time —presuming on their power—the House of Lords, which is not a national institution in any true sense, but which I say without hesitation is a mere party tool in the hands of the Ccnservative party, pre- suming upon those conditions the Howe of Lords has thrown out the Budget and by that very fact they have established—unless you rebuke them—they have established a claim to have control over finance. That I say is a reactionary claim. (Cheers.) That I say is a further point of positive reaction in the pro- gramme put before the country by the Conservative party. You may say "We are not members of Parlia- ment. We do not perhaps, take such a high view as you do of the House of Commons. We dare say it may be annoying you, but we don't see where we are involved in all this. We believe you are making too much fuss on the Constitutional point." But it touches you very nearly. I see before me a large audience of the good people of the town of Warrington—l see them here asking them- selves in their minds as to what they shall do in the near future with their vote they have got to give. What will you do with it? How will you use it?— in the interests of the country as a whole, or in your own interests and the interests of the town, or in promoting those causes which are larger than the onuses of any town, more important than the affair of any individual—the great</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>CAUSES OF HUMAN PROGRESS</head>
        <p>CAUSES OF HUMAN PROGRESS</p>
        <p>and destiny on which th-e—civillsiition of the world depends. (Cheers). What is your vote for? Is is to elect a member of Parliament. That is all the power you have in politics. You have no other Constitutional power, and if that vote becomes weakened or impaired, and if you elect members who go to Parliament less powerful, your political stand is proportionately lower. Every single man who has a vote,by that vote has a means of making his will effective on the governing power of the country. Your political rights as citizens of this great Empire depend on your baying votes which enable you to choose the chamber that chooses the Government? The House of Commons has the power to choose the Government because it has the control of money matters, and it has been able to say, "If we don't get a Government we like we will not allow it to have any money at all." By that means in the past we have successfully con- verted the monarchy of England from an autocratic monarchy like the Russian monarchy, tyrannical and despotic, to a constitutional monarchy which commands the respect of every class (Cheers.) By applying that power we have secured for the people's House this unique and valuable power of having the control of the executive government. But if the House of Lords over whom you have no control—some of whose members are now good enough to come on to public platforms and attempt to justify the action NV bich they have taken—if the House of Lords does anything you don't like you have no constitutional means at present of expressing your disapproval. (Voice: "Get rid of them.") You cannot change them. You don't vote for them. They represent nobody. They are responsible to nobody. They are chosen simply because they are the sons of their fathers What you have to decide on the Constitutibnal issue at this moment is: "Are you going to give more power to the assembly which you can control, or to the assembly which is beyond your control "? (Cheers). It seems to me that every man who votes for the veto of the House of Lords against the rights of the House of Commons is deliberately voting that he himself should have less political power in the future. (Cheers,) Every candidate or member who comes forward on a Conservative plan and asks the electors to return him to the House of Commons, asks his fellow citizens to confer upon him the honour of that assembly—every one of these gentlemen is asking to go to the House of COmmons in order to weaken the assembly which he is to enter. If we are confronted with reaction in one</p>
        <p>quarter, are there not good hopes of progress in the other? I think you have had six months to reflect on the Budget and its provisions. Of course, the Budget is a means of raising money. But what for ? The main, expense is old-age pensions. (Hear, hear). Old age pensions were not promised by us ; they were promised by the CAiservative party. They were not promind at the General Election by the Liberal party; but we thought it right to redeem that pledge on which votes have been asked for by the Conservatives in the past. I must say he is a very sour natured kind of man who goes about picking boles and finding little injustices in that act and dwelling upon them alone. Of course, there have been some omissions, but if the sessions had run its normal course we should have introduced a bill to ronnd off the hard cases here and there. (Cheers.) I believe that the Pension's Act marks a great and important milestone in the annals of the British democracy. It indicates an entirely new view on the part of the State in regard to the problems of poverty, and it is a fine thing to think that we have hem able to get this great system of money (27,000,000 or £8,000,000) circulating among the poor and worn-out workers, making all sorts of family unions possible which wore not possible before,uniting the parents and the children, the old wife and the old husband. I have no hesitation in saying that if we had done no other work we should have felt that we had not wasted our time at Westminster. (Cheers). The fact, that every one in the country at seventy is entitled to five shillings a week makes it easier for them to insure themselves against infirmity and invalidity. It will not in any way injure the great friendly societies or the great trade unions, but it will enable more benefits to be extended to our fellow country- men who by unfortunate circumstances or by their weakness have not been able to make provision (Cheers.) We propose in our Budget to deal for the first time effectively with land. (Cheers.) You in this town—a very growing town—must be very conscictisof the injustice of the process which enables value of land, due to the exertions cif the whole community to be put into the pockets of th )S3 IV to happen to own the fields and meadows upon which a geeat industrial town atises. Land is vital to the people and it is extremely limited in extent. Land is different in its character from almost any other ,corumo lity which can he mentioned. There is no doubt that the present conditions of tenure of land, which is needed by the whole com- munity for the busin s; of their lives and for their manufact iroe, for the dwellings of the population and the industriLs of the country, giv3 th 3 owier the power to</p>
        <p>SKIM TIM CIM.I.M off almost every other local, mon'cinal, 0 priva',e improvement. If the rosult of the land taxation should involve ins it (103 S the creation of a great system of v tuition of all the land ia the co I ttry,so th .t W 3 shall Itw.rw exa-tly what nll I.ml in wipoly is worth ald who possess s it, it will b a v.iluable basis for further advance in dealing with the hind system. (Cheers). On the subject ot -Free Irate we h ,ye the ri,:ht of free enterrrhe, oil which the welfare cf the country, and particularly the welrire of the jades: nab cemromiity. fibs itu'ely depends; the miinq nose of the rights of the House of Comm n u .impairail I y an innovation or intrusion on the p .rt o the hereditary asimbly, and efreanal curtailtm n' of the veto of tlnAt tissemhly, a c mstruc'ive policy of social reform, which has alri-ady been put in hand, and vhich most be s'eadily developed in the fut,ure—these are the ol+ cis of pro- gress for which we are working and fighting. Con- fronted by all (bete alternatives, yi u roust make your choice ; and I have no doubt that if the people of this coin' ry only think these matters out fir themselv,!s, and hear the arguments on both si Its, whit their answ3r will be. But the foroes ag th St us are complex, obscure, and formidable, and they w rk not by fair m3thols of ar,:avnent and discussion b it appea's to prejodice,nn by methods of prei.sera and intimidation. They work on the f, ebleness of some who are not politically very highly edtcated, in I they work through the pressure of powerful agenc•es and public mister) sentation Still, I believe that at the po'nt we hoe reached in the dem mrat:c eevelopmont of our country thl masses of the community tie siong en nigh to withstand the attacks with will :h they are mmaced, and will meet this appeal t ) re talon by the necessiry issue of progress, hurl off the assaults of three wly) seek to set back th ir mach, and go for.vard int-) a fty,ure which will be brighter for the-people of this conntrv. and which shall contribute to the brightners of all the nations of the world." (Loud cheers). Mr. CROSPIELD, who was raceived with lou I cheer- ing, said Oat aftern ion they • had had a demon- stration of political feeling and determination to hack up the cause of progress. They had had Mr. Ilamer Greenwood with them. (Cheers and a voice: And we enjoyed it.") lie was sure they had. 1 hey had also enjoyed themselves at the other meeting, too. At the • other meeting they Ind had a masterly exposition of the kind of fallacies, clap-trap and humbug with which the Conservatives were trying to bamboozle the public. lie had the great privilege ani honour to stand on the same platform with Mr. Churchill a week or two ago at Southport and he (the speaker) ventured to suggest that the case fir Protection was hollo .v, that it would not bear investigation and that it was nothing but andaeity, rapacity, and mendacity. (Cheers.) lie thought the one explained the other. They (the Censervatives) were not spending all that money and conducting such a raging propaganda for _nothing. There was a deal of wealth at the back of it and that explained the audacity and when they got the audacity, the rapacity and mendacity followed. (Cheers.) Ile was sure they would not be taken in. (Cheers.) If he were going to give his Tory friends in Warrington a tip he would say they (the Liberals) were doing pretty well and they could afford to be generous. He would make a suggestion to Alderman Bennett, Councillor Fulton Smith and Mr. W. P. Rylands that while they were organizitr, their spiders webs, to make them a bit narrower and not so wide.—(Cheers)—as their arguments were so thin and vaporous. (Cheers). The meeting concluded with the singing of " Auld lang syne," " God tare the Kiug,"and three cheers for Mr. Lloyd George.</p>
