      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>IWARRINGTON CHAPTERS &gt; ).Ne•-•%/..v‘A/e•oNANni.e•r*r‘eA/•,%:5.k. A WARRINGTON GHOST STORY.</head>
        <p>IWARRINGTON CHAPTERS &gt; ).Ne•-•%/..v‘A/e•oNANni.e•r*r‘eA/•,%:5.k. A WARRINGTON GHOST STORY.</p>
        <p>The Veiled Lady of Bewsey Hall.</p>
        <p>DR. BENNETT'S ADVENTURE</p>
        <p>Warrington is at the moment very much</p>
        <p>,o the for in Methodist literature, thanks</p>
        <p>to the facile pen of Mrs. Gladsling. The "Methodist Recorder" of December IQth under</p>
        <p>the heading of "A town of varied interests" presents a well-illustrated and delightfully written article upon Warrington and its past and present interests. The Christmas number of the " Methodist Times" has also a very interesting article " The Lady of Bewsey Hall; a real ghost story from War- rington." After briefly telling the -story of the Hall, the murder of Sir John Botelar and the bereaved Lady Margaret, Mrs. Gladding proceeds to her ghost story. The chief figure in the narrative is well known amongst us as a trusted and much respected medical practitioner, one of the last men certainly in our acquaintance whom we should have expected to find as the teller of such a tale.</p>
        <p>The ghost was no less a figure than Dame Margaret of many sorrows, so well known to local students as the widow of the murdered Sir John Fitz John de Botelar, and who sleeps with him under their alabaster tomb in the Parish Church. There is so much of local interest in the</p>
