      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>GATE WHERE DR. BENNETT SAW THE GHOST.</head>
        <p>THE GATE WHERE DR. BENNETT SAW THE GHOST.</p>
        <p>story that we hold back our usual contribu- tion in this column to quote in full the I "Real Ghost Story." "It was a dreary December night in the winter of 189—. The deep mullioned win- dows of Bewsey Hall threw never a gleam across the darkness that wrapped it about like a winding sheet. Down the lonely glen the wind sooned and sobbed, fretting the still waters of the lake till they surged like a sea. From marsh and mere the grey mist crawled and spread along the meadows and dripped like rain from tree and hedgerow. It wanted but an hour to midnight when' young James Bennett, a stalwart ruddy lad of twenty, made his way hurriedly over the bridged that part the B3wsey lands from the roadway, to his home in the old hall among the trees. Though the blood of no belted Botelar flowed in his veins, his lineage was without assail, his honour clean as that of any lad of his age and high Methodist upbringing. No reveller he, nor love- sick swain, nor "brave" in search of adventure. Ile was returning home from his band practice, an orchestral class in the town led by T. J. Down, the first organist of our chapel in Bold-street. Wet and hungry, he was no more inclined "to call spirits from the vasty deep" than to try his 'prentice hand, Orpheus-like, upon the sticks and stones that lay along his way. It was not a night for loitering, and no syren's song from the waters as be passed would have lured him away from the hot mincepie supper that awaited him in the big farm kitchen yonder. With his fiddle under his arm he quickened his footsteps, whistling as he went. Swinging into the narrow lane that skirts the wood. he made for the white barred gate and the path aver the moat that was his nearest and customary way in. This gate was always kept closely padlocked and had spikes along the top, but there was an aperture big enough for the lad to squeeze throdgh, as he had done many a time before. Just as he reached the gate, what was his surprise to find a woman standing in his way, tall and graceful, clad in clinging night attire and swathed about the head with a thin grey veil. Rooted to the spot, he neither quailed nor uttered cry, though conscious that he was in the presence of some occult power not known before. Unable to move his gaze from the apparition, he remained transfixed, waiting for the lady to make her wishes known. At length, as if satisfied that the youth was none of those she sought, the figure passed backwards through the gate, melted through it as though she too had been made of the mist,and pausing an instant on the other side, turned and went down the dark avenue to the left, that leads through plantations of laurel and rhododendron to the old chapel by the side of the house. The vision passed, the lad, startled but unafraid, pro- ceeded on his way. Testing the gate, he found it safely padlocked as usual, and, wondering much at the lady's passage through, he crept, under ,the top rung and made his way up to the house, resolved to say nothing to anyone about what he had seen.</p>
        <p>In the cosy ingle sat his father and brother and sister, and the savoury hot pies were on the table for supper. No sooner had he entered the room than all three exclaimed at his appearance. His rosy cheeks were blanched to the colour of snow, and the plentiful crop of bushy hair stood straight and stiff on end. Coming in such a question- able shape, an explanation was inevitable, and he had to make a clean breast of it. "I have seen the ghost," he said: and telling them what had passed, without more ado he put his fiddle in the corner and sat down to his meal.</p>
        <p>This was some years ago. Many a time since has James Bennett returned to Bewsey Hall of a winter's night, nut never again has he seen the ghostly lady, nor have any other members of the family been privileged with an interview. To this day Dr. Bennett cannot account for the experience by any natural means. Keen and practical to the core, with all a physician's deftness and cleanness of analysis, he is yet</p>
        <p>secrecy in the matter. This young man is a well-known citizen of Warrington to-day, but as he made discretion so obviously the better part of valour there is no need to give his name. "Jim was neither better nor naughtier than the rest of the boys," said Miss Emily Bennett, the elder sister, who was present when he came in that night; "it is strange that he should have been the one singled out for such an honour." " Did you ever see the ghost ?" I asked. "No," she said ; " yet I came home that way in the dark many a time, and have sat up far into the night in the dark and haunted rooms." Bewsey Hall, with its moat and bloody footprints,' links busy modern Warrington with the far-off days when but a ford cros- sing the Mersey gave to the the town its name, when bear-ward and falconer and fool were among the lord's retainers, when storks and herons had their home about the marshes, and the mighty forest rang with the shouts of the archers and merry men. Sir John Fitz John le Botelar and Dame Margaret his wife lie in their chantry tomb in the parish church of St. Elphin ; over their sleep through full four hundred years' generations of priests have sung perpetual requiem; yet the blood shed by a kinsman's hand still stains the old oak floors of Beviter Hall, and over weed-grown pleasaunce and the shuddering sedges there sobs the cry that rang down the echoing corridors and out across the welkin that winter's night four centuries agone so exceeding bitterly.</p>
        <p>lit?lra's La</p>
