      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>inclined to believe that there may be some things in heaven and earth not yet dreamed of in his philosophy. "I was not in an unduly nervous condition," he reasons; " had I been I should not have</p>
        <p>climbed through the gate where the ghost had just 'passed, but would have gone down the lane and in by the proper way. I was thinking neither of ghosts nor of the old Bewsey legend, which to my childish ken was as close and familiar as my mother's voice. Had I not been born and bred under the shadow of the haunted room and played about the mound where grew the tree on which they hanged 'the loathly knave ' ? We boys know every corner of the old oratory, and had played many. .a game at hide and seek down the gloomy dungeon</p>
        <p>steps. Fear to us was a -thing unknown, and mystery found no votary in our ranks; we were a happy, healthy, matter-of-fact set who would have laughed at a tale like this had it been told by a stranger. It is not as though I had eaten the mincepie either;" he added; "that might have accounted for a great deal—a pie of the dimensions we knew in those days eaten before going up to bed might have brought about a scene like that I have described; but no one -would suggest that the mere thought of the meal acting upon a hungry boy's imagination would produce such phenomena. Besides, some few weeks after, a friend coming to visit us at the Hall met the same lady in the darkness at the gate under the trees. Terrified beyond measure, he turned back and ran for his life the whole way back to Warrington, and never again would come likat way after dark." At this time, too, he knew nothing of Dr. i3ennett's experience, all the family having bound themselves to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>CALL RIGHTS RBSERVED.J</p>
        <p>it Asangd upon her thilt She hat" got to the heart of the mystery. Actual fear had not entered into her yet; her sole thought then was of the lover who was to die, and how, by this strange chance, she might yet save him. . " They spoke of another man at the trial." sql jo ltigq otn ti! aaq spasmol 90111 Rpi istiam eq sti ‘Spuaiostq, pps aq „‘eat 1U Noorj „ „•fl II os num aetnouV "Slmols pps 011.5 candle. "Why, you were deceived once your- self—when you thought I was John Baxen- dale. Ah "—he pointed at her jeeringly, as he saw heft face flush—" I see you remembers The sweetest lips ever I tasted, and I'll taste 'ern again when it suite me," he added brutally. "Why have you brought me to this place?" she asked. "To let you understand that one brother is as good as another—perhaps better. I am no milksop; I know more of the world than ever John Baxendale did; I can teach you what love really means, my dear. We,- shall suit each other nicely—and the gallows won't cheat you of your John Baxendale after all. You can pretend that I'm the man—and that pretence won't be difficult." "Then you are the man—the real Rufus Cridge—who killed the girl?!' said Evelya, quietly. "I don't mind admitting it," he replied. " She was in my way, and she threatened me. She was most in my way because I'd seen you," he added, leering at her across the table, "and I wanted you badly. More than that, I hoped to keep up that game of per- sonating John Baxendale for a longer time; and she knew me as Rufus Cridge, and would have prevented that. It would take too long to explain to you how it happened that she mistook me for Baxendale but that .was where my luck came in. if she didn't mis- take me then, she wrote that lie to save me— which was, perhaps, rather more than I de- served. And .by that lie John Baxendale dies, and I"—he bowed to her with a grin— "l am very much at your service to take his place." "John Baxendale shan't die," she said, steadily. "Now that I know that that other man exists, and now that I have your word that John Baxendale is innocent, I will con- trive somehow to drag you out into the light of day, and to let 'all men know that what wa. said at the trial is true."</p>
        <p>"You've set yourself something of a task, my dear," he said, seating himself on the table, and leaning across towards her. "How are you going to set about it? In the first place, you are in one of the worst slums in London, and you've come here voluntarily. What should I be doing if you started to crl out to anyone to arrest me? Besides, down in this quarter we're all rogues alike, and there is a certain camaraderie amongst us that im- pels one man to help another, if by any chance that man is likely to fall into the hands of the police. More than all, by the time you have set the law in motion, and have really proved that there is a Rufus Cridge, and have made your appeal, and what not, Johb Baxendale will be dead as mutton. You're too late, my dear. Best take things as they are and accept the inevitable. This is only a part of something that was planned five-and-twenty years ago, before you were born. You come into the thing as-a victim, against your will. But that's the fault of John Baxendale. Don't blame me; blame those who trained me, and made me what I am. I might have been as smug and respectable as John Baxendale, and then all this would never have happened. But in this strange old world the good don't always die in their beds, it's sometimes the rogues who do that, and in this case I shouldn't be sur- prised if, in due course, I died quietly, long after John Baxendale has ended on the gallows." "I beg you to let .me go," she said, as steadily as she could. "Have you not clone harm enough? " "I shouldn't let you go now, under any circumstances. In bringing you here I've burnt my boats," he answered. her. "If I let you go now I give away my secret to -every- body. They'll know where to hunt for Rufus Cridge, and he might by chance take the place of a certain John ,Baxendale. No, sweetheart, you belong to me, and there's a long and a merry life- before us both. It's a rogue's life, but you'll have more excitement in it than ever you could have had if you had belong to John Baxendale. Come, a kiss to seal the bargain." . _</p>
        <p>He vaulted over the table on which he had been seated, and tried to throw an arm about her waist. She got away from him, and made for the door, rattled at the lock, and raised her voice in a scream. And that scream was strangely 'answered by a voice outside, and by a storm of blows upon the door itself. The man stepped back, and sucked in his breath quickly, and looked about for a weapon. "So you've sold me, have you?" he mut- tered, as the yoke outside was raised again, demanding admittance. "How many ar- there of them?" "I don't know," she faltered. "I don't even know who they are. But thank Heaven they'll catch you!" "I don't think ' they will," he replied. "They haven't got me yet, by a long chalk. I'll kill the first man that Comes in at that door!"</p>
        <p>It was the voice of Felix Goode calling out- side, now encouraging the girl to be brave and to fear nothing, now shouting threats at Rufus Cridge. The girl herself was fumbling with the lock, but Cridge seized her and pulled her roughly away. "Come away from that!" he exclaimed. "If they want to come in, they must break it down. I'm off!"</p>
        <p>He dragged the table under the high win- dow and sprang upon it, and made a leap for the window-ledge. He hung there for a mo- ment by one hand, while he fumbled with the catch with the other, then, with an oath, dropped again to the ground, and caught up a heavy stool from the floor. Once more he mounted, active as a cat, to the window, and proceeded to smash the frame of it .with the heavy stool. And at the same moment the door flew open and Felix Goode and Munby rushed in.</p>
        <p>Dick Munby made a dash for the man hang- ing to the window-sill. But Rufus, with a final blelw, demolished the frame', and, wir hugh, flung the stool clown at the man low him. Dick put up an arm to save his head, and then, with a cry of pain, dropped the arm, broken, to his side. Rufus Cridge, half through the window, looked back into the room.</p>
        <p>"A narrow shave, gentlemen, but you'l' have to be smarter to catch me," he called "Good-clay to you!" • The window through which he climbed was practically on a level- with the street; ond be- fore Munby—who, despite his broken arm, dashed out courageously enough in search of the man—could reach the aide street into which the window opened, the fugitive was well away in that maze of courts and alleys in the midst of which the house was situated. There was nothing to be done but for the two men, with the girl, to retrace their steps. and to get out of that quarter with all por,sible speed. They found a cab after some difficulty, and Dick was taken to the nearest doctor's in order that his arm might be set; then, miserably enoitgli, the party went back to Evelyn's house. And on the way they dis- cussed probabilities and peeeibilities. "I see no hope at all,' said Felix Goode. " The Man has actually been in our hands— and is gone. Three of us have come face to face with him, and heard his voice; yet that does not save poor John Baxendale. Who will listen to us unless we can produce the man?' Sadly enough they left Evelyn in the care of her aunt, and went away together to Felix Goode's rooms. And there they talked half the night away, in a futile endeavour to dig- cover sonic plan by which an innocent man might be saved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HIS FATHER'S SON.</head>
        <p>HIS FATHER'S SON.</p>
        <p>BY TOM GALLON</p>
        <p>ut o r of " Tatterley," "Dicky Mer,teith,' "Kiddy," "A Rogue in Love," " Fateta Beg- gar Maid," "The Charity Ghost," &amp;o.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XII.</p>
        <p>Mr. Amos Voles, with his heart in hit mouth, and with a dreadful feeling that everywhere he turned, as he walked on that errand in search of Mr. Felix Goode and one hundred pounds, he saw Rufus Cridge imme- diately in front of him, came in due course to the house in Bloomsbury, where Felix Goode was to be found. For his own part, Amos Voles had two objects in view: to sell his Wares for cash down, and to be careful that no evil consequences attended himself with the selling. Consequently he set about his business in the most diplomatic way, remem- bering always that he had a man of law to deal with, and that, for professional remons, he distrusted men of law generally. Being announced as "a person of the name of Voles," he was ushered into the presence of Felix Goode, 'on the evening of that day when Evelyn had gone with Saul Tuttle to that mean alley near the river. Felix Goode looked at the man, with his highly respectable appearance and his flowing grey beard, and set him down as one who had seen better days, but was still a good citizen; wherein, as we know, he erred as• to the latter part. But Mr. Voles carried a passport to the old man's immediate attention with the mention of the name of Baxendale.</p>
        <p>"What do you know about Baxendale? " asked Felix Goode sharply. "I know a great deal about him—quite in a private way, sir," said Mr. Votes, fingering his hat nervously. "Also I know something about somebody wot I believe there's a re- :ward offered for—a matter of 2, 'undred pounds." Felix Goode looked at the man attentively, and nodded. "You mean, I suppose, the man for whom I've been advertising—Rufus Cridge ? " "That's the man, sir," said Mr. Voles, with energy. "And if you still want 'im, I can find .Lim for yer in a_matter of 'alf-an-hour." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"You mean this?" Felix Goode stared at the man with growing excitement. "Where is he?"</p>
        <p>"I'll tell yer that presently, sir," said Amos Voles, in reply. "First of all, there's the little matter of the reward; I want to be sure of that, you know." "And I want to be sure of the man," re- torted the lawyer. "It's possible for any man to walk in here and demand that money, on the strength of being able to find Rufus Cridge." "But surely, sir, you remember 'aving seen me before?" suggested Mr. Voles. • "At the trial, sir."</p>
        <p>"Yes, I remember now; you swore that you had known Baxendale as Rufus Cridge ; you knew all about the worst half of his life. So it seems you perjured yourself then; and now you come here and suggest that you can find the real Rufus Cridge. " "If it was perjury, sir, it was done under pressure," pleaded Mr. Voles. " "Rufus ain't the man to be trifled with; my life wouldn't be worth much if 'e knew wot I was sayin' at the present moment to you. But once I sEe 'im laid by the 'eels, I'll be willin' enough to tell the truth, sir, and to let everybody know that Rufus Cridge is not John Baxendale at all." "You've got to be quick about it," said the laWyer sternly; "there are not many hours left now for John Baxendale on earth.</p>
        <p>_ "Very well, sir; le: me see the money, and the man's yours." Felix Goode shook his head again. "Only when I'm sure of him," he said. "Am I likely to break my word? I want the man too badly for that. Give Rufus Cridge to me and you shall have a hundred pounds; tell the truth, when, in due course, he faces judge and jury, and you shall have another hundred." Amos Voles made a rapid calculation; he decided in a moment that he was only pledged to Mr. Saul Tuttle to the extent of fifty pounds, and that treble that sum would come to himself for his treachery. He closed with the bargain eagerly. "I'll do it; I'll trust to your word," he said. "But wot are you goin' to do about taking 'im, sir? 'E's not likely to be caught nappin', an' e'll make a bit of a fight of it." I can get the police," said the lawyer, thoughtfully. "At least, I don't quite see how I can do that," he added; "there's no warrant out—nothing on which I can charge him. So far as the -police are concerned, Rufus Cridge is in gaol, sentenced to death." "It's an ugly quarter to take anyone in," suggested Mr. Voles. "Most of 'em down there 'as bin in trouble one time or another, an' they don't altogether 'old with the perlice." "I'll manage it somehow," said the lawyer. Mr. Voles hesitated for a moment or two, and then decided that for once in his life he must be perfectly honest and straightforward. He coughed behind his hand, and added quietly: "Did I mention about the young lady, sir?" Felix Goode turned upon him • sharply. "The young lady? What young lady?" Thereupon Mr. Amos Voles proceeded to unfold that other plot in which Mr. Saul Tuttle was chiefly concerned; and as the vil- lainy was spread out before him the old Snarl's excitement grew, until he could scarcely contain himself. When it was ended he caught Voles by the arm and dragged him out of the room, and out of the house, pausing only in the hall to get his hat and stick. He muttered threats under his breath as he went along; and Amos Voles began to regret that he had said anything about what was, he felt, but a secondary matter. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Felix Goode 'bethought him of -Dir,k Munby, that loyal friend of John Baxenclale, and de- cided to invoke his aid. Hurrying Amos Voles unceremoniously into a cab, he set oil to Dick's chambers in Saxon Inn--only to find that that gentleman was out. Feeling that he might lose everthing if he went alone, the lawyer waited, until presently Dick returned; the moment the errand was explained to him he was eager enough to set off. But by that time it was •growing late, and even Amos Voles suggested nervously that they might Lnd their bird flown. Chiefly it was, however, of the girl they thought as they set off for the place to which Amos Voles was taking them. Meanwhile Evelyn, in the villainous place to which she had been drawn, faced this man whom now, for the first time, she knew as another than John Baxendale. Not that that knowledge came to her at first; when she went into the place, and came face to face with the man so like her lover, it seemed for a moment, niad as the suggestion was, that this must really be John Baxendale, miraeu- hiusly come out of prison. But only for a moment, for, as she stretched out her bands to him, and he caught the hands and drew her towards him, there was that in his face that repelled her, in spite of herself, as it had done once before. All in a moment, as it seemed, she understood that was not John Baxendale; she drew away from him, and tried desperately to free her hands. "Who are you?" she cried to this shadow of the man she loved.</p>
        <p>"Don't be frightened, my clear; it isn't the first time we've met," he said, with a laugh. "Only now that we've come lace to face, Inowing each other, it's a bit of a shook to begin with, isn't it? This is a poor place to ask- a lady to come to; but then, you see, I have no better; in any case, the lady my brother is partial to is very welcome, I assure you." He bowed mockingly as he spoke, and came forward to the table to light a candle, for only the dim light of the dying clay filtered in through a window some distance above the ground. "You are his brother?" she said, wonder-</p>
        <p>iugly. "That's it—his poor Mid neglected brother, my dear," said Rufus, setting a light to the candle. "It seems rather a pity that we shall never see him again, doesn't it? A very un- fortunate thing for a man—and a young man at that—to die such a death. I'm quite sorry for him."</p>
        <p>Slowly she was beginning to understand savnathinv of tiip mosition of affixirs: elowlr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>CHAPTER XIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII.</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, let us follow Rufus Cridge, fly- ing for his life through courte and alleys, and yet chuckling to himself at hie escape. But all the laughter in the world would not ap- pease his rage at the thought of losing the girl, and, above all, of having 60 narrow an escape of capture. Once again that warped and twisted mind of his set about schemee for further vengeance. He would 'Thew then all y,ct that he wile not lightly to he .ulaved with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <p>noove au, he breaficed out Inreatening6 against Amos Voles and Tuttle for having be- trayed him; .it weuld have gone hard with either of them had they met him then.</p>
        <p>And then it was that there came into his mind, when presently he had ceased running and was safe from pursuit, the most daring scheme of all, and one which was, in a rielfli&amp; characteristic of the man. John Baxendaie was to die on the day following that which was to dawn in an hour or two; he had but one clear uay ZIR Wit snough; he bhould come fnce lo f:lec with the man who was his brother, cud yet his bit- 'cereet enemy. In some f::..shien Rufus Cridge ietermined that he %you" ac•+iialli hay speech with the prisoner. How it was to be done he had not yet decided, but that it should be done he determined. There were ways and means of disguise, easily obtainable by such a man as Cridge. The thing should be done, even though he risked his own life in the doing it. "I'll stand face to face with him for the last time; I'll torture him with the hope of freedom; I'll jibe and jeer at him about his death, and about the horror of it; I'll add a new terror to it. More than all else, I'll makb him believe that the girl belongs to me—l'll stab him deep with that. Yes, that's a good idea! But how to carry it but?" He had cut his face badly in scrambling through the broken window, so that over one eye and bound round his head was 'a white. ha7:dkerchief, and on top of that his cap. All that he required to carry out his desperate purpose was a disguise; and he decided at last on a semi-clerical soft black felt hat, pulled down, over a wig of long grey hair. His tweed suit was good, though rough and shabby, and to complete matters he had on a long grey overcoat. He got the things easily enough from places he knew, and, disguised in that fashion, _set off at last to the prison in the country. But that was late on the_follow- ing day, when the time was narrowing 'down for John Baxendale. "</p>
        <p>Baxendale, for his part, had taken- his leave of the world. There was no hope for him, no earthly chance of a reprieve; so much he had learnt from those who had his interests most at heart. After all, a man must die once, and those who knew and love*: him would know that he died innocent; per- haps, in some better time, that innocentae plight be proved. It was the old business of the sins of the fathers; only in this case the sins had lighted on both sons, innocent and guilty alike. The one had suffered through all the years; to the other-came the punish- ment only at the end. If he had any thoughts of the world he was so soon to leave he thrust them from him, and strove not to think of the woman he loved. The one thing that seemed always to be in his mental vision was the gallows. He wondered if it might be given to him to die bravely when his time came, and when all hope was gone. The pitiful thing was to have kept him waiting thus long, counting the days and the hours before that last hour should strike. If only they had killed him at once! He knew nothing, of course, of that other man moving steadily towards the prison, with the brutal purpose in his mind of torturing the prisoner at the last. Rufus Cridge. had to remember the part he was to play—had, above all, to recollect that there were those now who were hunting for him, and who would do all in their power to get ,hold of him if they could. He was stepping straight into the lion's den; but Ile would not draw back. He was urged to what was before him by the thought of the man who, in dying, had, after all, cheated him of anything Rufus Cridge might have hoped to gain from his brother's death. Baxendale and Baxendale Hall would be swept away at the same moment—wiped out of existence for ever. The man blamed himself now (and yet blamed the other man for it, too) that he had not at first accepted that generous offer to divide the property. All this could never have happened. Too late now, and all the more reason for torturing the man who had meant to help him, and to be generous as well, as just. John Baxendale in his cell, with two warders to watch him, became aware of a shutting and slamming of iron doors in the distance, and then, curiously enough, of someone singing. He listened listlessly.; nothing. mattered much to him, on this, the last evening of his life. The singing drew nearer, and one of the warders opened the cell door to see what it was; then there floated to their ears the sound o a hymn, al- most shouted by a man's voice, 60 desperately in earnest was the singer. It came nearer and nearer, and there was a little colloquy at the door; then the warder came back and joitied his fellow. " SOmeone to see him. Got an order all right. Seems to be a parson," the man growled. "I want to see no one," said John Baxen- dale, looking up a moment from where he was seated on the side of his bed.</p>
        <p>"Better see him, sir. They've brought him from the governor's room," said th• other man. "He looks harmless enough." John Baxendale Said nothing more, and the man came in. He was a queer-looking fellow, tall, and with long grey hair, while wrapped about his head was a handkerchief that covered one eye. He stopped his hymn as he stepped into the cell, and bowed to the pri- soner. The two warders had withdrawn to the door, and. were looking at the prisoner and at his visitor, though with no great in- terest.</p>
        <p>"My poor friend, I come at the eleventh hour," said the visitor, standing in the very centre of the cell, under a gas jet immedi- ately above him that was flaring away in the warm atmosphere. "I bring a message to the sick, and sorry, and the dying." John Baxendale was staring at the visitor strangely. This must be a dream—or did he hear again, from the lips of this man, the voice so like his own that had issued from the lips of Rufus Cridge? He looked at the man closely, and caught his .breath, and then was still. For in this hour it behoved him to be careful, and to watch for any chance that might have come to him with this stranger, who was no stranger at all. While Rufus Cridge, in his disguise, went on talking, John Baxendale, watching him as one fascinated, was thinking deeply. Ile "knew now who it was—knew that the man had come at the last, to torture and wound him. when he was helpless and powerless. For John Baxendale knew well, that even if he tore off the man's disg6ise, and shewed him there before them all, they were powerless to alter what had been done and settled already. John ,Bakendale had . been condemned to death, and must die with to-morrow's sunrise. He might point out the likeness between this man and himself; might plead that the story told in court at his trial was now proved to be true. These warders had nothing to do with that, and were concerned only with their prisoner. He could do no good in that fashion; but there might be a way yet of .escape. _</p>
        <p>Ittifue Cridge, greatly daring, had seated himself beside the prisoner on the bed, and was talking in whispers. • "You know who I am, my brother, and you know why I come here," he said. "Not all the angels of Heaven can help you now. This is my hour, when I can come and go as I like, a free, man, leaving you 'to die to-morrow. More than that, I've something else to tell you." _ _</p>
        <p>John Baxendale had, careleEsly enough as it seemed, stolen .a hand along his bed, until he cpuld loosen the,. blanket at the ,head of it. Slowly and steadily, as the man went on talk- ing. Baxendale, with seeming carelessness, rolled that blanket down, until the folds of it were gathered in his hand, and he had a good grip upon it. He. was counting, too, the width of the cell, and exactly how far he_ was from the warders. His heart was beating heavily, for this was to be life or leath to him, and he must not miss his chance by a hair's breadth.</p>
        <p>"Something else I want to tell you," whN- pered Cridgc. "I've got hold of the girl Evelyn—got her by a trick. One brother's as good as another, you know, and I always had a fancy for her, since first • I stiw her. What do you think of that? " " It's a lie, and I won't believe it," said John Baxendale, still measuring lengths and distances with his eye. "It's the truth, as truly as I shall live and you shall die ! " said Cridge, getting up from the bed and leaning towards the other man. " Phew ! It's hot here. I'll take off my coat," he added, stripping off the long grey overcoat as he spoke.</p>
        <p>I,To 6e concluded.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P. THE REFORM OF THE VOTER.</p>
        <p>The agitation against the House of Lords must sooner or later raise in a definite form the question of the reform of our franchise laws. The House of Commons is an elected chamber, elected on a fairly democratic fran- chise: but if we are to abolish the House of Lords, or even if we only get so far just yet as to put an end to its veto, it will make a reform of both the House of Commons itself and of the way it is elected, absolutely necessary. _</p>
        <p>Another franchise Bill is overdue. There were only sixteen years between theigranting of franchise to the boroughs and its exten- sion to the counties; and it is twenty-five since the last-mentioned step was taken. Apart from the fact that when the last Act was passed it by no means met the actual needs of that time, there is DOW the added grievance and injustice that most of the inequalities, which were then left untouched, have grown in seriousness during the last quarter of a century.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>AN INTENTION FRUSTRATED.</head>
        <p>AN INTENTION FRUSTRATED.</p>
        <p>The present Government had promised a Reform Bill if it were permitted to live its allotted span, but the premature end of this Parliament has indefinitely postponed the consideration of the questiond say indefinitely because nobody knows what may happen as a resplt of the coming election. If, however, the next Parliament is a fairly long-lived one, it may undertake franchise reform, and if it does so, it must deal with the matter in a' comprehensive way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WHAT THE BILL MUST BE.</head>
        <p>WHAT THE BILL MUST BE.</p>
        <p>The question of women's suffrage cannot be left out of the next Reform Bill. Any party that extended the franchise to men without recognising the claims of the women would deserve execration. I am not so sanguine as to think that any Government which is likely to be in office within the next few years would propose universal suffrage. I should think that the next Reform Bill will not alter the household qualification, but will deal with such matters as the period of qualification, the transfer of the vote to another constituency, the pay- ment of election expenses, and, possibly, payment of members. The increase in the number of three-cornered contests is forcing the question of the Second Ballot or some other method of preventing the return of a minority candidate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>GLARING ABSURDITY.</head>
        <p>A GLARING ABSURDITY.</p>
        <p>• It takes a general election .to bring home to the people the absurdities of our present franchise and electoral laws. The coming election is going to be fought on a new register, but on that register no householder is going to be allowed to vote who was not in occupation before July 1908. The elec- tion will be in 1910. It i too absurd to believe that any commonsense people would have tolerated for over forty years such a registration system as that. There are tens of thousands of men householders who have been occupying houses and paying rates for periods up to eighteen.months, who will not be able to vote at this election. There are thousands of others who will have no vote, who have been householders for years, but who, unfortunately, through the conditions of their employment, have never been able to live long enough in one place to qualify for ,the vote. Three months, and not more, ought to be sufficient to give a householder the qualifica- tion. It is necessary that there should be some short period of residence in a constitu- ency, or otherwise the door would be open for all kinds of jerry-mandering of the register for party purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>ANOTHER SCANDAL.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER SCANDAL.</p>
        <p>All parties are agreed that the scandal of putting the whole cost of the election on the candidates should be stopped. At this coming election, as at all Parliamentary elections, every penny of the cost will have to be met by the candidates themselves. The candidates have to pay for the printing, of all the official notices, for the ballot papers for the pencils with which the voter marks the paper, for fixing the polling booths, for poll clerks, for the counters,indeed for every- thing. It is a wonder that the electors haven't rebelled against the indignity of such a pauperising of democracy. In the early part of this Parliament a resolution in favour of the payment of the official expenses of an election, out of public funds was carried with the unanimous sup- port of all parties. Nothing has been done to carry it out. Payment of official expenses does not mean all the expenses of a candidate; if official expenses were paid, the candidate would still have to bear all the expenses which he himself incurred, such as the dost of meetings, printing, and the like. If we had payment of official expenses it would be necessary-to have some protection against notoriety hunters getting nominated. This could be done by requiring a deposit from the candidate which would be forfeited if he did not poll a certain percentage of votes. AN OLD ITEM.</p>
        <p>Payment of Members is a very old item in the .Reform Programme. This Parliament has accepted that principle by resolution also. But I do .not think it is a reform which is likely to come just yet. But there may be developments in connection with Labour representation which will force this question into prominence. For the time being the Labour party • has solved this matter partially; but it is a big drain on one section of the workers to have to find the money to get Labour men into Parlia- ment, and to have to support them there. Absent of payment of members also limits the area of choice of candidates. The society which can find the money stands a better chance of getting its candidate adopted than one which has to trust to Constituency largely to find it. A member of Parlia- ment ought n6t to be allowed to pay either his election expenses, or his Parliamentary expenses himself, as it is a sort of subsidy to the constituency. ONE VOTE ONE VALUE.</p>
        <p>The question of Redistribution is also very urgently needing attention.. One man one Vote is a sound franchise principle: but one M.P. one value is equally important. There are constituencies with between 20,000 and 40,000 voters which only return one member with about a thousand votes to his credit. The M.P. who has polled 15,000 votes is just equal in the division lobby to the member with one-tenth the electorate behind him. It might be impossible to get absolute exact- ness, but it ought not to be difficult to devise some system by which the electorates of the several constituencies would be approxi- mately equal. THE TRIANGULAR FIGHTS.</p>
        <p>The there is the question of doing some- thing to prevent the return of the minority candidate, The system most generally in operation for that purpose is the Second Ballot. This is an expensive, an initiating, and an exhaustive way of getting at the object, and it is doubtful if it does always*. succeed in securing the return. of a man who</p>
        <p>fairly represents the opinions of the majority. To those who don't know how the Second Ballot is worked I may explain that if at the first election no one of the candidates obtains more votes than all the rest combined there is a second election in which the two highest go to the poll.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>NEW MOVEMENT.</head>
        <p>A NEW MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>In recent years, both in this country and abroad, attention has been directed to Pro- portional Representation. It removes some of the objections against the Second Ballot, but it is objected to by some on account of the cumbersomeness of the system of voting, and because it depends for its successful</p>
        <p>El</p>
        <p>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</p>
