      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>Daresbury.</p>
        <p>A CASE OF ANTHRAX.—At the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council held on Tuesday, it was reported that, a case of anthrax had occurred at Ford's, Lower House Farm, Newton-by-Daresbury. The carcase had been cremated and steps taken for disinfection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Walton.</head>
        <p>Walton.</p>
        <p>A NEW CLERK.—At the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday Mr. H. Lawrence wrote informing the Council that be had been appointed clerk to the Parish Council of Walton Inferior, in place of Mr. Richardson, who had resigned. Kenyon.</p>
        <p>SUICIDR.—A sid•case of suicide was enacted on the London and North-Western Railway Ccmpany's line nelr Kenyon Junction station on Friday afternoon last. The victim was Mr. Robert Gerrard aged 54, a farmer of Clough Farm, Kenyon. He was knocked down by the 2 p.m. express train from Liverpool to Manchester, his heed and one arm being severed. Deceased who is well-known in the Liverpool, Manchester and Wigan Markets lost his wife about three weeks ago and has since been very depressed. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest on the remains at the Kenyon Junction Hotel on Tuesday afternoon last an open verdict being returned owing to lack of evidence as to the state of the mind of deceased. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Pa dgate.</head>
        <p>Pa dgate.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—The Christmas social of th e Wesley Guild woe held in the schoolroom on Monday last, presided Over by the Rev. T. Gladding, president. A capital programme WAS gone through, including many items of musical interest. A song entitled "The last night' WM excellently rendered by Mrs. J. Bennett,and "When the heart is young " given by Master A. Green- ough was very much appreciated. Mrs-Greenough, sen., sang with ranch taste "Mary "and " Good Company," and a duet entitled "The Shepherds' song by the Misses Gladding was very sweetly given. Miss Dulcie Gladding played Chopin's Mazurka as a pianoforte solo mad she also officiated at the piano as accompanist. At the Close of the concert refreshments ‘were• served by the social committee, after which games &amp;c. were indulgeil in. A very profitable and enjoyable time was spent by all present.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall. NEW LAMPS.-At the fortnightly meeting of the ituncorn Ru:al District Council on Tuesday the Clerk to the Grappenhall Parish Council wrote conveying the following resolution passed at the last meeting of that body;:—" That this Council regrets that the lighting of Victoria-road was undertaken without the Parish Council being consulted on the matter but trusts that in the future they will be so consulted. They consider that two lamps are sufficient and recommend that two of them be removed. The Council also recommend that a lamp be placed in knutsford-road at the commence- ment of Albert-road "and forwarded a petition signed by 14 residents.—fr. Woodward stid that all recom- mendations they received from Grappanhall Council would receive their oonsideratiou.—Ar. Pilling agreed and said: "You must take into consideration whom you have to deal with." (Latighter.)—Hr. Linaker : We do. (Laughter.)—With regard to the proposed provision of a lamp in knutsford-road, Hr. Bolton pro- posed that till Council adopt that course.—Tnis was seconded and carried.—Mr. Baxter intimated that previous to those lamps being placed. Grappenhall had only two lamps in the village. (Laughter). Frodsham.</p>
        <p>SCARLET FEVEB.—At the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council held on Tuesday, it was Reported that seven cases of scarlet fever had broken out since the last meeting. Three cases had not been removed to hospital on account of no beds being avail- able. There was also one case of diptheria. RATEPAYERS AND ROADS.—At the fortnighly meet- ing of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday, the .Clerk drew attention to letters being received, complaining of the state of Church-street. Ho had for- warded the letters to tbe Surveyor, who would report. —Mr. Diggle (surveyor) said he had always taken great interest in keeping Frodsham streets clean. At the particular time of the complaints, the weather was in a state of freezing and thawing alternately, and the sand that was put down was not removed before another frost came on. They had done everything possible to keep the streets in good condition.—Mr. Linaker said he was not satisfied with the surveyor's explanation. The complaints were well founded, Instead of the machine brush being taken along the main roads, it was used on a road at Overton whore it was not needed. The state of the roads was very detrimental to the interests of the shopkeepers, and he thought they ibould be kept clean, as the ratepayers contributed to the rates. _ -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Culchetb.</head>
        <p>Culchetb.</p>
        <p>NEIVCHURCH SCI-tOOL.—A good concert was given in this school. on Saturday evening by the Twiss Green Pierrot Tr.upe. Owing to the scarcity of work in the village and the approaching festive season, the con- cert was not as well attended as was anticipated. How- ever a capital programme was gone through, the original and witty jokes and the numerous songs creating much amusement. Songs were rendered by Messrs. R. Fletcher, W. Kenyon, J. Sutton, E. Overton. W. J. Bate, E. Barton and H. Overton, Misses E. Kengon' Barton, M. A. Howard and E. Willcocks. Mrs. W. J. Bate, very ably officiated at the piano. CONSERVATIVE MEETING.—A meeting was held in Newehurch school on Friday last in support of the candidature for the I.eigh Parliamentary Division. The chair was taken by Mr. T. E. Withinshaw, J.P., who in his opening remarks said that be hoped the next time Mr. Smith appeared before then at Newchurch he would be their representative in' Parliament. Mr. Smith on rising said that he hoped to visit them again before the election as he liked the bard work which visit- ing the constituenoes entailed and if the one thing which would win the division was hard work he was the man to do it. He said ha believed the articles published in the" Daily Mail " by Mr. Robert Blatchford about the menace of Germany to this country. What is the Government doing in the naval question ? It is necessary for us to have a navy that will protect our interests throughout the Empire. We ought to study and see if it is right that a government who are pledged to maintain a two power standard and do not fulfil that pledge are worthy to have the confidenEe of the country. Speaking ,on the Budget he said it Was his opinion that the present Government never• intended the Budget to ppss. it was not a workable measure. The principal thing about it was a means of getting through Patliament several measures which the Government were unable to get through in the ordinary way. Mr. Lloyd-George in his speech does not condemn the Lords because they threw out the Budget, but because they threw out the Education and Licensing Billr Passing to Tariff Reform, be said we allow the foreigner to come into England and beat us at our game. -Are we going to see trade after trade ruined by the importation of cheap articles for the building of houses? He appsaled to all voters to vote for him at the forthcoming election, and let him do his little part in building up the future of our Empire.</p>
        <p>MALE</p>
