      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Photo H. Holloway. THE SNOW-PLOUGH AT WORK ON WILDERSPOOL CAUSEWAY.</head>
        <p>Photo H. Holloway. THE SNOW-PLOUGH AT WORK ON WILDERSPOOL CAUSEWAY.</p>
        <p>Hatton.</p>
        <p>THE WATER SUPPLY.—At the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council held on Tuesday the Clerk read a letter from the Clerk of the County Council reporting that the Public Health Committee wished the Rural District 'Council to use their best endeavours to secure a better supply of water to Goose- lane Farm, Hatton. The matter was deferred.</p>
        <p>fall between the platform and the train. His body was badly mangled. A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Hatton.</head>
        <p>Hatton.</p>
        <p>BILLIARDS.—A billiard watch took place between Hatton Church Institute and. Moore at Hatton,resulting in a win for the Moore team. It speaks much for the interest that the members of the rooms gave for the game, seeing that the Moore men had to walk about two miles in six or seven inches of snow. 'A return match will be played at the" Milner Institute," Moore, on Tuesday. December 28th. The Rev. T. T. Lee Jones put in an appearance and marked several games. The rev. gentleman said he WAS pleased to see the friendly spirit in which the games were contested. A series of games will shortly be arranged between the three Parish Room, Dareshury,-Moore and Hatton.to compete for the Sloan Cup, given by Mr. Sloan, The Beeches, Moore.</p>
        <p>MOORE. HATTON. T. Gandy F. For,ter E. Cartwright 3. Cartwright 7l L. Leach lOO F. Woods lOO S George B4 F.Antrobus lOO T. Rutter</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>571 Penketh.</head>
        <p>571 Penketh.</p>
        <p>BAND Op HOPE ENTERTAINMENT.—In connectio with the local Band of Hope a concert was held in the Wesleyan school on Friday evening. Mr. E. A. Murphy presided over a good audience and was supported by Mrs. ;lames Dodds. A first-class entertainment was opened with a pianoforte duet which was well played by the Misses Gladys and Hilda Baker. Recitations were given by Misses Doris Baker. Amy Brown, Ruth Loxlev, Eva-Ray, and Master Ronald Davies and were warmly -applauded. Master Albert Rates was also successful in his Yorkshire reading. Solos were well rendred by Messrs. S. and Alec Plumpton and the Misses Ireland and Amy Brown, Miss Nora and Master Alec Plumpton. Misa Ireland and Mr. N. Ray con- tributed duets, and the chorus" A good cup of tea" by five girls was well received. The Band of Hope rendered two chorus entitled" The United Kingdom" and "Miss Carnation." Miss Irwin was the accom- panist. The Misses Dodds,Diggory and Irwinare to be congratulated upon their admirable arrangements and for the manner in which the young people were trained to accomplish their parts. CHRISTMAS TREE.—On Tuesday evening, what is known as "Mr. Woodward's Christmas Tree" took place in the Infant School, Warrington-road. About 90 children turned up to take part in the fun. The first hour was occupied with lantern views of a humorous character, which were greatly enjoyed. Mr. Woodward worked the lantern. Games were then indulged in, and sweetg, oranges bon-bons and apples given to the children after which Mr. West, who is Mr. Woodward's right hand man, took the role of Santa Clans, and handed each child a present from the beautifully decorated Christmas tree. During the evening the Misses A. and M. Owen, Gilbert and Houghton, and rs. E. Dumbell helped greatly to tbe success of the gathering, which was brought to a close about 9 o'clock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LI BEE AL CANDIDATE FOR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Louis Charles Barnacchi, has been re-elected as the Liberal candidate for Widnes. Mr. Bernacchi has achieved considerable fame as a scientist, end, on the appointment of the Government, accomnanied Captain Scott in the Discovery ou his South Pole expedition. Be has travelled extensively, and is the author of several scientific works. He is also a Chevalier of the Legion of Honour. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. _ _</p>
        <p>The fortnigh-iv- meeting of the above Board was held at the Dutton Workhouse on Tuesda . Mr. T. Baxter presided and there were also present .I—Messrs., C. E. Linaker, C. E. Linaker, jun., ;I. Spruce, P. Collins, H. Nivnekyrne, J. Hallows, S. Woodward, L. Bolton, G. Pilling, T. Booth, T. Wright, E. Egerton Warburton. with Messrs. W. R. de Prez., W. Pearson, and T. Cooke (relieving officer), F. G. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), and E. F. Parsons (master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>CHRISTMAS RELI EV.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS RELI EV.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker proposed that extra out-door reliefbe granted to the people already on the books, to the extent of adults Is, and children under 16 years of age 6d. There would be about 510 adults and children in receipt of extra relief which would entail additional expenditure to the amount of £2O 14s. 6d. Mr. Linaker said it was not a large sum. The extra allowance would bring a great deal of joy to poor people, many of whom were worthy and very respectable. The proposition was carried unanimously. TO CONSIDER APPLICATIONS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that Mr. Spruce intended to with- draw his motion with regard to increasing the salary of the cook at the Workhouse. _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Davies moved that Mr. Spruce be allowed to withdraw his motion. Mr. Hallows seconded. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce then moved that a committee be formed to deal vtith applications of increases in salaries. This being agreed to, the following committee was appointed :—The Chairman, vice-chairman, and Messrs. Morris, Woodward, Linker, Linaker, jun.. Pilling, Wright, Norman. Mynekyme, Booth and Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>LADIES THANKED.</head>
        <p>THE LADIES THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce on behalf of the House Committee thania I all the ladies for the entertainment and gifts to the inmates.</p>
        <p>BEST WISHES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker at the close of the meeting wished Mr. Baxter and Mr. Wright " A very merry Christmas" 'on behalf of the members. He said the Council and ,the Board of Guardians had got genial warm hearted chairmen and the way they got through the business testified to that.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter and Mr. Wright thanked the members for their good wishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>ST, HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST, HELENS.</p>
        <p>SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS. Mr. Brighouse, county coronet, held the irquest at St. Helens on Tuesday, on IVilliarn Looker Spencer, (58). joiner, of Denton's Green-lane, who committed suicide the previous nay at Messrs. Doulton's Pottery,' Boundary-road. Lambert Spencer, son, said dec3ased had always been strong and healthy, and of a cheerful disposition. For a few da3a he had been somewhat low spirited. For the Tut thirty-five years he had hen employed at Doultons. He went to work on Monday morning, and before leaving called upstairs to bid witness" Good-morning." Albert E. Marshall, manager spoke to finding deceased at the end of his bench at 9.30 with his throat cut. Dr. Wilson was sent for, but Spencer died in a minute or two. There was a blood- stained pocket-knife ncar his head.—A verdict of • • " Suicide "was returned. WIDNES CLERK'S SAD END. •</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Tuesday morning, Mr. Coroner Brig- house held an inquiry touching the death of John Sm h. eged fifty-nine years, residing at Rock-lane, Upton a correspondence clerk at Messrs. Sullivan's works,who met his death on Friday last, at the Cheshire Lines Station, Widnes. He csme to %Sidnes presumably to post a letter, but failed to get out when the/train stopped. Shortly after the signal was given for " off " he was seen to 'alight, but stuck to the handle of the door for some yards. He was seen to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>A Religious Difficulty.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday even- ing, Councillor J. Fulton-Smith presiding. There were also prelent:—Councillor H. Roberts (deputy-chairman), Aldermen Bennett, Bolton and Forshaw ; Councillors Birtles. Jackson Holmes, Jenkins, Locker, and Steel ; Revs. James Davies, Cmion Stevenson and N. Wilson ; Messrs. R.T.Fairclough, L. Greening, A. J. J3lley, H. Kingdon, C. E. Parker, E. Plinston, F. It. Roberts, J. Upson, G. Walker, M and Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, essrs. J. Moore Murray (director) and R. B. Robert (assistant director). FAIRFIELD SCHOOL—THE RELIGIOUS DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>A further letter from the managers of the Fairfield school having been considered the School Manage- ment Sub-committee resolved that they agree, so tar as it lies within their power to do so, that, when the needs of the district require it, the Local Education Authority shall support an application to the Board of Education and grant all facilities for the building, by the managers of the Fairfield School, of either a Secondary or a Higher Elementary Church of England school for girls either at Fairfield or on church property in Beresford-street, or, in the alternative, that the Local Educition Authority shall, in the same event erect and open a secondary or a higher elementary school themselves at the east end of the borough, and grant to all religious denominations a right of access to the school to give, by their accredited representatives, denominational religious instruction in school hours to those children whose parents require it. Mr. JOLLEY moved an amendment that the minutes relating to Fairfield school be referred back: That resolution, he said, taas similar to the one sent back in October, and the objectionable element—that they ought to agree to pledge themselves to grant at some future time the right of representatives of denomina- tions to give dettonlinational instruction in school hours to the children of parents who desire it to be given—remained. Mr. G. WALKER 'seconded the amendment.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JAMES DAVIES said the part that was objected to at the last meeting had been omitted, and they thought this resolution would be unanimously accepted. _ - -</p>
        <p>Mi. PARKER said he did not think it was fair that the managers of Fairfield school should lay down con- ditions which affected religious instruction, when it was a question of economy that resurted in the amalga- mation of the two branches of the school. The present system was an expensive and inefficient one. They ought not to pass this minute, because it would place the committee in a very awk,Aard position in the future. Supposing the resolution were passed, and the question came up in regard to other schools, would it be con- sidered that they had settled the principle of the right of entry, and that they could not go back upon it if it was requested for other schools? The question of right of entry was a vital one about which there was a great difference of opinion, and he did not think it was right to decide it on the present issue. The Fairfield managers had held up the Education Committee. They said they saw 'the committee's desire to bring about economy, and the price of their co-operation was that they should have some privileges in regard to the future which they did not at present possess.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH said that instead of the committee being held up by the managers the Fairfield schools had been held up by the committee. They had their higher elementary schools and the Education Committee were forcing them into the position of abandoning it by bringing it down to a primary school. That being so the managers wanted the rights and privileges they had at present. They did not ask for any preference; they asked for equal rights for every- body, which was very reasonable. Canon STEVENSON considered that the managers had a right to make some sort of agreement with the com- mittee before they surrendered, or fell in with the wishes of the committee on the question of amalgama- tion. They did not ask for any favouriOsm for their own denomination. They asked that any or'all denomi- nations should have power to send their accredited representatives at their own expense to give religious teaching to the children of the parents who desired it At the last meeting one gentleman said they had never raised the reiigious difficulty in Warrington, and that. they bad had a peaceable time. As regarded the Sec, ndary School, they had made a desert and called it peace. There had been no religious teaching whatever. Was that a satisfactory solution? To his own milk' it was not a brave one. They had run away from the question, and he did not think they had satisfied the great body of people in Warrington. He th!iught the opinion of the country on the question of religious education had altered very materially upon during the last three or tour years. He quite admitted that three or four years ago there was a general feeling that the best form of giving religious teaching was the undenorninatiotial form, but now he thought the best educational opinion of the country was veering very much away from that. He had been sitting recently in London upon the Educational Settlement Committee—representative of all shades of opinion—who had been trying to find some solution of the religions difficulty. They bad been patiently work- ing for a year and they were now arriving at a con- clusion. It came out more and more as the work of that committee had been carried on that it was considered to be right and a good thing that every school in the country should not be shaped after the same pattern, but that there should be schools, especially in towns, where people who had different religious beliefs might have those beliefs taught. That was the wise and far-sighted opinion of men capable of judging. Mr. F. R. ROBERTS said it struck him as a lawyer that the resolution was futile. How could they bind their ruceessors, and say they must support some- thing of which they might utterly disapprove? Was it not also likely that the whole question would soon hp settled by Act of Parliament? Councillor JENKINS said as another lawyer- -as a practical lawyer—(laughter)—he saw no diffi- culty whatever. The governing principle of the resolution was that the commitiee agreed" so far as it lies in their power to do so," Mr. F. R. ROBERTS said that was the legal power. Therefore he did not think Councillor Jenkins bad answered the difficulty. Alderman BENNRTT said be certainly hoped this question would be settled by Act of Parliament, but that was no reason why Warrington should not lead the way. All this turmoil and storm nbout the religious difficulty was entirely superiluons, it would be settled if they adopted the principle of" Do to others as ye would they should .do unto you." He had never been able to agree with his Nonconformist friends in this matter. While he was absolutely satisfied with undenominational teaching he had no right to thrust it down the throats of Catholics ;Ind Churchmen. They ought to show they were eninahted by a spirit of all- round fair play. No solution would be %permanent, which did not recognise that. Mr. KINGDON said if the effect of the resolution would be to give preferential treatment to any par- ticular school, they ought not to adopt it. Councillor H. ROBERTS took exception to one or two things said recently in/ Warrington in regard to 'the absence of religious 'instruction' at the Secondary School. He would not have mentioned this bad it not been for, as it appeared to him, the very unhappy and ill-advised reference n ade by Canon Stevenson—words hich ought not to have been used, especially in views</p>
        <p>of the atmosphere which the clerical element were trying to create. To say because there was no definite religious instruction in connection with the Technical Schools khey bad made a desert and called it peace, he felt certain was not Canon Stevenson's honest opinion. It was a little bit of rhetoric, but it unfor- tunately represented to a considerable extent the feelings as they would have been expressed by some of the clerical gentlemen who were present at a recent , conference.in Warrington. They might depend upon it that if the parents—the thrifty, respectable and I elieious parents—felt that their children in the Secondary School were in a serious sense, eing deprived of a necessity in regard to their future bringing up because of the absence of religions instruction they would have heard of it before now. At the same conference .a serious innuendo was made in regard to the absence of religious instruction from one of the finest elementary schools England had ever possessed—he referred to PeopWs College. He was an old pupil, and he challenged anybody that the after lives of the children educated at that school were as highly moral, religious and respectable as the children turned out of any school in Warrington. (Hear, hcar). They believed on the grounds of educational efficiency as well as of economy that the two departments of Fairfield should be amalgamated. If there was a bona fide desire on the part of parents that religious instruc- tion should be iv en, and they were prepared to provide it at their owneost, and it was given without interfering with the regular programme of the day's work,in regard to secular instruction, then he could not see his way to oppose it. After further discussirm a vote was taken as follows: For the amendment: Mrs. Jackson Holmes' Coun- cillor Jackson Holmes, Mr. Jolley, Alderman Bolton Mr. Kingdon, Mr. Greening, Mr. Plinston, Mr. Walker, Mr Parker and Mr. F. R. Roberts—ten. Against: Councillor H. Roberts. Canon Stevenson, Rev. J. Davies, Mr. Faitclough, the Mayor, Councillor Steel, Alderman Bennett, Councillor Jenkins and Father Wilson—nine. The resolution was therefore referred back, and with this exception the minutes were adopted.</p>
