      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>SCHOOL FEES.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL FEES.</p>
        <p>REFLECTION ON SCHOOL TEACHERS RESENTED.</p>
        <p>Considerable discussion took place on the following resolution of the School Attendance Committee : "That, in view of the serious educational loss to many children and of the financial loss to the local authority resulting from the lowered average attendance under the existing system of enforcing payment Of school fees, it is desirable that revised instructions respecting the methods of collecting fees be issued to the head teachers of elementary schools maintained by the local authority ; that the head teachers of such schcols be accordingly informed that henceforth they must strictly carry out the follow- ing regulations :—(1) No child shall be excluded from school for non-payment of fees ; (2) No child shall be caned or otherwise punished for non-payment of fees; (3) No child shall be threatened with punishment or deprived of any educational advantage for non-payment of fees.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPsos protested against the unwarranted reflection which he said the resolution cast on the teachers of Warrington. He had never knotc n of a child being caned or otherwise punished for non-payment of fees. On the other hand he had known many cases of teachers paying fees so that a chijd should not lose the attendance. Teachers had also provided clogs and repaired garments so that a child could attend. The Committee should take with great caution the state- ments of parents as to why the chilaren bad not been to school, for it sometimes transpired that parents ke 3t their children at home to assist them. Mr. GREENING said it was a scandal to the twentieth century that the children should be sent home for not paying their fees. There was justification for the resolution by the fact that 58 were stated to have been sent home in October. The Rev. J. DAVIES said he considered Clauses 2 and 3 were a slander on the teaching profession. Mr. JOLLEY said it was a painful fact that the sub- committee were justified in submitting this resolution. There was a wide belief that if children did not pay their fees they were punished, but he had never known of a case that could be proved up to the hilt. There was no doubt that there were many cases where the levying of fees caused hardships to parents, educa-, tional loss to the children and financial loEs to this authority through diminished attendance. Councillor H. ROBERTS said no jury would convict the teachers on the evidence submitted, and he suggested the withdrawal of the resolution.' Mr.PLINsToN said he could assure the committee that he knew of cases of punishment that had taken place for non-payment of fees—(" Shame") and the com- plaints were increasing weekly as to difficulties in regard to school fees. The resolution was withdrawn, Mr. Plinston stating that he would submit it in another form, at the next meeting.</p>
        <p>NEW COUNCIL SCHOOL, QUESTION OF KNOB-STICKING.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Sub-committee resolved that s sub-committee consisting of Alderman T. Tinnion, Mr. R. T. Fairclough, Mr. George Walker, and the Chairman of the Committee (Alderman %V. Bolton), be appointed to open the competitive plans received for the Evelyn-street Council school; and that the Sur- veyor and the Director be instructed to report upon the,plans to the next meeting of the committee. Councillor ROBERTS asked whether it was a fact that the competitive plans had been received on terms which slembers of tbe Association of British Architects absO- lutely declined to accept, and that the percentage pro- posed to be paid to the architects of the selected plans was below the usual charges levied by the members of that institution. He put those two questions in justice to a profession to whice many of them were indebted. If this bad been a case of what was commonly called knob-sticking the effect was to exclude the best and ablest architects in the profession. Alderman BENNETT said he felt strongly that they were not goine the best way to get the best work. It was an improper thing to accept anything less than the standard rate of wage. Alderman BOLTON said there had been no knobstick- ing. It vas simply the Ordinary way of doing business. They wanted the best plans and it would not be a matter of the,amount of the tender. If the architects did the work at a lower rate than they ought it was not the fault of the committee. The tenders would not be opened until the plans had been adjudicated upon. They had asked all architects to tender and twenty-five had responded. Councillor ROBERTS moved an amendment that the minute be not confirmed. Alderman BENNETT seconded _ _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said they had done the work and it was no use sending the resolution hack. After further discussion the Chairman ruled that the amendment was out of order, and the minutes were then adopted. PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL IN WINWICK-ROAD.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Sub-com- mittee, it was resolved that the plan submitted by the law clerk of the site for a public elementary school in Winwick-road be approved, and be submitted to the Board of Education for their provisional approval; and that the freehold of the site in question be purchased. Mr. UPSON said he understood the Board of Educa- had already declined to approve of the site. The land cost £5OO and there was a ground rent of R,-10 upon it which would cost another £l,OOO to redeem. The site was not large enough for the school, so that they would have to purchase additional land and make new streets. The Council wanted 'to get rid of a hideously bad bargain by fostering it on tbe Education Committee. The DIRECTOR said they could not purchhse the land unless it was approved by the Board of Education.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ARM-CHAIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>LORD NEWTON'S WARNING TO HIS PARTY</p>
        <p>Presiding on Saturday over ES meeting of the Conservative and Unionist Association of the Knots- ford Division, Lord Newton, the president, said that apathy prevailed largely in the Conservative ranks in the country. This was a time when they could not afford to be apathetic. They really hail no use tor the arm-chair politician. He knew a nureber of such, and peers were amongst them—(laughter)—who were very fond of sitting in their arm-chairs denouncing Mr. Lloyd-George and Mr. Winston Churchill and talking of the country going to the dogs every hour in the day. They did nothing else but grumble. The least they could do was either to pay or play. Some people did neither. They would have a rude awakening one of these days, and would find things they had prized taken from them, largely on account of their own stupid folly. If they did not play up properly they would run very great risk.—Mr. Alan G. Sykes was adopted as Conservative candidate for the division. IRISH SUPPORT FOR MR. J. A. SEDDON, M.P Mr. Gwynn M.P., spoke on Saturday night in support of Mr. J. A, Seddon, M.P. for the Newt on division, at 131nckbrook, near St. Helens. Mr. Gwynn asked his audience to vote for Mr. John Seddon as if he were Mr. John Redmond. (Cheers.) He came to ask them to work for one who had been a friend of all the Irish members, and who belonged to a party which had the closest relation with the Irish party. He did not think anyone liind Olown such consistent zeal for the Irish cause as Mr. Seddon. (Cheers.) The House of Lords was the enemy of demccraey in Endand, but above till the House of Lords was the enemy or Ireland. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>WARRINGTON UNITED HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNITED HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>above club held their Christmas handicap from theiy headquarters "The Black Horse inn." Sankey Bridges, last Saturday. Trail was laid by W. Bolton, and .1. Garnett, of United, over the usual course of seven miles through Bttrtonwood and Bold district, the country being very heavy going. The first man started from the mark at 3.15, followed by 20 more at short intervals. C. Vose being scratch man. After a lapse of nearly 45 minutes, two runners appeared in sight, proving to be S. Millet and J. Battersby ; the former winning in the last few yards. F. Shadwell was third. The first eight to finish were as follows :—S. Millet, Battershy, F. Shadwell, W. Thorniley, A. Willcocks, C. Vose, W. Litherland and E. Hands.</p>
      </div>
