      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <p>Eyres' Press. Sir,—On looking at your correspondence column on Saturday last and not finding therin any teply from your contributor A.M. to the charge of plagiarism made against him by Mr. Osborne, I concluded that he bad decided to leave the matter as it stood. I was therefore more than surprised when my attention was called to another part of your paper, where, under the heading" Our Miscellany " A.M. attempts to evade the charge. To deal with the matter in this way is mere subterfuge and his assumption of the editorial " we " for the purpose, is ludicrous, and should not deceive anybody. I had no wish to take up this matter, but when A.M. adds insult to injury by asserting that the similarity of his article on William Eyres to my pamphlet on the same subject, is due to the fact that we both get • our information from the same source, I must correct him. His statement that "the story is so slender as not to admit of any marked variety of treatment" is remarkable, coming' as it does from one with literary pretensions. It is in inverse ratio to the truth, for the more slender the story the greater the scope for variety of treatment. A. M. says "the facts were placed together with great care by the late Fitchett-Maish in his Literary History of Warrington in the 18th century.' It is from this source that the information given in our article and Mr. Page's book is drawn." No exception was, or could be, taken to A.M. obtaining his informa- tion from any source open to him. In writing on any subject one is compelled to make use of already ascer- tained information. What Mr. Osborne pointed out in his letter was that A. M.'s article was so like my pamphlet that in places it appeared to be little more than a paraphrase of it. In spite of A. M.'s assertions it is quite evident that he could not have relied on Fitchett 'Marsh for his information. Take the follow- ing examples. FROM MY PAMPHLET. FROM A.M.'S ARTICLE. In 1760 he commenced Beginning with a sermon his long and honourable or two, he secured the career by printing a ser- printing in 1760, of Dr. mon or two and Priestley's Priestley's "English "Rudiments of English Grammar" the first of a Grammar," the, first of a series of text-books pub- remarkable series of text- Hailed by that fine scientist books by the celebrated and scholar. scientist. _ _</p>
        <p>Now, Fitchett Marsh does not mention either the sermons or the" Urammar." The elder brother is Thomas Eyres appears to supposed to have settled have established himself as down at Prescot where a a printer at Prescot and be few books with the imprint disappears from our view. of Thomas Byres were</p>
        <p>produced. Ficchett Marsh says of Thomas Eyres, when his name ceased to be associated in business with that of his brother : "I presume that he died shortly after- wards and left William the sole manager of the busi- ness."</p>
        <p>A. 31. says that " Dr. Foster's Travels" were printed by Evres in 1770. That information is not given by Fitchett Marsh, but is an error I made in compiling my account—an error which A. M. has faithfully copied. Of Forster's works Fitchett Marsh says "None I believe were printed in Warrington." Dr. Forster's first book of "Travels" was not pub- lished until 1773, but he translated the " Travels of Peter Kahn into North America," which was printed by Eyrea in 1770, and when writing my account I confused the two works. Not only does A. M. copy my mistake, but makes another through careless transcribing which would have been avoided if he had possessed a greater knowledge of the subject. For instance, be sass that Pennant " brought " his M.S.S. of the "-Tour in Scotland" and his "Zoology." Considering that both the .` Tour" and the " Zoology " had already gone through two editions printed elsewhere, Pennant could scarcely be said to have "brought the manuscripts" here for publication. Fitchett Marsh does not mention the series of chap- books to which I.M. devotes a large portion of his article. Here he shows his greatest originality for he describes two in his own possession, besides adding "The Murder of Maria of the Red Barn."— an amusing anachronism which he goes out of his way to defend. So much for A.M.'s_information. _</p>
        <p>In support of the charge of plagiarism I will simply quote a few sentenced from different parts of A.M..'s article and my booklet side by side and leave others to judge of the claim of A.M. to be "not conscious of borrowing phraseology." H. S. PAGE-.</p>
        <p>A. M.</p>
        <p>Processes were slow in Remember the slow and those days. Plates and difficult processes of a blocks were cut by hand, century and a half ago. each printed sheet had to remember that blocks and be struck off Separately in plates were cut by hand a hand press, and precision that each sheet was placed, was not a matter of separately under the press. mechanical arrangement, and its perfect and equal but of the 'skilled hand finish the result of a and trained eye. ' trained arm and eye, With the exception of Except by the far-famed Baskerville and Foulis it is Foulis and Baskerville, it questionable whether he is doubtful whether he was was equalled by any printer even approached—it is of his day. certain he was not equalled —by any printer of his age. The brothers Eyres bad While the Eyres brothers ambitions beyond the pre- did not disdain to offer the action of broadsides and humble fare of the chap chap-books. books they had ambitions of their own.</p>
        <p>There is a cpmintnAss of Taw prominence given conceit in this title that to the family name sug- shows that the Eyres' had gests that the Eyre s had a some faith in their own "good conceit" and no name. small belief in the name. I think I have quoted sufficient to dispose of A.M.'s protestation of innocence to the charge of plagiarism. A.M. says thatthe mention of" Maria Marten of the Red Barn" was " no error" although be includes it in a list of cheap books which, he says directly after- wards "mark a period in history and give us a clue to the intelligence of the 18th century " notwithstand- ing the fact that it could not have been published before 1828. _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ In any case A. M. must be sadly deficient in literary discrimination to classify the account of this vulgar crime with the chap-books of the 18th century. A. M. says he "borrowed the same illustrations as Mr. Page." He does not seem to be aware of the fact that the blocks he used were made specially for my boaklet.—Yours truly, H. STUART PAGE. December 23rd, 1909.</p>
        <p>Help the Poor Children. Sir,—Will you kindly allow me space to make an appeal on behalf of the poor children attending the Ragged School. It has been customary for many years past, through the generosity of kind friends to give the children a treat on Boxing Day, and also to those who have attended well instead of giving them toys, the Committee consider it better to give them something more useful, so that the boys receive a scarf and the girls a neck shawl. It will be very disappointing both to the children and Com- mittee, but not near so much to the Committee as to the children if they are unable to du in this year, si ilar to former. Therefore I earnestly appeal to al those friends who are in the position to assist, to tr to do something towards making the lives of some of the poorest and most destitute children of our town, bright and happy this Christmas tide atfd we shall enjoy Christmas all the more having the satisfaction to know that we have done something to brighten the lives of those who are not so fortunate as ourselves. Gifts of money, food or clothing will be gratefully received at the Mission, Bank Street, or will be called for. Yours faithfully, H. G. BRANDWOOD, Corresponding Secretary.</p>
        <p>TO TEST THE RITYI OF GOLF BALLS. There is a capital way, which we have discovered only lately, of testing the running of a golf ball off the putter. It can only be adopted on a day when the green or lawn is coated over with drops of moisture on the grass blades, either from white frost,dew, or the deposit of a sea fog or Scotch mist. When this is so each ball as it runs along leaves its track plainly marked by a green lino where it has swept the silvery drops off the grass blades. The difference in the way of running of balls of different makes, and of some balls of the same make, is _very marked and curious. We cannot mention the species which keep theiecourse well and those that are easily deflected, because this would savour rather of gratuitous advertisement on the one side and unasked for censure on thenthert; but we can assure all and sundry that it is a test worth trying, because the knowledge of the ball that will run true on the putting green is knowledge that will serve us well. It will not only make us choose the best ball, but also putt tbe better by reason of our confidence in the choice.— " Conntry Life." •</p>
