      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>LATE COUNCILLOR SINGLETON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE COUNCILLOR SINGLETON.</p>
        <p>being elected a member of the first Widnes Town Council in 1892, when the late Alderman F. H. Gossage was Mayor. Mr. Singleton had for someyears conducted an extensive business as a meat purveyor in Bridge-street, Warrington, and took an active interest in the work of the Butchers' Association. Two years ago he was returned to the Town Council as a repre- sentative of Latchford Ward, and had done good work on the Cemetery, General Purposes, and Town Committees. Mr. Singleton was a staunch Conserva- tive in politics and a Roman Catholic in religion, being a regular worshipper at St. Mary's Church. Much sympathy is felt with the widow and three daughters in their bereavement-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>FUNERAL</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Wednesday at noon. The Rev. Father Wilson performed 4e last rites. . The chief mourners were :—Messrs. J. /Wilson (cousin) and J. Harris (brother-in-law). Ainopngst others present at the graveside were : C'ouncillor George Howard, Messrs.. Robert Pierpoint, J. Mainwaring, A: J. Titmas, J. E. Warren, B. Shearer, H. Isherwood, John Lee, H. M. Burgess, E. Caldwell, T. Plant, Dr. Geary, Messrs. H. Mather, J. Shaw, B. Cross, H. Bowers (representing 'Warrington Butchers' Associa- tion). The wreaths were as follows :—" Wife and children," Warrington Butchers\ Association, Mrs. Rogerson, Mr. and Mrs. A. J. Titmas. " Jack, Em, and Tess" Mr. and Mrs. T. Plant and family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>THRIFTY WARRINGTON. A Year's Work of the Warrington Saving's Bank.</head>
        <p>THRIFTY WARRINGTON. A Year's Work of the Warrington Saving's Bank.</p>
        <p>The 92nd annual report of the Warrington Savings Bank was submitted at the annual meeting on Tuesday morning over which Mr. John Geddes, in the absence of Mr. W. Long, presided. There were present Messrs. IV. Tinsley. Henry Roberts, C. Jackson Holmes, Charles White, W. Richardson, J. Longland (trustees), Rev. W. Bracecamp, Mr. J. W. Hayward, Mr. J. S. Hill, Rev. G. Vale Owen, Messrs. C. D. Parkinson, W. Richardson, L. R. Rowson, J. Fulton-Smith (managers), and IV. A. Proud (actuary). The CHAIRMAN moved that tbe statement of accounts be approved. He said that the results of last year's working was most gratifying. To the extended hours of business must be attributed the large increase in depositors. There had been an increase in the trans- action of money, the turnover for the year being ..-C97,233 against £89,455 of last year. Fifty-nine new accounts, amounting to £72 Gs. I.ld. had been opened by means of transfers from school penny banks. A sum of £640 had been received from the Home safes which showed an increase of IGO. The business of the bank had gone on satisfactorily and they looked forward with confidence to the future. Mr. CHARLES WHITE seconded and said he felt that be was serving on one of the most valuable institutions in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) He was very glad to see that the amount due to depositors was so large and increasing. But he was not quite satisfied with it. and he did not think they ought to rest until they got the amount up to a million. (Laughter.) If they could stand in such a favourable position as they were in times which were admittedly bad, they had reason to hope that when prosperity set in once more in this country, there would be a very large increase in the balance at the bank: They had every reason to con- gratulate Warrington that the bank had been so pros- perous. The accounts were adopted.</p>
        <p>The 92nd annual report of the committee showed that in the ordinary department 924 accounts had been opened or renewed,and 605 closed,an increase of 319; in the investment department there was an increase of 84, together 403, and making a grand total of 16,175 open accounts. The surplus of assets over liabilities, inclu- sive of the value of bank premises, amounted to £8,076 10s. 4d., an increase of £7O 109. 7d. for the year. The deposits and payments account showed a net increase of £18,802 18s. 2d. for the year. The balance due to depositors now stood at £433,313 2s. 9d. At the Bank Quay branch there had been an increase in each department, whilst at Earlestown the branch had main- tained its strong position. Bank Quay continued to show a larger number of transactions. As had already been pointed out, this was accounted for by the fact that some of the depositors preferred to have their accounts kept at the head office and only use the branch for making deposits which, of course, were credited to head office and not to the branch. The safes provided the means for accumulating small sums uhich would not otherwise be saved. During the year £6lO Bs. Od. had been received through them, chiefly in copper and small silver coins, as against £5BO 7s. id. last year. In regard to the school banks, the corn- mittee took that opportunity of thanking all those who had associated themselves with the movement and desired to place on record their high appreciation of the services they had rendered in the cause of thrift. The head office was now open daily from ten to three except on Thursday and Saturday, when it closed at one o'clock; on Wednesday, the bank is open until 4 p in. add Saturday evening from 6to 8 p.m. The extended hours for business had proved beneficial as is shown by the large increase for the year—£lB,Bo2 18s. 2d. as against £6,822 13s. last year. The committee regretted the loss the Institution had sustained by the death of Messrs. Thomas Whittle and E. Braide, managers for a number of years. • Councillor FuuroN-SmiTH moved that the report of the Committee of Management, he adopted and that the meeting acknowledge the valuable services rendered by the gentlemen acting on the committee during the pas: year. He thpught he was right in saying that there bad been no standing still. It was very gratifying indeed to think that there was such a marked improve- wont in the deposits, considering the exceptionally hard times through which the town had passed. No doubt the innovation of introducing snore business hours had materially contributed to that as it indicated that the additional hours hld just been the hours to suit the new clientele. It had been a move in the right direction in encouraging and developing thrift in a section of the community which seemed to have been neelected in the past. Mr. J. W. HAYWARD seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr..J. LONGLAND proposed the re-election of Mr. Long as chairman and Mr. Geddes as vice-chairman. He said he had been a manager for several years and those gentlemen could not have attended to the business more closely if it had been their qwn.</p>
        <p>Mr. RowsoN seconded, and the resolution was carried. Mr. GEDDES, replying, said a more valuable chairman in Mr. Long they had never had at the Bank. (Hear, hear.) Councillor JACKSON HOLMES moved that•the follow- ing managers be elected to join the trustees :—Rev. J. Yonge and Messrs. T. B. Carter, J. S. Hill, R. T. Fair- clougb, and L. R. Rowson. They bad rendered excel- lent services during the year, and they would be phased to have them as their colleagues. Mr. TINSLEY seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. iiTICHA RDSON Proposed that lessrs. Spencer Wallhead, E. A. White, J. Glover and E. H. Turton be elected managers. The Rev. W. BRACECAMP, in seconding, said he was delighted to find thet the home safes had, brought in so much money. He was one of the first to use them, and they had been a great help to him. (Hear, hear.) The motion was adopted.</p>
        <p>COAGULINE," KLIN X,' TENASITIN E.' Cements for mending all things. 6d. each.—Adrt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>BOROL ___ ____.</head>
        <p>BOROL ___ ____.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL COUNCIL MEETING LAST NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Lively scene.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington Town Council was held last evening to con- sider the propriety, of promothig a Bill in the ensuing session of Parliament to confer powers for the erection of a new Warrington Bridge and other purposes. The MAYOR (Alderman W. J. FORSHAW) presided, and there were also present : Aldermen J. Smethurst, W. Pierpoint, A. Bennett, T. Burton, James Evans; Coun- cillors J. Fulton Smith, Skelland, Dickenson, Wilson, Wilkinson, Sankey, Craik, Steel, Hawthorn, Howard, Halliwell, Pemberton, C. Jackson Holmes, Jenkins, Bellian, Roberts, Birtles, Broadhurst, Messrs.J. Lyon Whittle(Town Clerk),A.T.Hallaway (Deputy Town Clerk), J.Fairhurst Borough Treasurer) T. Longdin, (Borough Surveyor), J. Deas (Water Engineer), F. V. L. Mathias (Electrical Engineer) Dr. J. Coote Hibbert (medical officer of Health), W. S. Haddock (Gas Engineer), M. Nicholls (Chief Constable). The MAYOR moved the following resolu- tion :</p>
        <p>1. That in the judgment of this Council (being the governing body of the Borough within the meaning of the, Borough Fund Acts 1872 and 1903) it is expedient for the Council to promote in the ensuing Session of Parliament a Bill for the construction of new Bridges over the River Mersey and other purposes specified in the notices summoning the meeting and also 2. That the costs, charges and expenses of or in relation to the promotion of the said Bill to be incurred by this Council be charged upon the Improvement Fund and Borough Fund in equal proportions, or in such other proportions as the Local GOvern- ment Board may direct. 3. That the Common Seal of the Cor- poration be affixed to the petition for the Bill. The DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON said they were going to spend hundreds of thousands of pounds and he should propose that they discuss the Bill clause by clause. Tne MAYOR: It is my intention to pro- pose that this Council meet the Manu- facturers, the Property Owners' Asstociation, and the Ratepayers' Association to discuss the Bill.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : That is very good. Jam glad to hear that.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: We can call a special meet- ing at any time to discuss it with those important and large bodies. Councillor PEAIBERTON : I think the Coun- cil before committing themselves 'should know something about it. We have been promised time after time that we should have particulars and now we know no more about the Bill than when we first passed the resolution to apply for it.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: -There will be ample time at the meeting.</p>
        <p>Couhcillor PEM.BERTON : • But the expendi- ture is going on.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : Any objections raised at that meeting will be fully considered. Councillor PEMBERTON : But every mem - ber of the Council ought to have at his finger ends every scheme included in the Bill. Outside the Warrington Bridge there is not a single thing that you could not get by a Local Government Board inquiry, but if it is included in the Bill ratepayer have to go to London to oppose it. The only reason for promoting a Bill for Warrington Bridge is that the Local Government, 'Board would not allow more than fifteen or Sixteen years for repayment of loan for ferro-concrete. We are promoting the Bill on the off-chance to see if we can get a greater length of time. The MAYOR: That Will all be discussed at the special meeting. Councillor PEMBERTON : Then I . don't think we ought to be called upon to vote for it to-night. I am not .going to s wallow that. Councillor ROBERTS said it was too much to go through the Bill clause by clause and he intended moving that the clauses be referred to the various committees concerned to report upon them to the Council.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said he was strongly in favour of the general principle of the Bill but he would like to urge one or two modifi- cations. He hardly saw why the Council, before discussing the matter in detail and making up their minds about it, should ask anybody else to come in.</p>
        <p>aounc-illor ROBERTS: We are not going to do.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: There will be a special meet- ing of the General Purposes Committee before we meet these bodies.</p>
        <p>AldermanBENNETT said he thought it would be humiliating for the Council to consult the other organisations before they themselves had discussed the Bill. Councillor Pember- ton had said all the powers except for the Bridge could be obtained from the Loca Government Board. Was that so? The TOWN CLERK: No, sir. There are very few clauses in the Bill that we could get through the Local Government Board. Councillor Pemberton has referred to certain sums we have already expended and for which we have not got the sanction of the Board. We are proposing to get a clean sheet by putting them in the Bill and remov- ing them from our books. Councillor PEMBERTON : Will the Town Clerk tell us which one they cannot get froin he Local Government Board? Is it that £22,500 over-spent on the water account?</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK said the bulk of the schemes could not be obtained through the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : We ought to give the ratepayers a chance of opposing them.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: They will have a chance at the ratepayers' meeting. Councillor 'PEMBERTON : How can they discuss the Bill in one night? The TOWN CLERK: They can have two nights if they like. Councillor ...PEMBERTON : It is a vast amount of money, and when you can get schemes by Local Government Board inquiries in this room why should the rate- payers be deprived of that power when we gain nothing by it? The resolution was then put to the meet- ing and carried by 23 votes to 1, Councillor Pemberton being the dissentient. Councillor ROBERTS then moved that the Bill be referred to tin Committees concerned with instructions to report to a further meet- ing of the Council or General Purposes Com- mittee. The DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : This only shows the weakness of our business. The Bill ought to have been brought forward at the request of the various committees, who should have known their requirements before anything was spent on promoting the Bill. It shows .the un businesslike way in which it ' has been rushed. It has been engineered by a few people in the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>r 24, 1909</head>
        <p>r 24, 1909</p>
        <p>•have no right to say that and I am not going to allow you to say •L</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : I don't think you can stop me from saying it. The MAYOR: You have no right to say it has been engineered by a few members of the Council.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : Ido say that. The MAYOR: I tell you you are not telling the truth. You cannot prove what you have said. Councillor PEMBERTON : But I can. Alderman SMETHURST : I have had nothing to do with it, for one. Councillor WILKINSON: I should make him prove what he has said.</p>
        <p>• Councillor PEMBERTON : The way. to prove it is this. There are not half a dozen men in the Council who could stand an examination on the Bill and know what it means. (Laughter). Alderman SMETHURST : I don't think there are.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : Almady, With- out the consent of the Council, there has been something struck out since the last meeting—something with regard to the waterworks. It is £60,000 worth, but none of you authorised it to be struck out. I don't make these statements without know- ing what lam saying. I have come over 200 miles to be here. I have been charged with being an obstructionist. That is the finest testimonial I have had in this Council. I admit it. That is what 1 was sent here for—to obstruct extravagant expenditure with all my might. When I find the rate- payers are prepared that the rates should go up is. 6d. in the £ I will get out. I get nothing for being here. I have nothing to sell.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT thought Councillor Pemberton had cast an unmerited reflection on the members of the Council in his last remarks. He (the speaker) was sure that his colleagues were acting disinterestedly in the matter. The Bill was a statesmanlike measure in the main and he believed the majority of the townspeople would be of that opinion. There was something in Councillor Pemberton's point about it being rushed. It looked like putting the cart before the horse to refer the matters embodied in the Bill to the committees at this stage. They ought to have proceeded with more deliberation at the outset. He believed the Bill was a much-needed one and he hoped the Council would press it and that they, would be backed by the enlightened sense of the community. The TOWN CLERK said he struck out the clause relating to the waterworks because they had no scheme to lay before Parlia- ment.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON : But this Council passed it, when they were asked to do so. I told them there was no scheme but they passed it and you have struck it out on your own initiative.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS: The Council has not passed anything except a purely formal resolution to put them in order for the purposes of Parliamentary procedure. It has not committed itself to a single clause in the Bill, yet.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS resented Coun- cillor Pemberton's implication that members of the Council were not doing right. He (the speaker) trusted he was doing right and he had every confidence that his colleagues were doing so. He did not like that kind of intimidation.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON: Where did Councillor Pemberton get his £60,000 from? The MAYOR: It is his own estimate. Councillor WILKINSON : lam getting sick and tired of it. He can't open his mouth without talking of hundreds of thousands of pounds. Continuing he said that the public would have every chance of going through the provisions of the Bill before the final touches were made.</p>
        <p>The resolution "That the Bill be referred to each committee to report on the clauses affecting them," was carried unanimously. Alderman BURTON proposed "That the manufacturers of the town, the Property Owners' Association and also the Ratepayer's Association be invited to meet the Council with a view to discussing the matter and the various items in the Bill." Alderman SMETHURsT seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES EVANS : I suppose a, requisition will be made to these bodies. What about the wider view of the rate- payers as a body? Several CouNciLLoßs : There will be a meeting in the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The resolution was carried unanimously. A meeting of ratepayers to consider the Bill will be held in the Parr Hall on Friday, January 21st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>DEATH OF MRS. J. B. CALDWELL.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS. J. B. CALDWELL.</p>
        <p>We little thought three weeks ago when we recorded the death of Mr. J. B. Caldwell, that we should be called upon at such an early date to acquaint our readerM of the demise of his widow, Mrs. Jane A. Caldwell, which took place at her residence, 110, Bewsey-road, on Monday morning last. The deceased lady had been ailing for some time, but was only taken seriously ill ,a fortnight ago, when she was stricken down with pneumonia and pleurisy. She appeared to improve and was on a fair way to recovery. As late as 7.30 on the morning of her death she was in excellent spirits,and her passing away half an hour 'Ater from heart failure, was quite unexpected. By her death at the age of 45</p>
        <p>Photo [Birtles THE LATE MRS. J. B. CALDWELL.</p>
        <p>Warrington has lost one of the most devoted enthusiasts in the realm of music. She was for some years a prominent member of the Musical Society and on several occasions took the contralto solos. When the present Pope came into office and desired that as far as possible the choirs should consist of male singers, Mrs. Caldwell kindly volunteered to train the boys of St. Alban's, Bewsepstreet, and she did so up till the time of her death. Much sympathy is felt with the two young sons in their double bereavement. The funeral took place yesterday at the Cemetery in the presence of a large gathering of sympathising friends. A Requiem Mass was held in St. Alban's Church during the morning.</p>
