      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the members of the Warring- ton and District Agricultural Society was held in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, on Monday afternoon. Sir Gilbert Greenall (president) was in the chair, and there were also present, amongst others, Messrs. J. Flepherd, H. F. Greenough. W. Strange, John Whitlow (Preston Brook), Thomas Warburton, J. K. Priestner, W. Norcott, J. T. Baxter, H. E. Gibbon, A.J. C. D.Parkinson, Edward Marsh, H. Beresford-Melville,, W. Bainbridge, H. Gandy, John R. Newton. Joseph Shakeshaft, W. Weir, W. Longton, T. M. West, Thomas Smith, H. Taylor, A. Broome (hon. treasurer) and J. Bainbridge (secretary). _ _</p>
        <p>The CU-AIRMAN read a letter from Mr. R. C. Parr regretting his inability to attend the meeting. FINANCIAL. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. A. BROOME submitted the statement of accounts, from which it appeared that the receipts for the year amounted to £776 7s. 4d., and the payments to £B7l 19s. Id., leaving a deficit on the year's working of £95 its. 9d. On the general account there was a balance in the bank, at the end of 1908. of £29 16s. 9d., Sir Gilbert Greenall had given a special donation of £l5O, and after allowing for the deficit of £95 lls• 9d., there was a balance in hand of £B9 ss. On the motion of Mr. BEREsFoith-MELvlLLE,seconded by Mr. WILLETT, the statement of accounts was approved.</p>
        <p>THE REPORT. The annual report stated that the twentieth annual show of the Warrington and District Agricultural Society was held on Thursday, August 19th, 1909, in Walton Park, near Warrington, again kindly lent for the occasion by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. The weather for tbe first time for nine years was fine and there was a distinct increase in the number of attendances, but a slight decrease in the amount received for entries, mainly owing to the abolition of all the Open Shire Clasdes. The figures of the balance sheet generally compare very favourably with those of last year. Subscriptions are slightly more this year, a lair number of new members have joined and the society is indebted to Messrs. Lyon, Palmer, Coxon, and Boumphrey, for four silver cups given as prizes. The Society is again indebted to their President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., not only for the use of Walton Park, but also for the cost of erecting the rings, grand stand, stalls for the exhibits, and other show yard expenses, in addition to giving special prizes for some of the sections, and a special donation to meet the deficit on the year's workings. Lady Greenall's Flower Show was again a great attraction, and no doubt helped largely towards the increased number. of visitors. The whole of the prizes in this section were given by Lady Greenall. Mr. Thomas Henry Lyon was vice-president, and very kindly gave a special prize in the Cattle Classes.—Tbe Council wish again to thank the Stewards for the efficient manner in which they carried out their arduous duties on the show day, and thereby in no small way contributed to the snccess of the show.—On the motion of Mr. J. T. BAXTER, seconded by Mr. Hepherd. the report was adopted.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF PRESIDENT. Mr. WARBURTON, in proposing the re-election of Sir Gilbert Greenall as President of the Society, said Sir Gilbert had acted in that position for many years, and he (the speaker) thought they would agree with him that they could not get a better man to fIU the office. (Hear, hear). Mr. H. E. Gibbon seconded and the motion was heartily carried. Sir GILBERT GREENALL, in responding, said he would be only too pleased to again act as President and to do all he could to make the Society a success. He thought the Show next year might have to be on a smaller scalethan usual owing to the visit of the Royal Agri- cultural Society to Liverpool, but that the Council would decide at their next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>VICE-PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>VICE-PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. LONGTO-N, seconded by Mr. BnoomE, it was decided to ask Lord Wolmer to act as Vice-President of the Society for 1910.</p>
        <p>THE NEW MEMBERS OF COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The following were elected to-filliCeseiga vacancies caused by the annual retirement of that number from the Council, the retiring members not being eligible for re-election for twelve months:—North Eastern Division, Messrs. A. J. Willett and H. F. Greenough. North Western Division: Messrs. W. Longton and Thomas Smith. South Eastern Division: Messrs. James Gould and Edward Marsh. South Western Division: Messrs. John Whitlow (Appleton) and J. F. Baxter.</p>
        <p>DATE OF NEXT SHOW.</p>
        <p>Mr. GIBBON proposed, Mr. GREENOUGH seconded and it was unanimously agreed that the 1910 show be held on Thursday, August 18th.</p>
        <p>SIR GILBERT AND "FIB ROYAL." The meeting closed with a cordial vote of thanks to Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall for the very kind and generous support they had given the Society during the past, year. Mr. Gandy in moving this motion congratulated Sir Gilbert on being elected president of the Royal Agricultural Society for 1910. They (the Warrington Agricultural Society) felt justly proud that such an honour bad been conferred upon their President, and be (the speaker) felt sure that he was only express- ing the wish of all the Members of the Warrington Agricultural Society in hoping that next year the Royal Show would be the greatest success it had ever been, under the presidency of the man who had done more than anyone else towards the attainment of the present flourishing condition of the Royal Agricultural Society of England. (Applause). Sir GILBERT UREENALL, in responding, thanked Mr. Gandy for the kind words he had said about himself and Lady Greenall. He had always taken a very great interest in the local Show and Would always do so. He felt greatly the honour that had been conferred upon him by the Royal Agricultural society in electing him their President for 1910, especi- ally s(1, as the show would be held in a district where he had spent the whole of his life and had many friends. He would do his very utmost to till the office efficiently, and with the assistance of the people of Lancashire and Cheshire felt sure the show would be a great success. The prize schedule would be the largest. the Royal had ever had, excepting perhaps when the society held their Jubilee Show at Windsor. The show ground at Liverpool was the best he had ever seen and he hoped all Lancashire would help to uphold the honour of the county by making the show the greatest success it had ever been, for, to use an expression greatly in evidence at present at political meetings, "What Lancashire thinks to-dity, England thinks to-morrow." (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE. ---- Meeting at Latchford.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE. ---- Meeting at Latchford.</p>
        <p>Despite the inclement weather a fairlytrlarge gathering assembled in the Parish Room, Latchfoxd on Tuesday evening, under the auspices of the Warrington Women's Suffrage Society— an organization which commends itself on account of its constitutional propaganda. Miss Lucy Broadbent presided and was supported on the platform by Mrs. Thew (Southport), Miss Waring and Mrs. Waring. Lady Beatrice Kemp as unable to be present through indisposition. _ _</p>
        <p>Miss BROADBENT said that on thg question of Women's Suffrage, which was one of social and moral progress, they sank their party differences. They desired the vote not as an end in itself, not because they wanted political power, but because they regarded it as a means to an end—the great aim of righteousness which they were all seeking. If it were for the good of the country it was no argument to say that some women did not want the vote. It was only just that they should have the vote. Women had to bear the burden of taxation and it was recognised that taxation without representation was tyranny and therefore it was only just that they should be able to use the vote. (Applause.) Mrs. Thew moved the following resolution: "That the time is now ripe for granting the franchise to women on the same terms as it is or may be granted to men." She said that movement was one of the greatest causes that ever existed. It was just as great a cause as it was to those men who were pressing and pleading for the franchise before they got the vote. They felt it a duty that they owed to themselves and to the community that they should possess the power of the vote. There were social questions which were so overpowering in their importance as to tax the best brains and men would not solve them without the aid and co-operation of women. (Hear, hear). It was absolutely needful that women should have the vote because present legislation was touching the home so much. When women helped men to be returned to Parliament who were not in favour of the suffrage they were disloyal to their sex, to their cause and to those men in the House of Commons who were supporting the cause. (Hear, hear). However good the intentions of men in Parliament were they could not deal with questions affecting women in ' the proper manner. (ARlanse). The idea that women were fighting for supremacy had been put forward. It was not that at all. They were only wanting to be allowed in public life to go band in hand with men as they did in private life. They wanted to be allowed to work with men and improve things which would benefit the people very materially. In conclusion she said that she could not see why the Government should not put women's suffrage on its programme. (A pplause). i Miss Waring n seconding the resolution said that a vote meant a wish and to deny women the vote was almost to deny that they had any wishes. The work that women did compared very honourably with men's work in the world. (Applause.) It had been said that if women had the vote it would not raise wages. Mr. Lloyd-George recently at the Albert Hall said that if women had the vote •it would be impossiblelor the Government to pay women lower wages than metn fbr doing the same work. (Applause.) The resolution was carried with enthusiasm. Mrs. Waring proposed a vote of thanks to the the Iv. C. Harvey Cook, in , secpnding, ' Suffrage' waS very vital and 'ally from the domestic side • ot Only a question tor ladle. ,• .• , •</p>
        <p>in a good position, but also for those of the working class community. Let him tell them quite candidly that those ladies who were taking up that question were taking it up, not for their own interests and for the advancements of class, but for the whole uplift of the womanhood of this country. (Applause). The resolution was carried unanimously, and a similar compliment was accorded to the President.</p>
      </div>
