      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-12-24_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>"Music bath charms " is an old and true saying and music both vocal and instrumental is very promi- nent this week. Will Van Allen, described as 'a tramp musician delights his hearers with charming music on the banjo, oboe and other instruments. When not playing on his instruments he is telling witty little anecdotes of his tramping travels. Barrington also presents a unique and interesting masical speciality on the pianoforte. His methods of playing are exceedingly eccentric for he plays standing on his bead and in other unusual positions. lan Colquhoun has a good baritone voice and intro- duces a new song which goes with a swing entitled "Mighty Mother England. The Bros. Ford dressed as immaculate youths are exceptionally good snnultane- ous dancers and reap well-earned applause. Ben Richards in miner's garb, has a food voice and sings " When the ebb tide flows." Ellis and Campbell provide a good cross-talking turn and give a fine exhibition of dancing. Gladys Witton is a charming vocalist and sings a song in praise of "Sandy." A novel and enterprising entertainment in the shape of an Amataur Carnival for ladies and gentlemen of the toWn and district is being provided this week. The carnival is open to entertainers of all kinds and 10 in prizes is being competed for. Some very good and exceedingly amusing turns have been witnessed during the week and the semi-final will he held at the first house to-night, die final being held at the second house. A tip in Wilbur Wright's reroplane is shown on the pictures. Part of the films were taken from the deck of the eeroplane and give a thrilling idea of what a flight in one is like. The orchestra leave nothing to be desired in their instrumental selections under Mr. WiddOp's leadership: Special attractions in the form of grand picture entertainments are announced for Christmas Day, Sunday and. Monday. )</p>
        <p>DEATHS.</p>
        <p>HODGE —At 14, Museum-street, Warrington, on the 17th inst., Mary McKinley McWilliam, the beloved wife of David R. Hodge.</p>
